<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>romanization of <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i></li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms masculine agent nouns, sometimes from the zero-grade form of the base.</li><li>Forms feminine nouns, sometimes from the zero-grade form of the base.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ô</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ō</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Are there cognates?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-agaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-haz</i>. Represents a Verner alternant of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs) with thematic vowel -a-.</p></html>:/ɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns from class 3 weak verbs in <i>-𐌰𐌽</i> (-ān).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ai-</i> (interpreted as the weak class 3 thematic vowel) + <i>&#42;-niz</i> (abstract deverbal ending). This interpretation was based on inflected forms like Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;libaiþi</i>, from <i>&#42;libjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-nds)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-andz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; forms agent nouns from other nouns or verbs, especially denoting occupations</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ārijaz</i>, from Latin <i>-ārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Variant of -𐍃𐌽𐌰 (-sna) when it is attached to an a-stem.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-asnō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-sneh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-assuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ly; forms adverbs from adjectives.</li></ol><p>Among the Germanic languages this suffix is only found in Gothic, where it seems to have been highly productive. There have been attempts to link it to other Indo-European languages; Joseph (1982) suggests a link with Ancient Greek <i>-φα</i> (-pha), an adverbial suffix (found in e.g.</p><p><i>μέσφα</i> (méspha)), from a supposed Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-bʰeh₂</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-y, -ic; forms adjectives from nouns and verbs with a sense of 'being, having, or doing'.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-gaz</i> (“-y, -ic”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kos</i>, <i>&#42;-ḱos</i> (“-y, -ic”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns from verbs, adjectives, and other nouns.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-dūþiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs)</li></ol></html>:/ˌdoːɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives from numerals, indicating an age in days</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōgaz</i> (“pertaining to a day, diurnal”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns from words (mostly adjectives) which represent "the state, quality or measure of" the word: -th, -ness, -ity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-į̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īgaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-let, -kin; forms diminutive nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>-īn</i>, Old English <i>-en</i> (whence English <i>-en</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms a-stem adjectives from nouns.</li><li>Forms feminine i&#47;o-stem nouns from verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īnaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; forms agent nouns</li><li>forms adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-jaz</i>, <i>&#42;-ijaz</i>.</p></html>:/xun/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Forms indefinite pronouns, with the suffixed word often being preceded by the particle <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni).</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Forms indefinite adverbs, with the suffixed word often being preceded by the particle <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-hun</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates locative adverbs.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-dʰe ~ &#42;-dʰi</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms masculine nouns from verbs, in some cases from the zero-grade form of the base.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þuz</i>. The related suffix <i>&#42;-ōþuz</i> is marginally attested in Gothic; only <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍉𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (manniskōdus) appears to match completely, although <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌾𐍉𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (gabaurjōþus), <i>𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌾𐍉𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (auhjōdus), <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐌽𐍉𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (gaunōþus) and <i>𐍅𐍂𐌰𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (wratōdus) also could be interpreted as containing that suffix instead of bare <i>-þus</i>.</p><p>This suffix is sometimes said to not have been productive in Gothic anymore, meaning that any word in which it is found would have to be an inherited term from Proto-Germanic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates adverbs that denote a motion from a place.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þrô</i>, derived by Dunkel from Pre-Proto-Germanic <i>*-tro-ad</i> (directional suffix with ablative ending).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms nouns from verbs, usually from the zero-grade form of the base.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-þiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms mostly abstract nouns representing a phenomenon associated with the base.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ją</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-igaz</i>, inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-i-kós</i> (which would make it a formal cognate of Ancient Greek <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), Latin <i>-icus</i>, Sanskrit <i>-इक</i> (-ika), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьcь</i> etc.), but perhaps more likely a parallel formation within East Germanic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; forms comparative adjectives from grammatically positive adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-izô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ity, -ness; suffix forming feminine ō-stem nouns.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-iþō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form personal names from common nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ilô</i>. Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>-ᛁᛚᚨ</i> (-ila).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-inassuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms nouns indicating a female individual.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-inī</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Derives denominatives from nouns referring to persons, meaning to be or perform the duties of that person.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-inōną</i>. Essentially an analogical formation derived from denominative verbs formed using <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn) based on an-stem nouns, which in some inflected forms end in <i>-in-</i>. Compare <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (fraujinōn, “to be a ruler”), from the an-stem <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰</i> (frauja) (gen. <i>fraujins</i>), with <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (skalkinōn, “to be a servant”), from the a-stem <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐍃</i> (skalks) (gen. <i>skalkis</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms adjectives from nouns with sense of ‘pertaining to, characteristic of’</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-iskaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-iskos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-est; forms superlative adjectives from grammatically positive adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-istaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>born of the thing specified by the root to which this suffix is added</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kundaz</i> (“born”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥h₁tós</i> (“born”), from <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to give birth”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-like, -ly. Forms adjectives from nouns and adjectives with the sense of 'similar to, characteristic of'.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-līkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms nouns meaning 'belonging to, coming from, descending from', often with a diminutive meaning.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-lingaz</i>. Cognates include Dutch <i>-ling</i>, English <i>-ling</i>, German <i>-ling</i>, Icelandic <i>-lingur</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms fientive verbs, with a sense of 'to become (the base)'.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-naną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ing. Forms present participles from verbs.</li><li>-er. Forms agent nouns from verbs. Identical in form and meaning, but not in declension, to the present participle.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ndz</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>-end</i>, English <i>-ing</i>, German <i>-end</i>, Latin <i>-ēns</i>, Ancient Greek <i>-ων</i> (-ōn), Sanskrit <i>-अन्त्</i> (-ant).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; forms masculine nouns indicating occupation, especially from verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-jô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Derives denominatives from nouns and factitives from adjectives.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-janą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er; forms feminine nouns indicating occupation, especially from verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-jǭ</i>, <i>&#42;-ijǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Added to the end of a word in a phrase (usually the first word) to make it a question.</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-u</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-we</i> (“or”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms entity or state nouns.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-umnijō</i>, <i>&#42;-umniją</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ugaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-hw</i>, <i>&#42;-uh</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-some; forms adjectives from verbs meaning "doing" or "pertaining to doing" the verb.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ulaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-er, -est; forms comparative or superlative adjectives.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-umô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms feminine ō-stem nouns denoting a tool or instrument.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-snō</i> (<i>&#42;-asnō</i>, <i>&#42;-isnō</i>, <i>&#42;-usnō</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-sneh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌽𐍃</i> (-ns) used after a coronal consonant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-sniz</i>, alternative form of <i>&#42;-niz</i> after a coronal consonant.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>doing, making</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taujan, “to do, make”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werdaz</i>, variant of <i>&#42;wardaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>years old</li><li>years lasting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-wintruz</i>.</p></html>:/ˌfalθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-fold (used to make adjectives)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-falþaz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>-feald</i>, Old Norse <i>-faldr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ly; forms adverbs from adjectives.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōzô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Derives denominatives from nouns and factitives from adjectives.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-nds)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōndz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms feminine i-stem nouns from class 2 weak verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōstaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) something which causes others to run, runner, router. Also interpreted as charger.</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rannjan, “to cause to run; to rout”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja). This is one of the only attested Gothic words from any runic inscription (there are only a handful of Gothic runic inscriptions in existence) of which the exact translation is largely undisputed.</p></html>:/ˈa.βa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man; husband</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) father</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>ἀββα</i> (abba), from Aramaic <i>אבא</i> (aba).</p></html>:/a.biː.leː.ni/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Abilene (a region of Syria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀβιληνή</i> (Abilēnḗ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Abijah (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀβιά</i> (Abiá).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Abiathar (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀβιαθάρ</i> (Abiathár).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) very, severely, extremely</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌱𐍂𐍃</i> (abrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abraham, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀβραάμ</i> (Abraám), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אַבְרָהָם</i> (ʼAḇrāhām). The <i>-h-</i> probably is due to influence of Latin <i>Abraham</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) severe, extreme</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abraz</i>. Compare Old Armenian <i>արբուն</i> (arbun).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (aggilus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌿</i> (aggilau)</li></ol></html>:/ˈaŋɡilus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) angel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos).</p></html>:/aŋɡwiθa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distress, anguish, affliction, tribulation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angwiþō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (aggwus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa). Compare Dutch <i>engte</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anguz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agaz</i>. The <i>-i-</i> in the second syllable is the result of morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the word was originally a consonant stem (the genitive, for example, being <i>*agiziz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) terrible, fearful, frightening, scary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic, equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃</i> (agis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks). Compare Old Saxon <i>egislīk</i>, Old High German <i>egislīh</i>, Old English <i>egeslīc</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shamelessly greedy</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;aglaits, “filthy, shameful”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;gastalds, “acquisitive, greedy”), the latter from or related to <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gastaldan, “to acquire”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fornication, lustfulness, lewdness, lasciviousness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agalaitį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;aglaits, “filthy, shameful”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lewdness, lasciviousness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agalaitiją</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;aglaits, “filthy, shameful”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shameful or obscene speech</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌹</i> (aglaiti) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/aɣliθa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) tribulation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (aglus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to hurt, injure, damage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aglijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (aglus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) difficult</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agluz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) shameful, humiliating, disgraceful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aglaz</i> (“disgraceful, shameful”). Compare Old English <i>egle</i> (“hideous; loathsome; hateful; horrid; troublesome; grievous; painful”).</p></html>:/ˈaɣloː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble, tribulation, suffering, anguish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Augustus, the Roman emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Augustus</i>, perhaps via the form <i>agustus</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adam, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀδάμ</i> (Adám).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Addi, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀδδὶ</i> (Addì).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Adonikam (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Αδωνικάμ</i> (Adōnikám), from Hebrew <i>אֲדַֹניקָם</i> (adonikám).</p></html>:/ˈa.kʷi.zi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) axe (tool for felling trees)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akwisī</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>æx</i> and Old Norse <i>øx</i>, and probably of similar origin as Koine Greek <i>ἀξίνη</i> (axínē), which it translates. For more, see the Proto-Germanic entry.</p></html>:/ˈazɡoː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ash</li><li>cinder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;askǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) easily, readily</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌶𐌴𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;azēts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) ease, comfort</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌶𐌴𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;azēts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easier, more pleasant; comparative degree of <i>&#42;𐌰𐌶𐌴𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;azēts)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌶𐌴𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;azēts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌰𐌶𐌴𐍄𐌹𐌶𐍉</i> (azētizō)</li></ol></html>:/ˈazymus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unleavened bread</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἄζυμος</i> (ázumos, “unleavened (bread)”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind, reason</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahô</i>, of uncertain origin; some have connected it to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“to see”), which however has significant phonological and semantic difficulties.</p></html>:/ˈa.haks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(tame) pigeon, dove (bird)</li></ol><p>Has often been thought to contain the same suffix as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habukaz</i> (“hawk”); Lehmann (1986) thinks it is most likely to derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”) and hence related to Old English <i>agu</i> (“magpie”), Old High German <i>aga</i> (“magpie”), an etymology first proposed by Solmsen.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) chaff</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahanō</i>. Cognate with English <i>awn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Aaron, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀαρών</i> (Aarṓn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirit, ghost</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>𐌰𐌷𐌰</i> (aha), which is of uncertain origin. The suffix represents Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-mô</i>, which derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) inspiration</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌷𐌼𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ahmatjan, “inspire”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), the former verb being a derivate of <i>𐌰𐌷𐌼𐌰</i> (ahma).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiritual</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌷𐌼𐌰</i> (ahma, “spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to think, assume</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahjaną</i>; compare English <i>ettle</i>, Dutch <i>achten</i>.</p></html>:/ˈahs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear (fruiting body of a grain plant)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahsą</i>, itself derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahaz</i>. Identical in formation to Old Norse <i>ax</i>, and cognate to Old English <i>ēar</i> and, more distantly, Latin <i>acus</i> (“chaff”).</p></html>:/ˈax.tɔː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōu</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓw</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>eahta</i> and Crimean Gothic <i>athe</i>, and Koine Greek <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈah.tɔː.doːɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) having lasted eight days, eight days old</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌰𐌿</i> (ahtau, “eight”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌳𐍉𐌲𐍃</i> (-dōgs, “days of age”).</p></html>:/ˈah.tɔː.teː.hund/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eighty (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtōutēhundą</i> of obscure formation. Akin in some way to Old English <i>hundeahtatiġ</i>, Old Saxon <i>antahtotig</i> and Old High German <i>ahtozo</i>.</p></html>:/ˈah.tɔː.teː.hund jah ˈfid.woːr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty-four (cardinal number)</li></ol></html>:/ˈah.tu.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the eighth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahtudô</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>eahtoþa</i> and Old Norse <i>átti</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽 𐌰𐌸𐌰</i> (maran aþa)</li></ol></html>:/aθːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, but then, but nonetheless</li></ol><p>From an uncertain conjunction + <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan, “then”). The former could be from either Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ak</i> (“but”) or possibly from a conjunction representing <i>&#42;aþi</i>, <i>&#42;adi</i> (“but”); see also Latin <i>at</i> (“but”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) years (<i>dative plural</i>)</li></ol><p>The exact lemma form from which this word derives cannot be reconstructed with any certainty; it may be either <i>&#42;𐌰𐌸𐌽</i> (&#42;aþn) or <i>&#42;𐌰𐌸𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;aþns). It is inherited from the equally uncertain Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aþna-</i>, which is related to Latin <i>annus</i>, itself proposed to derive from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;atnos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ét-no-</i>, probably from <i>&#42;h₂et-</i> (“to go”). Compare the related term <i>𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌸𐌽𐌹</i> (ataþni). For the root, compare Sanskrit <i>अतति</i> (atati, “he wanders, goes”). In Italic and Germanic, the root shifted to refer to "year" only (as a "going" of time).</p><p>In Latin <i>annus</i>, the nasal geminate can be the product of assimilation of a t by a following n, cf. <i>penna</i>, from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;petnā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂r̥ ~ pth₂én-</i> (“feather, wing”), from <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to fly”). Such assimilation allows for the connection with this Gothic term. The accent must have been on the first syllable, because end stress would have triggered Kluge's law, giving Proto-Germanic **<i>atta-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɛːβr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) gift, offering</li></ol><p>Unknown; the word appears to have no Germanic, and indeed no clear Indo-European cognates.</p><p>Attempts have been made to account for the transmitted form <i>aibr</i>, for which see Lehmann (1986) and Snædal (2015), who both dismiss these proposals. Snædal instead proposes a scribal error as the most likely explanation: the initial <i>𐌰</i> would have been <i>𐌻</i> (which is very similar in appearance), which would instead yield <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐌱𐍂</i> (&#42;libr), derived from <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (galēwjan, “offer, give, hand over”). Alternatively, the <i>𐌰</i> may be a sloppy scribal error for <i>𐍄</i> (which is also similar in appearance), rendering <i>&#42;𐍄𐌹𐌱𐍂</i> (&#42;tibr), thus tying it to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tibrą</i> (“offering, sacrifice”). Lehmann appears to favor the emendation to <i>*tibr</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɛːɣan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to possess, own</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eh₂óyḱe</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂eyḱ-</i> (“ability, possession”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiginą</i>; related to <i>𐌰𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (aigan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) brass, bronze or copper</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) coppersmith</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌶</i> (aiz, “copper, brass, bronze”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌼𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (&#42;smiþa, “smith”), the latter element being an agent noun in <i>-𐌰</i> (-a) that is otherwise unattested outside this compound but appears to be based on or related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smiþaz</i> and the verb <i>&#42;smiþōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (aigan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray, request, supplicate</li></ol><p>Unknown. One possibility is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eyg-</i> (“to lament (?)”), with cognates Old Irish <i>éigid</i> (“cries in distress, groans”) and Ancient Greek <i>οἶκτος</i> (oîktos, “pity”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer, request, supplication</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍂𐍉𐌽</i> (aihtrōn, “to pray”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possessions, property</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aihtiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (aigan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/ˈɛː.θiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiþį̄</i> (“mother”) (or <i>&#42;aiþǭ</i>), a nursery term (Kroonen 2013). According to Mezger (1959), possibly originally meaning <i>wife</i> in a legal sense (a woman whose children are entitled to inherit as opposed to a concubine), from <i>𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (aiþs, “oath”); however, Kroonen regards this as implausible. Cognate with Old Norse <i>eiða</i>, Old High German <i>&#42;eidi</i> (attested in <i>fuotareidi</i> (“nanny”)) and the thence inherited Middle High German <i>eide</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or</li><li>otherwise, if not</li><li>Introduces a dependent clause after an irrealis main clause.</li></ol><p>For earlier <i>&#42;𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌸𐌰𐌿</i> (&#42;aihþau), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ehþau</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congregation, church</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐκκλησία</i> (ekklēsía).</p></html>:/ɛ.li.sa.bɛθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elizabeth, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἐλισάβετ</i> (Elisábet), from Hebrew <i>אֱלִישֶׁבַע</i> (Elisheba). The final <i>-þ</i> (instead of <i>-t</i>) is probably due to influence of Latin <i>Elisabeth</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>my God! (said by Jesus on the cross)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>ἐλωΐ</i> (elōḯ), itself a transliteration of Aramaic <i>אלהי</i>, from <i>אלהא</i> with first person singular pronoun as possessor.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity,&#32;said of Jesus) one who is only-begotten</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains, “[the number] one”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (baur, “one who is born; a child”), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>μονογενής</i> (monogenḗs). Compare German <i>eingeboren</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>only, sole</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) only-begotten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (-ahs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) alone, without companion (glosses <i>μεμονωμένος</i> (memonōménos, “having been left alone”, perfect mediopassive participle of <i>μονόω</i> (monóō, “to make alone”)))</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains, “one”); compare Old Norse <i>ekkja</i> (“widow”) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainakjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alone</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains, “one”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains, “one”) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (munds, “thought”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ness, -ity”).</p></html>:/ˈɛːn.lif/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleven (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainalif</i>. Cognate to Old High German <i>einlif</i> and Old Norse <i>ellifu</i>. Contrast Crimean Gothic <i>thiinita</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) some</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁óynos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anyone, anything</li><li>(with <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni)) no one, nobody, nothing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (-hun).</p></html>:/ˈɛːnˌfal.θa.ba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) simply (in a simple manner)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (ainfalþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simplicity, singleness</li><li>liberality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (ainfalþs, “simple”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei, noun-forming suffix). The sense ‘liberality’ is a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>ἁπλότης</i> (haplótēs, “simplicity; sincerity; liberality”).</p></html>:/ˈɛːnˌfalθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) simple (uncomplicated)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainafalþaz</i> (“single, simple”); equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains) +&lrm; <i>-𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (-falþs). Cognate with Old English <i>ānfeald</i> (“single, simple”) (and thence English <i>onefold</i>) and Old Saxon <i>ēnfald</i> (“simple”); close in formation to Ancient Greek <i>ἁπλόος</i> (haplóos), which it translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) each (of two)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌿𐌷</i> (ƕaþaruh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>every one</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐌶𐌿𐌷</i> (ƕarjizuh). Glosses <i>εἷς ἕκαστος</i> (heîs hékastos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌷𐍉</i> (ainahō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌿𐌼𐌼𐌴𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (ainummēhun)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) bishopric</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>ἐπισκοπή</i> (episkopḗ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) bishop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>ἐπίσκοπος</i> (epískopos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a letter, correspondence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιστολή</i> (epistolḗ, “letter”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌹𐍀𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌴</i> (aipistaulē)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>epistula</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>early, soon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airi</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Erastus (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἔραστος</i> (Érastos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>error (of doctrine)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌶𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (airzeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erroneous, deceived</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;irzijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>error (of doctrine)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌶𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (airzeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delude, to mislead</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;irzijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþō</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>eorþe</i>, Old High German <i>erda</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) born of the earth, earthly (as opposed to heavenly)</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþōkundaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰</i> (airþa, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds, “born of, coming from”). Often considered (e.g. by Lehmann) to be a calque of Latin <i>terrigenus</i>, <i>terrigena</i> not inherited from Proto-Germanic, by analogy with <i>𐌷𐌹𐌼𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (himinakunds); the cognate Old English <i>eorþcund</i> similarly may be explained as a parallel post-Proto-Germanic formation instead of an inherited cognate, but this is difficult to ascertain.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthen</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) earthly (as opposed to heavenly)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;irþīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰</i> (airþa, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earlier, sooner</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ancestor</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌹𐍂</i> (air) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be an ambassador or representative</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airinōną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (airus, “messenger”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (-inōn). Glosses <i>πρεσβέυω</i> (presbéuō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earlier, sooner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂</i> (air) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sincerity, purity (of faith)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (airkns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa), the former element deriving from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erknaz</i> and found also in <i>𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (unairkns, “unholy”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) holy, sincere or pure (of faith)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erknaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, envoy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;airuz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>ār</i>, Old Saxon <i>ēru</i>, Old Norse <i>árr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to have regard for, revere, reverence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aistāną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eysd-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ever, always</li></ol></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lifetime, eternity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂óyu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌴𐌻𐌾𐍉</i> (aiwaggēljō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) evangelist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>εὐαγγελιστής</i> (euangelistḗs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) to preach the gospel, evangelize</li></ol><p>From Koine Greek <i>εὐαγγελίζω</i> (euangelízō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) gospel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal, everlasting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwīnaz</i>, whence also Old High German <i>ēwīn</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐍃</i> (aiws, “eternity, lifetime”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwiskaz</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/ˈɛːwiskoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to behave in a shameful manner, to do something unseemly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐌹</i> (aiwiski, “shame”). Glosses Ancient Greek <i>ἀσχημονέω</i> (askhēmonéō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) benefaction, donation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>εὐλογία</i> (eulogía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lifetime, eternity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂óyu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) thanksgiving, the Eucharist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>εὐχαριστία</i> (eukharistía).</p></html>:/ˈɛ.ʍaˌtun.di/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bramble (thorny shrub in the genus <i>Rubus</i>)</li></ol><p>A compound; the first element is <i>&#42;𐌰𐌹𐍈𐍃</i> (&#42;aiƕs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ehwaz</i> (“horse”). According to Lehmann (1986), the second element is either derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i> (“to bite”) (then with the sense of "point" and related to <i>𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (tunþus, “tooth”)), or from a word related to <i>𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (tundnan, “to be burning”) (then with the sense of "burning" and related to English <i>tinder</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>after a negative sentence</i>) but, however</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ak</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐌺𐌴𐌹</i> (akei)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>after a positive sentence</i>) but</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌺</i> (ak) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>acētum</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍄</i> (akeit)</li></ol></html>:/ˈakran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akraną</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ógeh₂</i> (“berry”). Compare Old Norse <i>akarn</i> (“acorn”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) profitless, fruitless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌺𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (akran, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus, “devoid of”), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἄκαρπος</i> (ákarpos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cultivated) field (wide, open space used to grow crops or to hold farm animals)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;akraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵros</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) A type of alabastron; that is, a small container, perhaps a flask, phial or box, especially one containing ointments</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀλάβαστρον</i> (alábastron); this attested spelling with apparently superfluous <i>-l-</i> may well be a scribal error for <i>*alabastraun</i>, which would be closer to the Greek original. Compare also Latin <i>alabastrum</i>, <i>alabaster</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) burnt offering, holocaust</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌽𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;brunsts), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brunstiz</i>. The term is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὁλοκαύτωμα</i> (holokaútōma).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) in want, needy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls, “all, completely”) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌱𐍃</i> (þarbs, “needy”). The phrase <i>𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌰 𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (alaþarba wairþan) glosses Ancient Greek <i>ὑστερέομαι</i> (husteréomai, “to be in want”).</p></html>:/alɛksandrus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ᾰ̓λέξᾰνδρος</i> (Ăléxăndros).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all together, in unison</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. suffix</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) all of humanity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to grow, be nourished</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation, age</li><li>lifetime</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) old age (<i>dative singular</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌰𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (alds). Uncertain lemma form; possibly <i>&#42;𐌰𐌻𐌳𐍉𐌼𐍉</i> (&#42;aldōmō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) ell, cubit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alīnō</i>. Cognate with English <i>ell</i>, Old High German <i>elina</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₃l-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>oleum</i>.</p></html>:/ˈa.leː.waˌbaɡms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive tree</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍅</i> (alēw) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌲𐌼𐍃</i> (bagms).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of olives, pertaining to olives</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍅</i> (alēw).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alhs</i> (“protection, building, house, temple, settlement”), cognate with Old English <i>ealh</i> and Proto-Norse <i>ᚨᛚᚺ</i> (alh). Further cognates in Baltic.</p></html>:/ˈal.θiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldaz</i> with a suffix <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis), via a variant <i>&#42;alþaz</i> or perhaps more directly <i>&#42;alþijaz</i> (compare <i>&#42;aldiz</i> (“lifetime, age”), whence <i>𐌰𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (alds)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>older, elder; comparative degree of <i>𐌰𐌻𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (alþeis)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alþizô</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌻𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (alþeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from every direction</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌰</i> (alla) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) completely, wholly, totally, fully, entirely</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls, “all”) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (andeis, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix). Possibly a calque of Koine Greek <i>ὁλοτελεῖς</i> (holoteleîs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfect, all-accomplishing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅</i> (waurstw).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sincerity, agreeability, simplicity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;wērei), from <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;wērs, “true, friendly”) (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēraz</i>) + <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei); alternatively from <i>&#42;𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌰𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;allawērs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>all</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;allaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almighty ruler</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (alls, “all”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;waldands, “ruling, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>than, except, unless</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>besides, apart from</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>other, another</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élyos</i>. The <i>-jis</i> in the nominative singular results from morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the expected outcome of <i>*-jaz</i> in Gothic following a consonant would otherwise have been <i>*-is</i>. Other examples of this development include <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (harjis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis) and <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis).</p></html>:/aljaθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to elsewhere, elsewhither</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis, “other”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸</i> (-þ, location adverb suffix). Compare Ancient Greek <i>ἄλλοθι</i> (állothi).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from elsewhere</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (alja) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of different kin, strange, odd</li><li>unnatural</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis, “other, different”) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (kuni, “kin”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>otherwise, differently</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;aljaleiks, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix), from <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis, “other, different”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks, “-ly, -like”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be differently formed, to differ; to be allegorical</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;aljaleiks, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn, verb-forming suffix), from <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis, “other, different”) + <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks, “-ly, -like”), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀλληγορέω</i> (allēgoréō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>otherwise, differently; comparative degree of <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉</i> (aljaleikō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to cause to grow fat, to fatten</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagerness, zeal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljaną</i> (“to cause to grow, to fatten”), causative of <i>𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (alan).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljaną</i> (“zeal, strength”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be zealous, to strive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljanōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere, somewhere else</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljar</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>other, another</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aljaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élyos</i>. The <i>-jis</i> in the nominative singular results from morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the expected outcome of <i>*-jaz</i> in Gothic following a consonant would otherwise have been <i>*-is</i>. Other examples of this development include <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (harjis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis) and <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐌻𐌷𐍃</i> (alhs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Amminadab, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀμιναδάβ</i> (Aminadáb), from Biblical Hebrew <i>עַמִּינָדָב</i> (ọminadab).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) amen</li></ol><p>From Koine Greek <i>ἀμήν</i> (amḗn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אמן</i> (amén, “so be it”, “surely”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) shoulder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;amsaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃émsos</i>. Germanic cognates include Old Norse <i>áss</i> (“shoulder”); Indo-European cognates include Latin <i>umerus</i>, Sanskrit <i>अंस</i> (áṃsa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>an interrogative particle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;an</i> (“so, then, well; whether”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂en</i> (“there, on the other hand”). Cognate with Old English <i>ono</i> (“if, well, then, now”). Compare also Old High German <i>inu</i>, <i>ino</i> (“well, yea, though, because, if, for example”), Old Saxon <i>inu</i> (“well, yea, because, namely”), Old English <i>eno</i> (“if, well, then, now”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) onto, to, at (directed towards)</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) on, at (stationary)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>added to verbs</i>) on, onto, to, against</li><li>Indicates perfectivity in some verbs.</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (ana).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add, to augment</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (aukan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command, instruct, order</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anabeudaną</i>, from <i>&#42;ana</i> + <i>&#42;beudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commandment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anabūsniz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌿𐍃𐌽𐍃</i> (*būsns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to approach, come</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to get drunk</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (drigkan).</p></html>:/ˈanakʷal/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) quiet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌰𐌻</i> (&#42;qal, from a variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwalō</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to denounce, censure, blaspheme</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html>:/anaˈkʷiman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to come upon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to revitalize, rekindle</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (ana) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;qiujan), or directly from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (qius) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈanakʷiss/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”), or from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (anaqiþan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take hold of, possess</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (habān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present, at home</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌼𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;haimeis, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimijaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) to invoke, beseech, call upon</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to scold, reprimand</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to get dressed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌼𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;hamōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lay down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hnaiwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down to, bow to, bow down to</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (hneiwan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) anathema, a curse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀνάθεμα</i> (anáthema).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to enslave, subjugate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;þiwan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewāną</i>. Compare <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (þius).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in good spirits, relieve, comfort</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐌰𐍆𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þrafstjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to impart or confer through argumentation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (insakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to overburden, overload</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kaurjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down at a table (for a meal), to recline</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌺𐌿𐌼𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;kumbjan), from Latin <i>&#42;cumbō</i>; compare <i>accumbō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) reading</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (anakunnān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read, especially to teach or learn through reading</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (kunnan). May be a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀναγιγνώσκω</i> (anagignṓskō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly</li></ol><p>Connected by Pokorny to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;naglъ</i> (“sudden, quick, hasty”), both of which he derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂en-</i> (“onto, on”), similar to English <i>on</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a putting-on or laying-on of something on something, an imposition</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (analagjan, “lay upon, put upon”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay upon</li><li>to inflict (wounds)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;analagjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) secrecy</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌽𐍃</i> (analaugns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei); from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugnaz</i> (“hidden”) (compare <i>&#42;laugnijaną</i> (“to conceal, deny”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) secretly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌽𐍃</i> (analaugns, “secret, concealed”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, “adverb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret, concealed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;laugns). See also <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laugnjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prevent, hinder</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be without opportunity</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (latjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;latjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) likewise, similarly</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;analeiks, “similar”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix), from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (ana) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌸</i> (anamahtjaiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do wrong</li><li>to defraud</li><li>to do violence</li><li>to ill-treat, abuse</li><li>to accuse falsely</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (anamahts, “tyranny, reproach”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tyranny</li><li>reproach</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (mahts)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to register, enroll</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan).</p></html>:/ˈanaminds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) surmise, suspicion, supposition</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mindiz</i>. The suffix is equivalent to <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to name, call</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌰𐌼𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (namnjan).</p></html>:/anaˈnanθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, be brave (enough), be bold (enough)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;nanþjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nanþijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to compel, to force</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (nauþjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renew</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;niujan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) renewal</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ananiujan, “renew”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”)</p></html>:/anaˈpraŋɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to trouble</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍀𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;praggan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pranganą</i>.</p></html>:/anaˈsilaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to cease, stop, fall silent</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (*silān), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silāną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈanasiu̯ns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (siuns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to quiet down, to become still</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (slawan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall asleep</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌻𐌴𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (slēpan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beginning, start</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (anastōdjan, “begin”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;stōdjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōdijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to build upon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to press upon, to crowd upon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌼𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;trimpan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trimpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the future, to come</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (-wairþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to mar, stain</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wammjan, “to stain”), which is related to the noun <i>𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼</i> (wamm, “stain, flaw”) and adjective <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼𐍃</i> (gawamms, “unclean”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equanimity, moderation, dignity</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;anawiljis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), from <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;wiljis, “willing(?)”); compare <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (wilja, “will”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entrust with, to give for safekeeping</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (filhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to give someone a break, to give rest or repose</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕeilān, “to pause, rest, cease”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) along, through, into, against</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anda</i>, related to the prefix <i>&#42;anda-</i> whence Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-). Possibly continues Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entó</i>; compare Proto-Tocharian <i>&#42;ā́ntë</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>un-, de-</li><li>Verbal prefix signifying an action "against" or "toward".</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anda-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anda-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) ransom</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;bauhts), from <i>𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bugjan, “to buy”); or directly from an unattested verb <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;andabugjan, “to ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) punishment, castigation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍄</i> (&#42;beit), the latter element being from <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (beitan, “to bite”). Or possibly from an unattested verb <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;andabeitan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confession, profession</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄</i> (&#42;hait, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haitą</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reply, answer</li><li>defense</li><li>verdict, sentence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;hafts), the second element thought to have been a noun (different from the attested adjective <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (hafts)) related to <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (haftjan, “to hold [on], adhere to”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensible, rational, thoughtful</li></ol><p>Apparently a past participle or verbal adjective to a verb very close to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (andþagkjan). Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanhtaz</i> (“thought, idea”) is closely related but is a noun, whereas this is an adjective. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) recompense, requital, reward</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌽</i> (laun, “reward”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (nahts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposite, opposed, hostile</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;neiþs), related to <i>𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌸</i> (neiþ, “hate, malice”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) an instance of taking or accepting</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (andniman) or a variant thereof.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acceptable, pleasant</li><li>accepted</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (andniman) or a variant thereof, or from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌴𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;nēms), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēmiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) taking, accepting, holding fast</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽𐌴𐌼</i> (andanēm) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acceptable, pleasant</li><li>accepted</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (andniman) or a variant thereof, or from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌴𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;nēms), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēmiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking up, acceptance</li></ol><p>From a variant of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (andniman) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs), or from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌿𐌼𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;numts), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;numþiz</i>.</p></html>:/anˈdɔːɣi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>countenance, face</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌿𐌲𐍉</i> (augō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, in plain sight</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌹</i> (andaugi, “countenance”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, “adverb-forming suffix”) or from a closely related (unattested) adjective with the same suffix.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, in plain sight</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌹</i> (andaugi, “countenance”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, “adverb-forming suffix”) or from a closely related (unattested) adjective with the same suffix.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abominable</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (andsitan) or a variant thereof, or from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌴𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;sēts), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sētiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opponent, adversary</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (andstandan) (thus lacking the n-infix that marks the present tense) or a variant thereof. The suffix appears identical to the <i>-jis</i> in <i>-𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (-tōjis). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially&#32;law</i>) adversary</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳</i> (and, “against”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌰</i> (staua, “judge”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) purchase price, cost</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸</i> (wairþ, “worth, value”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐌹</i> (andawaurdi)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answer, response</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawurdiją</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). Cognates include Dutch <i>antwoord</i>, German <i>Antwort</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>livelihood, wages</li><li>necessity, something essential</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌶𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;wizns), of uncertain origin; possibly related to <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to be, remain”) and/or <i>𐍅𐌹𐌶𐍉𐌽</i> (wizōn, “to lead a life”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>countenance, face</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰-</i> (anda-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌶𐌽</i> (&#42;wleizn), of uncertain origin. Perhaps related to <i>𐍅𐌻𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (wlits). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, ministry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtiją</i> (“service”). Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (andbahts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/ˈand.bah.ti.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve, to administer, to perform</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtijaną</i> (“to serve”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant, minister</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtaz</i> (“servant”), with hypercorrection due to the influence of compounds in <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite back at, to snap at</li><li>to rebuke, to scold</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (beitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbind, to loose, to release</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andabindaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (bindan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be loosened</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (andbundans, past participle of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (andbindan)) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html>:/ˈan.diːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end (final point in space or time)</li><li>end (cessation)</li><li>end (purpose)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andijaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entíos</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>ende</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἀντίος</i> (antíos, “facing, opposite”) and Sanskrit <i>अन्त्य</i> (antya, “last”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say farewell, to take leave</li><li>to greet, to salute, to address</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acknowledge, to confess, to admit</li><li>to profess, to praise, to honour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andahaitaną</i>; compare Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;andahaitan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to divest oneself of</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌼𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;hamōn, “to clothe oneself”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamōną</i>. Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀπεκδύομαι</i> (apekdúomai).</p></html>:/andˈhɔːs.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hearken, heed, listen to</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan, “to hear”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reply, to respond, to answer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hafjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncovering, revealing, revelation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (andhuljan, “to reveal”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to uncover, to reveal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (huljan). Compare Dutch <i>onthullen</i>, German <i>enthüllen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to investigate, explore, raise a question</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐍂𐌿𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (*hruskān). Glosses Ancient Greek <i>ἀνακρίνω</i> (anakrínō).</p></html>:/andˈθaŋkjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to decide, make up one's mind</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;reflexive</i>) to remember, consider, think of</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þagkjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) either (introduces the first of two options)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (andeis, “end”) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus, “devoid of”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be released, to be separated</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;lētnan), fientive in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) related to <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accept, take in, adopt</li><li>to receive (in return)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳</i> (and) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andrew, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀνδρέας</i> (Andréas).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute, argue</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-, “along”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to speak against, oppose, dispute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andasakaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay or offer as tribute</li><li>to sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (saljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disturbed by, to shrink from</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (sitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to despise, reject</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (speiwan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supply, provision, purveyance</li></ol><p>From or closely related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (andstaldan, “to supply, provide”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to supply, put at someone's disposal, provide</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;staldan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staldaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand against, to oppose</li><li>to withstand, to resist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andastandaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html>:/andˈstɔrran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to murmur against, scold</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturnāną</i>. More at <i>&#42;staraz</i> (“rigid”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold to, be devoted to</li><li>to accept (endure patiently)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;tilōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tilōną</i> (“to strive, reach”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presence (often used with <i><i>𐌹𐌽</i> (in) + dative case</i> to mean <i>in face of</i> or <i>in the presence of.. </i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (andwairþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+ dative) opposite</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (andwairþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present (in the immediate vicinity)</li><li>momentary, lasting a moment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawardaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (-wairþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present (in the immediate vicinity)</li><li>momentary, lasting a moment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawardaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (-wairþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to answer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;andawurdijaną</i>.</p></html>:/andˈwasjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to strip (<i>a person (accusative)</i>) of (<i>clothing (dative)</i>); to take (<i>clothing (dative)</i>) off (<i>a person (accusative)</i>)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌽𐌳-</i> (and-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wasjan)</p></html>:/ˈan.θar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>the other of two, the one that is different from the one just mentioned</li><li>the second (numeral)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anþeraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énteros</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>ōþer</i> and Old Norse <i>annarr</i>, and Sanskrit <i>अन्तर</i> (antara, “different”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (anþarana) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌹𐍃</i> (anþaris) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otherness, difference</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;anþarleiks, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (anþar) + <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) otherwise, differently</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;anþarleiks, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix), from <i>𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (anþar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anna, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἄννα</i> (Ánna).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>income of a soldier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>annōna</i> (“ration”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌼𐌱𐌴𐌹</i> (anakumbei) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden beam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ansaz</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) merciful</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌽𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (ansts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grace, beneficence, favour</li><li>joy, thankfulness</li><li>gift, blessing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anstiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌴𐌹𐌸</i> (anastōdeiþ)</li></ol></html>:/ˈan.ti.ɔ.ki.a/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Antakya (a city in Turkey), the ancient Antioch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀντιόχεια</i> (Antiókheia).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eternity</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌾𐌿𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;ajuks, “eternal”, from <i>&#42;aiwukiz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌳𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (-dūþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) window (<i>literally, "eye-door"</i>)</li></ol><p>Apparently, from <i>𐌰𐌿𐌲𐍉</i> (augō, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍉</i> (daurō, “door”). The second element differs from this word in gender and inflection and is a <i>plurale tantum</i>, which makes the connection slightly problematic. Cognate with Old English <i>eagduru</i>, Old High German <i>ougatora</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;augijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;augô</i>, whence also Old English <i>ēage</i> (English <i>eye</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>oculus</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oko</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil eye</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bliss, fortune, happiness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (audags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to bless someone or consider them to be blessed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (audags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blessed, fortunate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audagaz</i> (“blessed, fortunate”); equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌰𐌿𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;auds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags). Compare Finnish <i>autuas</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɔːðaˌhaɸts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fortunate, blessed</li></ol><p>From unattested <i>&#42;𐌰𐌿𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;auds, “fortune, prosperity”) + <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (hafts, “bound, laden with”); the first element is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɔː.zan.dil/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the morning star: Venus, Lucifer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Auziwandilaz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>ēarendel</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌿𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (auhumists)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) furnace, oven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uhnaz</i> (“oven”). As the only attestation in Gothic is in the accusative singular - the form of which is identical between masculine and neuter a-stem nouns - this word could also have been a neuter a-stem; however, the gender of its Germanic cognates makes that very unlikely.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumult</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (auhjōn, “make noise, clamor”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make an uproar, be noisy and tumultuous</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>higher, superior</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ufumô</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍆</i> (uf) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma), with dissimilation of the labial <i>f</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highest, greatest; superlative degree of <i>𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (auhuma)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (auhuma) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html>:/ˈɔh.sa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox, neat (adult bovine specimen)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uhsô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;uksḗn</i> (“bull”), akin to English <i>ox</i>, German <i>Ochse</i>, and Icelandic <i>oxi</i>, and Sanskrit <i>उक्षन्</i> (ukṣan). A variant u-stem lemma <i>*auhsus</i> was once thought to underlie the forms <i>auhsau</i> and <i>*auhsum</i> (superseded reading, see below), but this view is no longer current among scholars.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deserted, desolate, barren</li><li>wild</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþijaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɔː.θi.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wilderness, desert</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (auþeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌳𐌰</i> (-ida)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>for, because</li><li>but also</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auk</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewg-</i>. Cognate with English <i>eke</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aukaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɔːkan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aukaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to grow larger, become increased</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (aukan, “to grow, increase”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html>:/ˈɔːrahjoːm/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tombs (<i>dative plural</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auraz</i> (“earth, mud”).</p></html>:/ˈɔː.raː.li/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) kerchief, napkin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>ōrārium</i> (“napkin”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jug</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>urceus</i> (“jug, mug”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orchard, garden</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;aurts) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards). The first element of the compound seemingly derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;urtiz</i>. According to Lehmann and others, however, the first element of this compound is simply Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;orto</i> (“garden”), perhaps its genitive form, from <i>hortus</i>; compare Italian <i>orto</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>ortġeard</i> (English <i>orchard</i>); this is taken by Lehmann to be a parallel formation, not a common inheritance from Proto-Germanic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husbandman, tenant farmer, vineyard worker</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;aurts, “garden”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, occupational suffix); for the etymology of the former, see <i>𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (aurtigards).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ausô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ows-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perhaps (indicates a lack of certainty)</li><li>by chance, haply</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) apostleship</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (apaustaulus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈapɔstɔlus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀπόστολος</i> (apóstolos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (apaustaulus)</li></ol></html>:/ˈa.ra/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) eagle (bird)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃érō</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>earn</i>, Old High German <i>aro</i>, Old Norse <i>ǫrn</i>, and Hittite <i>𒄩𒀀𒊏𒀸</i> (ḫāras).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a peninsula of West Asia between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἀραβία</i> (Arabía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Aram, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀράμ</i> (Arám), from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֲרָם</i> (ʾĂrām).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, labor, toil</li><li>to strive, struggle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbaidijaną</i>. Cognate with German <i>arbeiten</i>, Dutch <i>arbeiden</i>, Old Saxon <i>arvedian</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labour, work, toil</li><li>hardship, distress</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbaidiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heritage, inheritance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbiją</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an heir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbijanumjô</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌹</i> (arbi, “inheritance”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌿𐌼𐌾𐌰</i> (&#42;numja), the latter part (which is not attested outside of this compound) being an agent noun formed from <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman, “to take”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbijô</i>. See also <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌹</i> (arbi, “heritage”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent noun”).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heritage, inheritance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbiją</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an heiress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arbijǭ</i>, feminine equivalent of <i>&#42;arbijô</i> (“heir”), whence <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌾𐌰</i> (arbja, “heir”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>arfa</i> (“heiress”), feminine equivalent of <i>arfi</i> (“heir”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Abrahamic&#32;religion</i>) ark</li><li>money box, chest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>arca</i>.</p></html>:/ˈarkaŋɡilus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) archangel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχάγγελος</i> (arkhángelos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) mercy, compassion</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (armahairts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), calque of Latin <i>misericordia</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (armahairts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html>:/armahɛrts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) merciful, compassionate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (arms) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍉</i> (hairtō), calque of Latin <i>misericors</i>. Compare the similarly calqued Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;armahert(ī)</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms, charity</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (arms, “poor”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. suffix?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take pity, show compassion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) poor, wretched</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armaz</i> (“arm”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armaz</i> (“poor”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>most wretched; superlative degree of <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (arms)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armōstaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (arms) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ōsts).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) securely, surely</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌰𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;arns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba). The former element derives from <i>&#42;arniz</i>, whence English <i>earnest</i> can also ultimately be derived, and the latter element is an adverb-forming suffix.</p></html>:/ˈar.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to plough (to use a plough)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éryeti</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>erian</i>, Old Norse <i>erja</i>, and to the first element of Koine Greek <i>ἀροτριάω</i> (arotriáō), which it translates.</p></html>:/ar.tak.sɛrk.sus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Artaxerxes (Persian king)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρταξέρξης</i> (Artaxérxēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌰𐍂𐍄𐌰𐌺𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌺𐍃𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (artaksairksaus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for free, without charge, free of charge</li><li>without cause, for nothing</li></ol><p>Probably related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arwaz</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈar.ʍaz.na/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) missile (air-based weapon), arrow, dart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arhwō</i> (“arrow”) + <i>-𐍃𐌽𐌰</i> (-sna).</p></html>:/aˈroːmata/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;attested&#32;only in plural) sweet spices</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἀρώματα</i> (arṓmata), plural of <i>ἄρωμα</i> (árōma, “herb, spice”).</p></html>:/ˈa.sans/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest (yield of harvesting)</li><li>harvest (season of gathering the ripened crop)</li><li>summer (hottest season)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;azaniz</i> (“harvest”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;esenь</i>.</p></html>:/aˈsi.a/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Asia (an ancient province of the Roman Empire in western Anatolia, existing between 133 BC (during the Roman Republic) and the 7th century AD)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀσία</i> (Asía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) millstone</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (asilus, “donkey”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;qairnus, “quern”, from <i>&#42;kwernuz</i>). Compare Old English <i>esolcweorn</i>.</p></html>:/ˈa.si.lus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey (animal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>asellus</i>, or possibly from an assimilated form of Late Latin <i>asinus</i>. Compare Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;asil</i> (whence Old English <i>esol</i> and Old High German <i>esil</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day laborer, hireling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;asnijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a low-value copper coin, a penny</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>assārius</i>.</p></html>:/ˈastaθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certainty</li></ol></html>:/ˈasts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch (woody part of a tree arising from the trunk)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;astaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ósdos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) at (a place, a point in time)</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) at (a time)</li><li>(<i>with present participle in dative</i>) while, when</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;at</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix indicating presence, proximity, or intention&#47;action towards&#47;at something. Sometimes indicates perfectivity.</li></ol><p>The preposition <i>𐌰𐍄</i> (at) used as a prefix, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;at</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éd</i>; compare Old English <i>æt-</i>, Latin <i>ad-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) year</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>𐌰𐌸𐌽𐌰𐌼</i> (aþnam, “years”) (dative plural, from a lemma <i>&#42;𐌰𐌸𐌽</i> (&#42;aþn) or <i>&#42;𐌰𐌸𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;aþns)), from an equally uncertain Proto-Germanic etymon Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aþna-</i>, which is related to Latin <i>annus</i>, Oscan <i>akno-</i> (“year, holiday, time of offering”), and Umbrian <i>acnu</i> (“years”). The prefix is <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-); the suffix is <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to bring before</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (augjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring (to), to present (to), to offer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come up to</li><li>to access</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html>:/atɡaˈrɛhtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to set in order</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (garaihtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give (to), to deliver (into)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;atgebaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (giban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to carry</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (dragan).</p></html>:/atˈdriu̯.san/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall towards, down, or into something</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (driusan).</p></html>:/atˈhabaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to come up to, approach</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (habān).</p></html>:/atˈhaːhan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to let down (allow to descend)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (hāhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐍄𐌷𐌰𐌷𐌹𐌸</i> (athāhiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to summon, call</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lift down (from), to take down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hafjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, drag or pull towards</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (þinsan).</p></html>:/ˈa.tisk/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornfields, cropland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;atiska-</i> (“grainfield”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂edos-</i> (“sort of cereal, grain”), related to Old English <i>āte</i> (“oat”), Latin <i>ador</i> (“spelt”), Hittite (ḫattāi-, “to cut (grain)”).</p><p>Germanic cognates include Old High German <i>ezzisc</i> (German <i>Esch</i>), Old Saxon <i>etisk</i>, Middle Dutch <i>esch</i> (Dutch <i>es</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to acknowledge, [duly] grant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (kunnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay, set or cast something down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, to bid one come, to invite in or forth</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (laþōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be present with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (ligan). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>παράκειμαι</i> (parákeimai).</p></html>:/atˈneːʍjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;reflexive</i>) to arrive; (<i>in the past tense</i>) to be at hand</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;reflexive</i>) to approach, come near, be near</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌴𐍈𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (nēƕjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to take to a place, transfer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to run up to (somewhere or something)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-, “at, to”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set before, to present</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan).</p></html>:/atˈsnarpjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to handle</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌽𐌰𐍂𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;snarpjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, halt</li><li>to come in or against someone or something</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to descend</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (steigan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;attô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;átta</i>, whence also Old High German <i>atto</i> (Middle High German <i>atte</i>, Southern German <i>Ätti</i>), archaic Dutch <i>ette</i> (“judge”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to make contact with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌴𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (tēkan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring (to), to convey, to bring up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (tiuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw (to), to hurl forth (to)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (wairpan).</p></html>:/atˈwalwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to roll (<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>𐌳𐌿</b> (du) <small>‘up to’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;walwjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walwijaną</i>.</p></html>:/atˈwandjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to return</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to be”).</p></html>:/atˈwitɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) observation (act of observing or being observed)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witān, “to observe”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to (here), to summon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐍉𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wōpjan).</p></html>:/atˈfarjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to arrive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (farjan).</p></html>:/ˈa.weːθi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock of sheep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awidiją</i> with devoicing maybe due to Thurneysen's Law. Cognate to Old English <i>ēowde</i> and Old High German <i>ouwiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (awiliuþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give thanks</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (awiliuþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer of gratitude, thanksgiving</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (liuþ) with an uncertain first element. Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awi-</i> (“help ~ thanks”), potentially found as a borrowing in Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;api</i> (“help”, noun). Pokorny further derives this from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hew(H)-</i> (“to help”); compare Proto-Norse <i>ᚨᚢᛃᚨ</i> (auja, “good fortune”), Old Saxon <i>ālat</i> (“thanks”), and possibly Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;audanaz</i> (“granted, given”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈa.wistr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sheepfold (enclosure for keeping sheep)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awistrą</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>ēwestre</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) grandmother</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éwh₂os</i>. Cognate with Hittite <i>𒄷𒀪𒄩𒀸</i> (ḫu-uḫ-ḫa-as), Old Prussian <i>awis</i>, Latin <i>ava</i>, Old Armenian <i>հաւ</i> (haw) and Old Irish <i>aue</i>.</p></html>:/aɸ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+ dative</i>) 's, of, from, by, away from, out of</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ab</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>off, away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aba-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare away, to frighten off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;agjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renounce, to forswear, to disavow</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;aikan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aikaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to misguide, to deceive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (airzjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) after, following (in time)</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) after, following (in space)</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) according to, in accordance with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;afar</i>. Cognate to Albanian <i>afër</i> (“close, nearby”), Sanskrit <i>अपर</i> (ápara, “posterior, later, following”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) descendant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aferô</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (afar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go after, to follow</li><li>to accompany</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (afar) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) morrow, next day</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (afar) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (dags).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (afar) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laistjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) only used in <i>𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃 𐌰𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍃𐌰𐌱𐌱𐌰𐍄𐌴</i> (dags afarsabbatē)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (afar) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌱𐌱𐌰𐍄𐍉</i> (sabbatō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become blind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌻𐌹𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (blinds, “blind”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, go out, go aside</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to depart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abagebaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (giban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) ungodliness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌲𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (afguþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godless, ungodly, impious</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ). Compare Dutch <i>afgod</i>, German <i>Abgott</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the deep, abyss</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆</i> (af) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;grundus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa). Compare Old English <i>æfgrynde</i>, Dutch <i>afgrond</i>, German <i>Abgrund</i>.</p></html>:/afˈdɛːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to give, distribute, share</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dailjan)</p></html>:/afˈdɔːbnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become numb, blind, hard, stubborn</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (daufs, “hardened”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put to death, to kill off, to eliminate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dauþjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) be silent, be still</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌿𐌼𐌱𐍃</i> (dumbs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to cast down, to thrown down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;drausjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunkard, winebibber</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (drigkan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) be silent, hold one's peace</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐍉𐌱𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;dōbnan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōbnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) condemnation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (afdōmjan, “to condemn”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌰𐍆𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌸</i> (afdōmjaiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn</li><li>to curse</li><li>to abjure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dōmjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to confuse, trouble, vex</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐍉𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;dōjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōwijaną</i>; see also <i>&#42;dawjaną</i> (“to die”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an eater, glutton</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌴𐍄𐌾𐌰</i> (&#42;ētja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forsake</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to abstain (with <i>𐌰𐍆</i> (af, “from”))</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (habān)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>away from home</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌼𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;haimeis, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimijaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to undress</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌼𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;hamōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamōną</i>.</p></html>:/afˈhlaθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lade, weigh down, oppress, burden</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-, “off”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hlaþan, “to load”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to bewitch, lead astray</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hugjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to wipe off, to clean off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hrainjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off, wipe off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrisjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to cheat (someone out of something), to take (something from someone) by false accusation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍉𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (hōlōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be thirsty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;þaursjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurzijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ters-</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>तृष्यति</i> (tṛṣyati).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to flee</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þliuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þwahan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) remission</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (aflagjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay down, put away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abalagjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, withdraw</li><li>to travel, take a journey</li><li>to give place, make room</li><li>used to translate Ancient Greek <i>παρατηρέω</i> (paratēréō, “watch”), but this may be a mistranslation of the Greek</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐍆𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (aflētan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐍆𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰</i> (aflētanda)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remission, forgiveness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; stem of <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to relinquish</li><li>to absolve, to release, to let off</li><li>to forgive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to relinquish</li><li>to absolve, to release, to let off</li><li>to forgive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to depart</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;linnan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;linnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain, abide</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐍆𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;lifnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to chop off, to sever</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (maitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceitfulness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (afmarzjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offend</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (marzjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to tire, become fatigued</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐍉𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;mōjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to remove</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abanemaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) divorce</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (afsatjan, “to divorce”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set aside, to put away, to remove</li><li>to dismiss, to divorce</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate off, to sever</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (skaidan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast away (discard)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌹𐌿𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skiuban), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skeubaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li><li>to kill, slay</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (slahan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slahaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be amazed, to be dumbfounded, to become speechless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;slauþnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in doubt, be in despair</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;slauþjan). According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)lew-</i> (“slack; loose; limp”), see also Old Norse <i>slokna</i> (“to go out”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to put off, lay aside</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;slaupjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slaupijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostasy</li><li>divorce</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-, “off-”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;stass, “[act of] standing”) or from <i>𐌰𐍆𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (afstandan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs); thus literally, "[act of] standing apart", in its attested uses in the Gothic Bible specifically a separation from an earlier state of affairs. Renders Koine Greek <i>ἀποστασία</i> (apostasía) and <i>ἀποστάσιον</i> (apostásion), of which it may be at least a partial calque or semantic loan.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come down</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (steigan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stīganą</i>. Compare German <i>absteigen</i>, Dutch <i>afstijgen</i> (“go down, dismount”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to despair</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swaggwjan), possibly cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swangijan</i> (“to cause to swing”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to blot out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swairban), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerbaną</i>; cognate with Old Saxon <i>swervan</i> (“to wipe”) and Old English <i>sweorfan</i> (“to scour”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) behind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftai</i>. The single occurrence of this word in the Gothic corpus was considered by Streitberg to have been a misreading for <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍂𐌰</i> (aftra). There have since been objections to this interpretation, however, for which see Lehmann (1986).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from behind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftanē</i>; cognate with <i>&#42;afteraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be torn, rent</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of <i>&#42;𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;aftairan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) (compare <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (gataurnan)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌹𐌸</i> (aftaurniþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;afterô</i>; cognate with <i>&#42;afteraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to pull away, to draw away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abateuhaną</i>; cognates include Old High German <i>abaziohan</i> (German <i>abziehen</i>), Old Saxon <i>aftiohan</i>, Old English <i>oftēon</i>, Middle Dutch <i>aftien</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐍆</i> (af) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (tiuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) last, final</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftumô</i>, from the same root as <i>&#42;afteraz</i> with the suffix <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very last, last of all</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftumistaz</i>, from the same root as <i>&#42;afteraz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (aftuma) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li><li>back</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) revival, renewal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍂𐌰</i> (aftra) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (anastōdeins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to move away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast off, to throw off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (wairpan).</p></html>:/afˈwalwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll away (transitive)</li></ol><p><i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;walwjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walwijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (cause to) turn away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan). Compare Dutch <i>afwenden</i>, German <i>abwenden</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to hide</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (filhan).</p></html>:/afˈʍap.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be choked</li><li>to be quenched (<i>of a fire</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍀𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ƕapnan), fientive variant in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) of <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ƕapjan, from <i>&#42;hwapjaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke off, to asphyxiate, to suffocate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌰𐍆-</i> (af-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ƕapjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwapjaną</i> (“to breathe”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ékʷeh₂</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbaɡms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree (large woody plant)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bagmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;badją</i>. Cognates include English <i>bed</i>, Dutch <i>bed</i>, German <i>Bett</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>both, one and the other (referring to exactly two items)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bai</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>bēġen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, compel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baidijaną</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>bǣdan</i> (“to compel”) and Old Norse <i>beiða</i> (“to compel”).</p></html>:/ˈbɛ.raˌbaɡms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sycamine</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰</i> (&#42;baira) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌲𐌼𐍃</i> (bagms), literally “bear-tree”. (compare Swedish <i>björnbär</i> (“blackberry”, literally “bear-berry”)), though Lehmann (1986) mentions the possibility that the first element may be folk-etymologically altered from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰers-</i> (“point, tip”) and/or from a word related to <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (barizeins, “made from barley”) (compare Old Norse <i>barr</i> (“leaf of a conifer; barley”)).</p></html>:/ˈbɛran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, to carry, to bring forth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéreti</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beroea, an ancient city in Thrace near modern Stara Zagora</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Βέροια</i> (Béroia).</p></html>:/ˈbɛr.ɡa.hiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill country, highland, mountainous region</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌲𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (&#42;bairgahs, “hilly, mountainous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), from <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌲𐍃</i> (&#42;bairgs, “hill, mountain”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bergaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerǵʰ-</i> (“high, elevated”). Translates Ancient Greek <i>ὀρεινή (χώρα</i> (oreinḗ (khṓra, “mountainous (area)”).</p></html>:/ˈbɛrɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep (maintain the condition of, preserve) [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;berganą</i> (“to preserve, keep”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brightly, clearly</li><li>openly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (bairhts, “bright, clear, manifest”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, “adverb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation</li><li>publicity, openness, manifestness</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (bairhts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make clear, show, disclose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;birhtijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (bairhts, “bright, clear, manifest”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, denominative suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make clear, show, disclose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;birhtijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (bairhts, “bright, clear, manifest”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, denominative suffix).</p></html>:/bɛrxts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, clear</li><li>manifest, evident</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;berhtaz</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>beorht</i> (English <i>bright</i>), Old High German <i>beraht</i>, Old Saxon <i>berht</i>, Old Norse <i>bjartr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bitterly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (baitrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitterness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (baitrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bitter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baitraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, sack</li><li>wineskin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balgiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boldly, frankly, openly</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;balþs, “bold”, from <i>&#42;balþaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, adverb-forming suffix).</p></html>:/ˈbalθiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boldness</li><li>openness, frankness, plainness of speech</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balþį̄</i> (“boldness”), from <i>&#42;balþaz</i> (“bold”). By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;balþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈbalθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be bold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balþijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;balþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈbal.joːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to roar (of animals, to make a loud deep noise)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baljōną</i> (compare <i>&#42;bellaną</i>). Cognate to Old Norse <i>belja</i> (“to bellow, roar”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balm, balsam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>balsamum</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wickedness, malice</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;balus, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balwaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punishment, torture</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌻𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (balwjan, “torment”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torture, torment</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;balus, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;balwaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bandī</i> (“fetter, bond”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prisoner</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (bindan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sign, indication</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bandwjan, “to signify, mark, indicate”), see there for further etymology.<i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈbandwiduh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍅𐌹𐌳𐌰</i> (bandwida) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to signify, show, indicate</li><li>to beckon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bandwijaną</i> (“to show, signify”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, token</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bandwjan, “to signify, mark, indicate”), see there for further etymology.<i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;banjō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barn, storehouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;banstiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈba.joːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>both (referring to each of two items or of two sets of items)</li></ol><p>From or closely related to <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (bai), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bai</i>. The last element is of disputed origin; it may possibly be from a neuter dual or plural <i>*bajō</i> followed by a form of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sa</i>; thus, it would resemble the formation of Old Norse <i>báðir</i>. A derivation with the suffix of <i>𐌼𐌴𐌽𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (mēnōþs) is also formally possible, but is judged less likely by Lehmann (1986).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling, residence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌰𐌽</i> (bauan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains).</p></html>:/ˈbɔːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell, inhabit, live, reside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;būaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boanerges, an epithet given by Jesus to the disciples James and John.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Βοανεργές</i> (Boanergés).</p></html>:/bɔːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deaf</li><li>mute</li><li>flavorless, dull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bauþaz</i>, <i>&#42;baudaz</i> (“dull, deaf”), of uncertain origin. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰew-</i> (“to strike”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>son</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;buriz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐍃</i> (baurgs, “city”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, occupational suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castle, fort</li><li>polis, city</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burgz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerǵʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city wall</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐍃</i> (baurgs) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;waddjus).</p></html>:/ˈbɔrθiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burþį̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbarian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros).</p></html>:/ˈba.ri.ziːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of barley</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barizaz</i> + <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bosom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barmiz</i>, <i>&#42;barmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barną</i> (“child”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young child, especially one's own</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (barn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌻𐍉</i> (-ilō).</p></html>:/ˈbarniskiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childish thing</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (barnisks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html>:/ˈbarniski/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childhood</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (barnisks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/ˈbarnisks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childlike, being a child</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;barniskaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (barn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks). Cognate with Old Norse <i>bernskr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to show loyalty towards, to show (familial) piety towards</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>better; comparative degree of <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gōþs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;batizô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>best; superlative degree of <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gōþs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;batistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Germanic elements <i>&#42;bataz</i> (“good”) + <i>&#42;winiz</i> (“friend”). It is uncertain whether this is a Proto-Germanic formation or whether the name postdates Proto-Germanic. No equivalent names are attested in other Germanic languages which might suggest a common Germanic origin.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌱𐌴𐌸𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌼</i> (bēþlaihaim)</li></ol></html>:/beːθ.lɛ.hɛːm/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethlehem (a city in the West Bank, Palestine), believed to be the birthplace of Jesus.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Βηθλεέμ</i> (Bēthleém) or Latin <i>Bēthlehēmum</i>, ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>בֵּית לֶחֶם</i> (bêṯ leḥem).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌱𐌹𐌸𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌼𐌹𐍃</i> (biþlaihaimis)</li></ol></html>:/ˈbiːðan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to await, to wait for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bīdaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbiːst/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaven (agent used to make dough rise)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bīstą</i>. Related to <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (beitan, “to bite”) (compare Old High German <i>billa</i> (“unleavened bread”) and Old English <i>andbita</i> (“yeast”)).</p></html>:/ˈbiːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bītaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeyd-</i> (“to split”).</p></html>:/ˈbeːrusjoːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parents</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bērusjaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative or dative</i>) near, at (a place)</li><li>(<i>+accusative or dative</i>) at (a point in time)</li><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) about (concerning someone or something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bi</i>. See also <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>A general prefix adding transitivity to verbs</li><li>A prefix, added to verbs regardless of transitivity, forming transitive verbs with a sense of 'throughout', 'adjacency', 'enclosure', and sometimes indicating perfectivity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bi-</i>; cognate with English <i>be-</i>. See also 𐌱𐌹 (bi).</p></html>:/biˈabrjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be astonished</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌱𐍂𐍃</i> (abrs, “severe, extreme”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/biˈɔːkan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase</li><li>to add, give more</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (aukan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, to grow, to abound</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (auknan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work towards, to strive for</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (arbaidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camp, fortified place</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;bibaurgjan, “set camp, fortify”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), the verb from <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) + <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;baurgjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burgijaną</i> (whence also English <i>bury</i>, Dutch <i>borgen</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind up, bind around</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bibindaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (bindan). Cognate with Old Frisian <i>bibinda</i>, Old English <i>bebindan</i>.</p></html>:/biˈɡɛrdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to gird oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bigerdaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gairdan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to discover</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bigetaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround through digging (by means of trenches)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bigrabaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (graban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a request, plea, prayer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bedō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) beggar</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌱𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bidjan, “to beg”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌱𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌸</i> (bidjaiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to request, ask</li><li>to pray, beg</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bidjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰedʰ-</i>.</p></html>:/biˈdoːmjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dōmjan).</p></html>:/biˈkʷiman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come upon (befall, affect)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bikwemaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman). Cognate with German <i>bekommen</i>, Dutch <i>bekomen</i>, and English <i>become</i>. Glosses Ancient Greek <i>ἐφίστημι</i> (ephístēmi).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slander, false rumors</li><li>abusive language</li></ol><p>From an unattested verb <i>&#42;𐌱𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;bihaitan), from <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) + <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braggart, boaster</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌱𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;bihaitan, “to brag, speak abusively”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, occupational suffix). See <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh at someone; to ridicule, mock</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hlahjan, “to laugh”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bethink; to think about something</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biþankijaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þagkjan).</p></html>:/biˈθeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>later, afterward</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>when, as</li><li>as soon as</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌴</i> (þē).</p></html>:/biˈθeːh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>when</li><li>after</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹𐌸𐌴</i> (biþē) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌱𐌴𐌸𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌼</i> (bēþlaihaim)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run ahead</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þragjan, “to run”).</p></html>:/biˈθwahan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wash</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þwahan).</p></html>:/biˈkuk.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss all over, to cover with kisses; to bekiss</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kukjan, “kiss”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make known</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;kunþjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunþijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave behind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;laibjan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laibijaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌲𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;laigōn, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laigōną</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyǵʰ-</i> (“to lick”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make fun of, to mock, to ridicule</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (laikan).</p></html>:/biˈlɛːf/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) meaning unknown, possibly a clerical title</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain, stay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bilībaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˌbiˈliːβan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain, stay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bilībaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave behind, abandon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (bimaitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to circumcise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (maitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mock, deride</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐌼𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;mampjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mampjaną</i> (“to mock”), from the iterative <i>&#42;mump-</i> (“to stain”), or otherwise from an unattested strong verb <i>&#42;mimpaną</i>. See English <i>mump</i> (“to cheat, deceive”), and compare obsolete Dutch <i>mompen</i> (“to deceive”).</p><p>While Kroonen claims Ancient Greek <i>μέμφομαι</i> (mémphomai, “I blame, accuse”) and the Germanic forms are reconcilable (with Kluge's law), Beekes rejects this, stating that Germanic <i>-p-</i> cannot here correspond to Greek <i>-φ-</i> and that this etymological connection is not widely considered reliable.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be permitted, may</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;binuganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bindaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, benim</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;binemaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconnoiter, to scout out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;niuhsjan); the latter is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niuhsijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, bow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beuganą</i>.</p></html>:/biˈuːxti/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom (frequent repetition of the same act)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (biūhts, “accustomed”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/biˈuːxts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accustomed, wont</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (*ūhts), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhtaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ewk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;beudaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biraubōną</i>.</p></html>:/biˈriːkiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger, peril</li></ol></html>:/biˈriːkiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in jeopardy, in danger</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form or misspelling of <i>𐌱𐌹𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (bireikeis)</li></ol></html>:/biˈrinnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encircle, come round about, run round about</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;birinnaną</i> (“to run around”), equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan). Cognate with Old High German <i>birinnan</i>, Old Frisian <i>birinna</i>,</p><p>Old English <i>birinnan</i>.</p></html>:/biˈruːnɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scheming, beguiling</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌰</i> (rūna) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌱𐌴𐍂𐌿𐍃𐌾𐍉𐍃</i> (bērusjōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murmuring, grumbling</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐍂𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (birōdjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/biˈroːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murmur, complain</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (rōdjan). Glosses Ancient Greek <i>γογγύζω</i> (gongúzō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look for, seek</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisehwaną</i> (“to look, besee”). Equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defilement, pollution</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bisauljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/biˈsɔːlnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be polluted, defiled</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bisauljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan)</p></html>:/biˈsɔːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile, pollute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisaulijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;sauljan). Cognate with German Low German <i>besölen</i> (“to dirty, defile”), English <i>besoil</i> (“to make dirty”).</p></html>:/biˈsatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put up around, surround with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisatjaną</i>; cognate with English <i>beset</i>, Dutch <i>bezetten</i>, and German <i>besetzen</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neighbour, live nearby</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bisitjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (sitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbour</li></ol><p>Substantivized form of <i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (bisitands), present participle of <i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (bisitan).</p></html>:/biˈskaban/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to shave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biskabaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (skaban). Cognate with Old High German <i>biskaban</i>.</p></html>:/biˈskiːnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine round about</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biskīnaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (skeinan).</p></html>:/biˈsmiːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to anoint</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌼𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;smeitan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smītaną</i>.</p></html>:/biˈsniwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to precede (with <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (faur))</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (sniwan).</p></html>:/biˈsunjaneː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>round about</li></ol></html>:/biˈspiːwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit on</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (speiwan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bistandaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan). Cognate with Old English <i>bestandan</i> (English <i>bestand</i>), Old High German <i>bistantan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat against</li><li>to stumble against</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (stigqan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stumbling block</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (bistigqan, “to stumble against”) or from a prefixed variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stunkwiz</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/biˈswɛrban/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swairban), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swerbaną</i>.</p></html>:/biˈswaran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjure, charge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biswarjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (swaran).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to traverse, to move around through, to go about</li><li>to lead around, to take along through</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (tiuhan). Cognate with Old English <i>betēon</i>.</p></html>:/biˈwɛːbjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap around, clothe in</li><li>to surround, encompass</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;waibjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waibijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyp-</i>.</p></html>:/biˈwandjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avoid, shun</li><li>to refuse, reject</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biwandijaną</i> (“to turn around”); equivalent to <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan). Cognate with Old English <i>bewendan</i> (“to turn around”) (whence English <i>bewend</i>).</p></html>:/biˈwindan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap (in something else)</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) to swaddle, wrap in swaddling clothes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biwindaną</i>; cognate with Old English <i>bewindan</i> and Dutch <i>bewinden</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;windan).</p></html>:/biˈwisan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make merry</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to feast, make merry”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avarice, greed, usury or fraud</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>𐌱𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (bifaihōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to covet</li><li>to defraud, swindle</li><li>to take advantage of (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;faihōn), likely related to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu). Compare <i>𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (gafaihōn).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covetousness, greed, avarice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (bifaihōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covetousness, greed, avarice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (bifaihōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html>:/biˈʍɛrban/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throng around</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕairban, “to walk”).</p></html>:/biˈʍeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whereby, how?</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌴</i> (ƕē)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix, to blend</li><li>to mingle, to intermix</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blandaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈblɔːθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make void, render powerless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blauþijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbliːθiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pity, compassion, mercy</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (bleiþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be merciful, take pity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blīþijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (bleiþs, “kind, merciful”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be merciful, take pity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blīþijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (bleiþs, “kind, merciful”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciful, kindhearted</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blīþiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beat, scourge, cut, kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blewwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blindaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overflowing with blood, hemorrhaging</li></ol><p>Identical in form and inflection to a participle, but it is unlikely a matching verb <i>&#42;𐌱𐌻𐍉𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;blōþarinnan) actually existed. It is more likely that it was formed directly from its components <i>𐌱𐌻𐍉𐌸</i> (blōþ, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (rinnands, “running, flowing”).</p></html>:/ˈbloː.ma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) lily (flower in the genus <i>Lilium</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōmô</i> (“flower”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worship, to honour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōtaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worship</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐌻𐍉𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (blōtan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus)</p></html>:/ˈbnɔːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to rub</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;binū(w)aną</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>gnúa</i> and Old High German <i>niuwan</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈbuɣ.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, purchase</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bugjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) breadth, width</li></ol><p><i>𐌱𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (braiþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) broad, wide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;braidaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wrestling-match, fight</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blink, glance</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring</li><li>to lead, to conduct</li><li>to make, to render, to cause to become</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bringaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrenk-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbrikan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break</li><li>to struggle, contend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brekaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbrin.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to burn (to be consumed by fire)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brinnaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbrin.noː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fever</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brinnaną</i> (“to burn”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrewh₁-</i>.</p></html>:/bruːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bride</li><li>daughter-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūdiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌸𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (brūþfaþs)</li></ol></html>:/ˈbruːθ.ˌɸaθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bridegroom</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (brūþs) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;faþs).</p></html>:/ˈbruː.kjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to employ</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūkaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbruːks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful, profitable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūkiz</i> (“useful”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>source, wellspring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brunnô</i>. Cognates include English <i>bourn</i>, Dutch <i>bron</i>, Crimean Gothic <i>brunna</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breastplate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brunjǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breast, chest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brusts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrews-</i> (“to swell, blow, inflate”). Compare Old High German <i>brust</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōþēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰréh₂tēr</i>.</p></html>:/ˈbroːθrahans/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) brethren</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐍂𐍉𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (brōþar), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brōþēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰréh₂tēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brotherly love</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌱𐍂𐍉𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (brōþar) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌱𐍉</i> (&#42;lubō, “love”, from <i>&#42;lubō</i>), probably a calque of Ancient Greek <i>φιλαδελφία</i> (philadelphía).</p></html>:/ˈbyssɔn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine linen</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>βύσσον</i> (bússon), accusative of <i>βύσσος</i> (bússos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any writing or text, especially a letter, deed, or contract</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) book, writ</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōkō</i>, likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂ǵos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribe, writer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōkārijaz</i>; compare Old English <i>bōcere</i> (English <i>booker</i>). By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐍉𐌺𐌰</i> (bōka, “a writing”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benefit, use</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōtō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈboːtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to profit, be of advantage</li><li>to better, improve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bōtijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌱𐍉𐍄𐌰</i> (bōta) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Indicates completeness or wholeness.</li><li>In verbs, indicates perfectivity (a finished action).</li><li>Indicates togetherness or association: co-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóm</i>. Germanic cognates see there.</p></html>:/ɡaˈaŋɡwiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enforcement</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (aggwus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html>:/ɡaˈaŋɡwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distress</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (aggwus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan), or from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ga-angwijaną</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to appropriate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌹𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;aiginōn). The latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiginōną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈɛːnaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to take from</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains, “one”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌽</i> (-ān).</p></html>:/ɡaˈɛːstaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to have regard for, to reverence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (aistan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgrace, dishonour, put to shame</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍉𐌽</i> (aiwiskōn, “to behave in a shameful manner”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈɔːkan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to abound</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (aukan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈarbja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow heir</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌾𐌰</i> (arbja). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>συγκληρονόμος</i> (sunklēronómos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take pity, show compassion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍂𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (armān).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbɛːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to force, compel</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (baidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear (children), give birth; engender</li><li>to compare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaberaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbɛrɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preserve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (bairgan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation, appearance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gabairhtjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, disclose, bring to light</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bairhtjan, “to make clear, disclose, show”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbandwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make signs</li><li>to signify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bandwjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbɔːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lodge (stay in any place or shelter)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌰𐌽</i> (bauan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbɔr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribute</li><li>collection (gathering of money for charitable or other purposes)</li><li>reveling, carousing</li></ol><p>From the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaburjaną</i> (“to be due”); compare German <i>Gebühr</i> (“fee”).</p><p>From the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaburjōną</i> (“to befit, be favorable”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbɔrɡja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow citizen</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌾𐌰</i> (baurgja); calque of Ancient Greek <i>συμπολίτης</i> (sumpolítēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) genealogy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (gabaurþs) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd), calque of Ancient Greek <i>γενεαλογία</i> (genealogía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth, genesis</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaburþiz</i>. In the Gothic Bible translated by Ulfilas, the Greek word γένεσις (génesis), meaning birth or origin, is rendered as 𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍃 (gabaurþs). This Gothic term derives from &#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍃 (&#42;baurþs) (“bearing, carrying</p><p>pregnancy, birth”) with the prefix ga- intensifying the sense, and it accurately conveys the idea of generation or birth.</p></html>:/ɡaˈbɔrjaba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gladly, happily</li></ol><p>From the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaburjōną</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaburjōną</i> (“to befit, be favorable”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus). Related to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (gabaur, “revelry”) and <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌱𐌰</i> (gabaurjaba, “gladly”). Compare Crimean Gothic <i>borrotsch</i> (“desire”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to profit, benefit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;batnaną</i> (“to get better”) with <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, riches</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (giban) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (gabigs)</li></ol></html>:/ɡaˈbiːdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (beidan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbiːstjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leaven</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐍄</i> (beist) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html>:/ˈɡabiɡaba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>richly</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (gabigs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba)</p></html>:/ˈɡabiɡnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be rich</li><li>to be enriched</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (gabigs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan)</p></html>:/ˈɡabiɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to enrich, make rich</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (gabigs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈɡabixs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, wealthy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabīgaz</i>. Equivalent to the stem of <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (giban) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-igs) or <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs), variants of <i>-𐌲𐍃</i> (-gs).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbidjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabidjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, connection</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gabindan, “to bind”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, bind fast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gabindaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (bindan). Cognate with Old English <i>gebindan</i>, Old Saxon <i>gibindan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bond, tie</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gabindan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ɡaˈbiu̯ɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to bend</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (biugan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈblɔːθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to triumph over</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (blauþjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compassion, pity, mercy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gableiþjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/ɡaˈbliːθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have compassion on</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bleiþjan)</p></html>:/ɡaˈblindjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to blind</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌻𐌹𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (blinds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bond, tie</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gabindan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ɡaˈbranː.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn up (destroy by burning)</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;brannjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gabriel, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Γαβριήλ</i> (Gabriḗl), from Hebrew <i>גבריאל</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈbrikan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break</li><li>to throw down</li><li>to bruise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (brikan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fragment, piece</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (gabrikan, “to break”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html>:/ɡaˈboːtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to restore, improve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍉𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bōtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather together</li><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to come to pass, result</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to join, to compact</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gahaftjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉𐌽</i> (galeikōn)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to defile (make impure or dirty)</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (gamains, “vulgar, unclean”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join together, to connect</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (gatilōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reconciled</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌹</i> (gawairþi) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌹</i> (gawairþi) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html>:/ˈɡaŋɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganganą</i> (present tense) and <i>&#42;gāną</i> (past tense).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel, journey</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gangijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan, “to go”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, iterative suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌴𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌿</i> (gageigaidēdjau)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gain</li></ol><p>Related to English <i>gain</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌴𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌿</i> (gageigaidēdjau)</li></ol></html>:/ɡaˈɡudaba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) godly, in a godly way</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌿𐌳𐍃</i> (gaguds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba)</p></html>:/ɡaˈɡudiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godliness</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌿𐌳𐍃</i> (gaguds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html>:/ɡaˈɡuθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) godly, honorable</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ). The normalized spelling would be <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (gaguþs), but the only attested spelling is <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌿𐌳𐍃</i> (gaguds).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decree</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen</li><li>to be suitable, be appropriate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;daban), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dabaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partner</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (dails) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html>:/ɣaˈdɛːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distribute</li><li>to divide, partition</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dailjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdɔːbjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden, make callous</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (daufs, “hardened”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;dauþnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to kill, put to death</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dauþjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdɔːkans/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) household</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare</li><li>to be brave, to be courageous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gadurzaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deed; a (completed) act of making, rendering</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;dēþs). Compare Middle High German <i>getāt</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈdiɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to form, shape, mold</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (digan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdiɡis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the thing formed</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (digan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative</li><li>kinsman</li><li>cousin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gadilingaz</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈdiu̯pjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to go deep</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍀𐍃</i> (diups) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hew out, to chisel</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;draban, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drabaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amass, accumulate, pile up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gadraganą</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (dragan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdraŋkjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give to drink</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dragkjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier, warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;druhtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;drausjan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drauzijaną</i>).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdrin.kan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to drink</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (drigkan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdriu̯.san/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (driusan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈdroːbnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be troubled</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐍉𐌱𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (drōbnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌳𐍉𐍆</i> (gadōf)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deem, consider, reckon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dōmjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proper, becoming, seemly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gadōbiz</i>. Closely related to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (gadaban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concur, to agree with one another</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come together, congregate, meet</li><li>to reach, to attain</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to life</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;qiunan). The latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwiknaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈkʷiu̯jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken, give life</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;qiujan), or directly from <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (qius) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concurrence</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concurring, agreeing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”).</p><p>Derived from or closely related to the above. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gathering, congregation, assembly</li><li>synagogue</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌿𐌼𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;qumþs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kumþiz</i>, or directly from <i>𐌲𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (gaqiman) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury (<i>dative singular</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>γαζοφυλακίῳ</i> (gazophulakíōi), dative singular of <i>γαζοφυλάκιον</i> (gazophulákion).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sharp, pointed object; possibly a sting, goad or spike</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gazdaz</i>. Translates Ancient Greek <i>κέντρον</i> (kéntron).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold (onto), to retain</li><li>to withhold</li><li>to maintain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahabjaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (habān). Akin to Old English <i>gehabban</i>, Old Saxon <i>gihebbian</i>, Old High German <i>gihabēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>coherently</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become healthy again, to be healed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hailnan), which is from <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (hails) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heal another</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahailijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hailjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈhɛːls/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (hails). Compare Dutch <i>geheel</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise, pledge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahaitą</i>. Cognates include Old High German <i>giheiz</i>, Old English <i>ġehāt</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convoke, call together</li><li>to profess, claim to be</li><li>to promise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to put on, clothe</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌼𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;hamōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamōną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈhɔːnjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humble, abase</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (haunjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gahausjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahauzijaną</i> (compare Old English <i>ġehīeran</i>, German <i>gehören</i>, Old Saxon <i>gihōrian</i>). By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈhardjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to harden</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hardijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (hardus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈhaftnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to cleave to, stick to</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (hafts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to join</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (haftjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+genitive) to help</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌹𐌻𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (hilpan, “to help”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahlaibô</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍆𐍃</i> (hlaifs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html>:/ɡahˈmeːlida/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌹𐌳𐌰</i> (gamēlida) infixed with <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh) after the prefix <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-)</li></ol></html>:/ɡaˈhnɛːwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lower, abase, humiliate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hnaiwjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈhnipnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become sad</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind, reason, disposition</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hugjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/ɡaˈhuljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, wrap, veil</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (huljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleansing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gahrainjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse, purify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hrainjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈhoːbɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temperance</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōbiz</i> (“fit, proper”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains)</p></html>:/ɡaˈhoːrinoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to commit adultery</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍉𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (hōrinōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, deliberation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þagkjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be completely silent</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þahan, “to be silent”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, dry up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;þairsan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þersaną</i>).</p></html>:/ɡaˈθɔrfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>temperate</li></ol></html>:/ɡaˈθɔrsnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry up, be dried up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wither, be withered</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurznaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈθarbaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abstain from (<i>+ genitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to be temperate</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þarbāną</i></p></html>:/ɡaˈθiːhan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prosper, flourish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaþinhaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þeihan). Cognates include Dutch <i>gedijen</i>, German <i>gedeihen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þiuþjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subjugate, enthrall</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;þiwan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewāną</i>. Compare <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (þius).</p></html>:/ɡaˈθlahsnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be troubled</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) + a verb in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) from the same root as <i>𐌸𐌻𐌰𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þlahsjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comfort, console</li><li>to encourage, exhort</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;þlaihan), of uncertain origin. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfort, consolation</li><li>encouragement</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌸𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (gaþlaihan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þliuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, endure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (þulān). Cognate with Old English <i>geþolian</i>, Old Saxon <i>githolōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) threshing floor</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐌹𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (þriskan, “to thresh”). Compare the formation <i>𐌲𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (gabaur).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consolation, solace</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌸𐍂𐌰𐍆𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaþrafstjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to console, comfort</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐌰𐍆𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þrafstjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress, trouble</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þreihan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈθwastjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, make firm</li><li>to restore</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be strong</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐍅𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;þwasts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) hell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>γέεννα</i> (géenna), from Biblical Hebrew <i>גֵּיהִנּוֹם</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to level, to make even</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌹𐌱𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ibnjan), from <i>𐌹𐌱𐌽𐍃</i> (ibns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to regret, repent</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐌳𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍉𐌽</i> (idreigōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack, want</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaidwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make glad</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gailijaną</i>. Alternatively, from an unattested adjective <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;gails, from <i>&#42;gailaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girdle</li><li>purse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gerdō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire, longing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;girnį̄</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;gairns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈɡɛrnjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desire, covet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;girnijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;gairns, “eager, desirous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(excessive) eagerness, (greedy) desire</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;gairns, “eager”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i), but the <i>-u-</i> is unexpected. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈɡɛː.tiːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) kid (young goat)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍃</i> (gaits, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (-ein, diminutive suffix).</p></html>:/ˈɡɛːts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) goat (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaits</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>gāt</i> and Old Norse <i>geit</i>, and Latin <i>haedus</i> (“kid”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kannjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈkɔːsjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to prove</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kausjan)</p></html>:/ˌɣaˈka.roːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to care, to be concerned</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌰𐍂𐍉𐌽</i> (karōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove, test</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gakeusaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (kiusan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persuasion, compliance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (gakunnan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs) (compare <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (gamunds), formed similarly). Said to be separate from <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (gakunþs), which is of unclear meaning.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Semantically unclear; used in the phrase</i> uf gakunþai<i>, translating</i> ἀρχόμενος<i>.</i></li></ol><p>Uncertain, perhaps related to <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (kuni) or some other derivate from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i>. Said to be different from <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (gakunds, “persuasion, compliance”), although some scholars regard this to be the same word despite the apparent semantic difficulty.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to subject oneself or defer to another's authority</li><li>to get to know, note, observe</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (kunnan). Note the different conjugations.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, test</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (gakiusan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐍂𐍉𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌰</i> (gakrōtōda)</li></ol></html>:/ɡaˈkroːtoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, crush</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay, put or cast down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galagjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call together, convoke</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (laþōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to learn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laisjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (galaistjan, “to follow”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow, pursue</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laistjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belief, faith</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faithful, religious</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (galaubjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to believe</li><li>to entrust</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galaubijaną</i> (“to believe, to hold valuable or pleasing”). Cognate with Old English <i>geliefan</i>, Dutch <i>geloven</i>, German <i>glauben</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide</li><li>to be hidden</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laugnjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make loose or free, set free</li><li>to liberate, rescue</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lausjan).</p></html>:/ɡalɔːfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valuable, costly, expensive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galaubaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (&#42;laufs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galatia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Γᾰλᾰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (Gălătĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to hinder, restrain, stop</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (latjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Galatian</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Γαλάτης</i> (Galátēs). For an explanation of the morphology, compare the borrowing of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌺𐌹𐌳𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (makidōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stake</li><li>gallows</li><li>cross</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Cross</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galgô</i>. Cognates include English <i>gallows</i>, Dutch <i>galg</i>, German <i>Galgen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, go away, set out (for)</li><li>to go forth, come</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈliːkaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkāną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (leikān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkiją</i> (whence Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;galīkī</i>). Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (galeiks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (galēkinōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alike, similar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare</li><li>to imitate, resemble</li><li>to equate, make equal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galīkōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal, cure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (lēkinōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand over, surrender</li><li>to betray</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lēwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galigriją</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌲𐍂𐍃</i> (ligrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lie, falsehood</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (&#42;liug), the latter part being related to or derived from <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (liugan, “to lie”). Related to Dutch <i>leugen</i>, English <i>lie</i>, German <i>Lüge</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false apostle</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (galiug) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (apaustaulus), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ψευδαπόστολος</i> (pseudapóstolos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a false brother</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (galiug) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍂𐍉𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (brōþar), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ψευδάδελφος</i> (pseudádelphos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false god, idol</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (galiug, “falsehood”) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ, “god”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to marry</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (liugān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) false prophet, pseudoprophet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (galiug, “falsehood”) +&lrm; <i>𐍀𐍂𐌰𐌿𐍆𐌴𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (praufētus, “prophet”), calque of Koine Greek <i>ψευδοπροφήτης</i> (pseudoprophḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false witness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (galiug) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (weitwōþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) false Christ, pseudomessiah</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲</i> (galiug, “falsehood”) +&lrm; <i>𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (xristus, “Christ”), calque of Koine Greek <i>ψευδόχριστος</i> (pseudókhristos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring light, illume</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (liuhtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather together</li><li>to meet together</li><li>to assemble</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (lisan, “to gather”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock, shut</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;lukan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lūkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become closed</li></ol><p>Fientive verb in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) corresponding to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (galūkan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈmaɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to avail</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (magan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crippled, wounded, maimed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;maiþs), the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maidaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellowship, communality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (gamains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌳𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (-dūþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellowship, community</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (gamains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congregation, community</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamainjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partaker</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamainjan, “to partake”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common, shared</li><li>vulgar, unclean</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamainiz</i>. Related to English <i>mean</i>, Dutch <i>gemeen</i>, German <i>gemein</i>, Latin <i>commūnis</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to partake, to share in something</li><li>to make common, to share</li><li>to defile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamainijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common, shared</li><li>vulgar, unclean</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamainiz</i>. Related to English <i>mean</i>, Dutch <i>gemeen</i>, German <i>gemein</i>, Latin <i>commūnis</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈmɛːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to mutilate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (maitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) melt, dissolution</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌻𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gamaltjan, “to melt”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maltijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈmalw.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to crush (figuratively, to overwhelm by pressure or weight)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malwijaną</i> (“to crush, grind, break”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, fellow, partner</li><li>companionship, partnership</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamaną</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, keep in mind</li><li>to recall, recollect, call to mind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (munan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (manwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) remembrance, recollection</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamaudjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maudjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈmɔrɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten, cut short</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murgijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stumbling-block, impediment</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamarzjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offend</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (marzjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈmarkoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to border (share a border with)</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌺𐌰</i> (marka) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn)</p></html>:/ɡaˈmatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (matjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing, scripture</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamēljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write down</li><li>to register, inscribe</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to send together with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sandjan); possibly a calque of Ancient Greek <i>συμπέμπειν</i> (sumpémpein).</p></html>:/ɡaˈmikiljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to magnify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mikiljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory, remembrance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaminþiją</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈmitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to distribute</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (mitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;gamitōn, “to reason, deliberate”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns), from <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (mitōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, keep in mind</li><li>to recall, recollect, call to mind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (munan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remembrance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamundiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (gamunan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have space, have room</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐍉𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;mōtan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōtaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, encounter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gamōtijaną</i> (compare Old English <i>ġemētan</i>).</p></html>:/ɡaˈnaɡl.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to nail (employ a nail as a fastener)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naglijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈnɛːtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to handle shamefully, mistreat, abuse</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naitijaną</i>, whence <i>𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (naiteins, “blasphemy”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;transitive) to name, designate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌰𐌼𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (namnjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈnanθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, stop, cease</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;nanþjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nanþijaną</i> (“to dare”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈnɔha/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sufficiency</li><li>contentment</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (ganauhan, “to suffice”) and <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (ganōhs, “sufficient”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈnɔhan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganuganą</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sufficiency</li><li>contentment</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (ganauhan, “to suffice”) and <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (ganōhs, “sufficient”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, rescue</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (nasjan). compare гонозити.</p></html>:/ɡaˈnatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make wet; to wash</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (natjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈnawistroːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury (<i>a dead body</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌽𐌰𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂</i> (&#42;nawistr) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn). <i>&#42;𐌽𐌰𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂</i> (&#42;nawistr) probably meant "grave" or "burial" or referred to some other object or tool related to burial, and derives from <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (naus, “corpse”) with the suffix <i>&#42;-istrą</i>, a suffix also found in <i>𐌰𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂</i> (awistr) and <i>𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂</i> (hulistr).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinsman</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take (along)</li><li>to acquire or receive</li><li>to learn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganemaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get a hold of, obtain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganeutaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (niutan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be saved, to be healed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganesaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvation, health</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganistiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (ganisan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be the cause of, to generate</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become abundant or sufficient</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (ganōhs, “abundant, sufficient”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy</li><li>to make abundant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganōgijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (ganōhs, “abundant, sufficient”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, suffix forming factitive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abundant, plentiful, sufficient</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganōgaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome, conquer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌹𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (jiukān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pair, couple</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌿𐌺</i> (juk).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) companion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌿𐌺</i> (juk) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) companion</li><li>simile, parable, proverb</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌿𐌺</i> (juk) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;+dative) to notice, heed</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to appear, seem</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaumijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈunleːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to become poor</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐌽𐌻𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (unlēþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamentation, mourning</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (gaunōn, “to lament, mourn”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, mourn</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of a region</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹</i> (gawi, “region, district”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land, region, environment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawją</i>. Cognates include German <i>Gau</i>, and the element <i>yeo-</i> in English <i>yeoman</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (gaurs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief, sorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (gaurs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html>:/ˈɡɔːrjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to aggrieve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (gaurs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, sorrowful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gauraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰewro-</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>घोर</i> (ghōrá, “terrible, terrifying”), Serbo-Croatian <i>žuriti</i>, Old High German <i>gōrag</i>, Old Irish <i>gúrе</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to clothe</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍀𐌰𐌹𐌳𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;paidōn), from <i>𐍀𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰</i> (paida) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html>:/ɡaˈraɡinoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to give counsel</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (raginōn)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) neighbor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garaznô</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌶𐌽</i> (razn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) neighbor</li></ol><p>Feminine equivalent of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌶𐌽𐌰</i> (garazna). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ɡaˈrahnjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to value</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌷𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (rahnjan)</p></html>:/ɡaˈraθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to count, number</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raþjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rule, ordinance, decree</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (garaidjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/ɡaˈrɛːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, give orders to, appoint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garaidijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (raidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>righteously, justly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) righteousness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correction, rectification</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (garaihtjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) righteousness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>righteousness, justness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts, “righteous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rectify, correct, make right</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garihtijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan) or <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;raihtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>righteous, just</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garehtaz</i> (compare Old English <i>geriht</i>, German, Luxembourgish and Dutch <i>gerecht</i>); equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (raihts).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (garaihts, “righteous, just”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza, comparative suffix).</p></html>:/ɡaˈrɛːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appointed, ordained</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garaidaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɡar.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) pen (enclosure for animals)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gardô</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yard, court</li><li>house, household</li><li>family, lineage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gardaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰórdʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master of the house</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards, “house”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;waldands, “ruling, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>See 𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌴 𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌴𐌹 (þanē gardei)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳</i> (gard, accusative singular of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards)) + <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei, relative particle)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yard, court</li><li>house, household</li><li>family, lineage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gardaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰórdʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>commendably, honestly, properly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdaz</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html>:/ɡaˈreːdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide for, to intend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garēdaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rēdan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fixed time</li><li>plan</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garēhsniz</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hrek-</i> (compare <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽</i> (ragin)), though like <i>𐍂𐍉𐌷𐍃𐌽𐍃</i> (rōhsns), the attested form would have to be analogical.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convene, gather together</li><li>to obtain, reach</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (gariuþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modesty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (gariuþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dignity, respectability</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (gariuþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (gariuþs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>respectable, honorable, dignified</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;reudaz</i> (“red”). The semantic shift from the Proto-Germanic 'red' is explained as a shift first perhaps to 'beautiful' (compare Russian <i>красный</i> (krasnyj)) or to 'easily blushing'.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(secret) counsel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;garūniją</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌰</i> (rūna) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (garuns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐍉</i> (-jō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>market square or street</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;runs, “course”). The latter is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runsiz</i> (“stream, course”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach</li><li>reproof</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasahtiz</i>, <i>&#42;gasahtuz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;sahts, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sahtiz</i>) and/or <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (gasakan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, perceive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasehwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebuke, scold, reprove</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasakaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌱𐍉𐌽</i> (salbōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>make an oblation of</li><li>to offer up, sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (saljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send along, send to accompany</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sandjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasatjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to put down, lay down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;sibjōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sibjōną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈsin.kʷan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be swallowed (to be taken in, be consumed)</li><li>to set (of a heavenly body&#58; to disappear below the horizon)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (sigqan, “to sink, to set”). For the sense of "to be swallowed", compare Danish <i>synke</i> (“to sink, to swallow”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈsiɡljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sigljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasinþô</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (sinþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (sinþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down, take a seat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasitjaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (sitan). Cognate with Old English <i>gesittan</i>, Old Saxon <i>gisittian</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌳𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gaskadwjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). The former element is an unattested verb, presumably meaning to <i>cover up</i> (originally <i>cover with shade</i>), related to <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (skadus, “shadow”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm, injure, damage</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skaþjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to separate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (skaidan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gaskaidan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;intransitive) to become separated</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (skaidan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow servant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐍃</i> (skalks, “servant”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/ɡaˈskamaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to be ashamed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (skamān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaskapjaną</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>gescieppan</i>, Old Saxon <i>giskeppian</i>, Old High German <i>giscaffan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation</li><li>creature</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaskaftiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaskapjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interpret, explain</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skeirjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīrijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈskoːhi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) shoes, sandals</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (skōhs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i)</p></html>:/ɡaˈskoːhs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shod</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐍉𐌷𐍃</i> (skōhs)</p></html>:/ɡaˈslawan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to keep quiet, be still</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (slawan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm, injure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;sleiþjan), from <i>𐍃𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (sleiþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall asleep</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌻𐌴𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (slēpan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈsmiːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌼𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;smeitan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smītaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smiþōną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈsniu̯mjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to hurry, rush</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sniumjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈsniwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attain to</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (sniwan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective</i>) to found</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;suljan). The origin of the latter verb is uncertain; it may be related to <i>𐍃𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (sulja, “sole”), or to <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐍃</i> (sauls, “pillar”). In the latter case, the -u- might in fact be long.</p></html>:/ɡaˈsunjoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to justify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (sunjōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season, spice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌿𐍀𐍉𐌽</i> (supōn).</p></html>:/ɡaˈspiːwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to spit</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (speiwan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to announce, tell, proclaim</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (spillōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to cause to stumble or hit against</li></ol><p>A causative to <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (gastigqan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquire</li><li>to possess</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;staldan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staldaną</i>. Related to Old English <i>stealdan</i> (“to possess”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, halt, stand still</li><li>to abide, uphold</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan). Cognate with Old English <i>gestandan</i>, Old Saxon <i>gistandan</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈstɔrknan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become rigid</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturknaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascend, climb</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (steigan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (stigqan).</p></html>:/ˈɡas.tiˌɡoː.diː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) hospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gastigōþs, “hospitable, welcoming”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gastigōþs)</li></ol></html>:/ˈɡas.tiˌɡoːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hospitable, welcoming</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (gasts, “guest”) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gōþs, “good”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈstrɔːjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to furnish</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (straujan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gastiz</i> (“guest, stranger”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈstoːθaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make upright, cause to stand</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;gastōþs, “steady”) (of which the negative <i>𐌿𐌽𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (ungastōþs, “homeless”) is attested), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”), + <i>-𐌰𐌽</i> (-ān).</p></html>:/ɡaˈstoːjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge, to sentence</li><li>to determine</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (stōjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈstoːjans/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreasonable? wicked?</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gastōjan, “to judge, determine”), though the semantic development is unclear. It is used at 2&nbsp;Thessalonian 3:2 to translate Ancient Greek <i>ἄτοπος</i> (átopos), which most literally means “out of place” but in this passage is generally translated “unreasonable” or “wicked”.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honor, glorify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (swērān, “to honor”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known</li><li>to manifest</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swikunþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (swiltan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be strengthened, to become strong</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (swinþnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to strengthen</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swinþjan), from <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swinþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sigh, groan</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐍉𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swōgan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōganą</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satiate, sate, satisfy [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> dative <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍉𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;sōþjan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōdijaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gasōkijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sōkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to teach, instruct</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taiknjan).</p></html>:/ɣaˈtɛran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy</li><li>to violate, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;tairan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈtalzjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to teach</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (talzjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to tame</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tamjaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈtand.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sear (to mark permanently, as if by burning)</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (tandjan)</p></html>:/ˌɣaˈtɔːjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>do, make, effect</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taujan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tear, rent</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (gatairan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, a tearing down</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;taurþs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;turþiz</i>, or directly from the zero-grade of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (gatairan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vanish, come undone</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (gatairan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flag, mark, note</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐍂𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;tarhjan), derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tarhijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dorḱ-éie-</i>, <i>&#42;dorḱ-éye-ti</i> (causative), from <i>&#42;derḱ-</i> (“to see”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to take away, deprive, rob</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐍂𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;tarnjan), a causative of <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;tairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, display</li><li>to tell</li><li>to report</li><li>to preach</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;teihan, “to show”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>appropriately, congruently</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌴𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;gatēms, “appropriate, congruent, matching”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba), ultimately related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;temaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈteːwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌴𐍅𐌰</i> (tēwa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suitably, fittingly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (gatils, “fit, convenient”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit</li><li>convenient</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;tils), the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tilaz</i>.</p><p>The word <i>𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌻𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (untilamalsks) suggests the existence of an unattested antonym <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;untils, “unfit, inappropriate”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to achieve, to obtain</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;tilōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tilōną</i> (“to strive, reach”).</p></html>:/ɡaˈtiman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to befit, match; to agree or be congruent with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;temaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edification, construction</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gatimrjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build up, construct</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building, edifice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gatimrjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐍉</i> (-jō).</p></html>:/ɡaˈtiu̯.han/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conduct, lead forth, bring</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (tiuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reinforce, make firm</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌿𐌻𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (tulgjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust, be confident in</li><li>to be convinced or persuaded</li><li>to entrust</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌰𐌽</i> (trauān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to trample, tread underfoot</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐍂𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (trudan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gatwǭ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in motion, make move</li><li>to shake</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wagjan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈwadjoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise in marriage, espouse</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wadjōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceful, peaceable</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌹</i> (gawairþi) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-</i>. Compare the etymologies of <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸</i> (wairþ) and <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (wairþan).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawerpaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (wairpan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈwaknan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;intransitive) to be awake</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waknaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈwal.dan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to rule, to exercise authority over</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawaldaną</i>; cognates include Old English <i>gewealdan</i>, Old Saxon <i>giwaldan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (waldan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selection, choosing, election</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawaljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, select</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unclean</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;wamms, “unclean, blemished”), from <i>𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼</i> (wamm, “spot, blemish”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawandjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn</li><li>to return (something)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to return; come back</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;religion) to convert</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan). Compare Old English <i>ġewendan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communication, conversation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawurdiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(something done for) gain, profit</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌹</i> (&#42;waurki) or a direct formation from <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawaurkjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). Compare Old English <i>geweorc</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, make, achieve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waurkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow worker</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅𐌰</i> (waurstwa).</p></html>:/ɡaˈwɔrts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rooted</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (waurts)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condemnation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawargjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condemn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wargjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wargijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawasjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/ɡaˈwasjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clothe</li><li>to put on clothes</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wasjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hallow, consecrate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (weihān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit</li><li>to review</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to expect, ween</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wēnjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land, region, environment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawją</i>. Cognates include German <i>Gau</i>, and the element <i>yeo-</i> in English <i>yeoman</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to shake together</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wigan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, join</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawedaną</i>.</p></html>:/ɡaˈwizniːɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of delight</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌶𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;wizns) (of uncertain origin; possibly related to <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to be, remain”) or <i>𐍅𐌹𐌶𐍉𐌽</i> (wizōn, “to lead a life”)) + <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concordant, unanimous, of one will</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (wilja), with an adjectival suffix.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawinnaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (winnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to remain, abide</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “remain, abide”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint, ligament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawissiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (gawidan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to watch over, guard, defend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawitāną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to wound</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawundōną</i>, from <i>&#42;wundōną</i>. Cognates include Old Saxon <i>giwundon</i>, Old English <i>ġewundian</i>, Middle High German <i>gewunden</i>, Middle Dutch <i>gewonden</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gawrekaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (wrikan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to mature, bear fruit</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wreskwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catch, something that is caught</li></ol><p>Prefixed form of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fangą</i>, with leveling from the related verb (see <i>𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (fāhan)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (fāhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌷𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;fahrjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fagrijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) synonym of <i>𐌲𐌰𐌰𐌹𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (gaaiginōn)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;faihōn), likely related to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu). Compare <i>𐌱𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍉𐌽</i> (bifaihōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>council</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;faurds, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furdiz</i>).</p></html>:/ɡaˈfɔːrs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-behaved, sober</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) + the root of <i>𐌿𐌽𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (unfaurs, “gossipy, tattling”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, to hold firm</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fastān).</p></html>:/ɡaˈfeːhaba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>honestly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fēhiz</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html>:/ɡaˈfeːtiːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌴𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gafētjan, “to dress up, adorn”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins); the former element is an otherwise unattested perfective variant of <i>𐍆𐌴𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fētjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide</li><li>to bury</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (filhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concealed, covered</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (fulgins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to fully satisfy, persuade</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌰𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fullaweisjan). Used to translate Ancient Greek <i>πληροφορέω</i> (plērophoréō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become full</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (fullnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fulljan).</p></html>:/ɡaˈfraθjiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobriety (quality or state of being grave or earnestly thoughtful)</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fraþjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html>:/ɡaˈfrɛhnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask after, ask about</li><li>to find out (<i>by asking</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (fraihnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to rule, subordinate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-, perfective verbal prefix) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (fraujinōn, “rule”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gafreidjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), the former being a perfective variant of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (freidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile, pacify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;friþōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;friþōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconciliation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (gafriþōn, “to reconcile”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiss</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (frijōn, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be depicted, to take shape</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;frisahtnan), from <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (frisahts, “depiction, visual image”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html>:/ɡaˈfrisahtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engrave</li></ol><p><i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (frisahts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amenable, agreeable, persuadable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gahwerbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest, respite</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (gaƕeilān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to rest, cease</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕeilān, “to pause, rest, cease”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to whiten</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍈𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ƕeitjan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwītijaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complain, rebuke</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐍉𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕōtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ, “god”)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌲𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (guda)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌲𐌿𐌳𐌹𐍃</i> (gudis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gebō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinnacle or apex (of a building)</li></ol><p>Cognate with German <i>Giebel</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax, tribute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;geldą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sickle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gelþō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel-</i> (“to cut”). Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;galdijaną</i> (“to castrate”), whence English <i>geld</i> (“to castrate”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax, tribute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gelstrą</i> (“tax”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax registration, taxation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌹𐌻𐍃𐍄𐍂</i> (gilstr, “tax”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;mēleins, “writing, registration”), from <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;geutaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɡis.traˌda.ɣis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) tomorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂𐌰-</i> (gistra-, compare <i>yester-</i>) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;dagis, “by day”, adverbial form of <i>𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (dags)). Alternatively, the compound may be an adverbial form of an unattested noun <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (&#42;gistradags, “tomorrow”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is).</p><p>The first element, which usually points to a preceding day (and not a following day, as in Gothic), has attracted some scholarly attention. It has been suggested that the meaning in Gothic shifted from “yesterday” to “adjacent day”, and thence to its singly attested meaning of “tomorrow” in Gothic. Compare also the use of the etymologically related Old Norse <i>gær</i> (which normally means “yesterday”) to indicate “tomorrow” in <i>Hamðismál</i> 30:6.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diligently, meticulously, precisely</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐍃</i> (&#42;glaggws) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba), with root from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glawwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌰𐌱𐌰</i> (glaggwaba)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) exactly, precisely</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍅𐍃</i> (&#42;glaggws) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō), with root from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glawwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to glisten, gleam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glitmunjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudalausaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ, “god”) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus, “devoid of”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form of God, image of God</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌿𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;skaunei), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaunį̄</i> (“beauty”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>god-fearing, devout</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudafurhtaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ, “god”) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (faurhts, “fearful, afraid”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worshipper of God</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ, “god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌻𐍉𐍃𐍄𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;blōstreis, “worshipper”), ultimately a derivate of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blōstrą</i> (“sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple, sanctuary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudahūsą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Unclear, possibly from <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ, “God”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌱𐍉</i> (&#42;lubō, “love”) (from <i>&#42;lubō</i>), or with a second element <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (liufs, “dear”) (thus meaning <i>dear to God</i>). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌸</i> (guþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudjô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priesthood</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰</i> (gudja, “priest”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to serve as a priest</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰</i> (gudja) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>god, deity</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) God</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gudą</i> (“god”).</p></html>:/ˈɡulθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gulþą</i> (“gold”). Cognate with Old English <i>gold</i>, Old Frisian <i>gold</i>, Old Saxon <i>gold</i>, Old Dutch <i>golt</i>, Old High German <i>gold</i>, Old Norse <i>gull</i>. See also Finnish <i>kulta</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gulþīnaz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌲𐌿𐌻𐌸</i> (gulþ, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html>:/ˈɡu.ma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gumô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰǵʰm̥mō</i>. Germanic cognates include Old Saxon <i>gumo</i>, Old High German <i>gumo</i>, Old Norse <i>gumi</i> (Norwegian <i>gume</i>), Old English <i>guma</i>. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>homō</i>, Proto-Baltic <i>&#42;žmo-</i> (Old Lithuanian <i>žmuo</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (guma, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds, “born of, coming from”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male being</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (guma, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (-ein, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangrene, canker</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gundą</i>, variant of <i>&#42;gundaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the Goths</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐌿𐍄𐌰</i> (&#42;guta, “Goth”) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda, “people”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>Gotþjóð</i> (“the Gothic nation, the land of the Goths”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, trench</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grabō</i> (“grave, trench, ditch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrebʰ-</i> (“to dig, scratch, scrape”). Compare Old Saxon <i>graf</i> (Low German <i>Graf</i>, <i>Graff</i>), Dutch <i>graf</i>, Old English <i>græf</i> (English <i>grave</i>), Old Frisian <i>gref</i> (West Frisian <i>grêf</i>), Old Norse <i>grǫf</i> (Danish <i>grav</i>, Icelandic <i>gröf</i>), Old High German <i>grap</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɡraban/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grabaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrebʰ-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɡramjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gramjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splinter, speck (<i>dative singular</i>)</li></ol></html>:/ˈɡras/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blade (narrow leaf of a grass or cereal.)</li><li>herb (plant whose stem is not woody)</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) herbage (herbs collectively)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grasą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreh₁-</i> (“to grow, become green”). Cognate to Old English <i>græs</i> (“grass”), Old High German <i>gras</i> (“grass”), and related to Latin <i>grāmen</i> (“grass stalk”).</p></html>:/ˈɡreːdaɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grēdagaz</i> (“hungry, greedy”); equivalent to <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (grēdus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags). Cognates include English <i>greedy</i>, Dutch <i>graag</i> (“gladly”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grēduz</i>. Contains the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus). Cognate with English <i>greed</i>, Old Norse <i>gráðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunger, be hungry</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (grēdus) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html>:/ˈɡriːpan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grab, grip, take hold</li><li>to lay hold of, seize, take (prisoner)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīpaną</i> (“to grab, grasp”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (grētan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weep, cry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grētaną</i> (“to weep, cry”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weeping, crying</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grētaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) rank, grade, standing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gridiz</i>. The only attested sense is a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>βαθμός</i> (bathmós) which occurs in the Greek text corresponding to this Gothic attestation and which (though its primary meaning is "step") has a figurative meaning of "rank" or "degree".</p></html>:/ˈɡrindafraθjis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) faint-hearted, timid, demoralized</li></ol><p>From the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grindaną</i> (“to grind”) (apparently in the sense of “crushed”) + <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌹</i> (fraþi, “mind”). Likely a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὀλιγόψυχος</i> (oligópsukhos), which it translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation, low wall</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌲𐍂𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;grundus, “earth, ground”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;waddjus, “wall”). Neither of the elements are attested outside of compounds.</p></html>:/ˈɡroːba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxhole (burrow of a fox)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grōbō</i>. Cognates include English <i>groove</i>, Dutch <i>groef</i>, <i>groeve</i>, German <i>Grube</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸</i> (gōþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble birth</li><li>well-born</li><li>of good descent</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐍉𐌳𐍃</i> (gōds, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds, “born from”).</p></html>:/ˈɡoː.diː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) goodness, virtue</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōdį̄</i>; by surface analysis, <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gōþs)</li></ol></html>:/ɡoːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōdaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰedʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greeting, salute</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌲𐍉𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gōljan, “to greet”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to greet, to salute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōlijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;epithet&#32;of&#32;God,&#32;hapax legomenon) day-bringer, dawn-bringer</li></ol><p>Substantivized from the present participle of <i>&#42;𐌳𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;dagan, “to dawn”), which derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagāną</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌳𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;dagan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-ands).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰegʷʰ-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>dagr</i> (Danish <i>dag</i>), Old High German <i>tag</i>, Old English <i>dæġ</i> (English <i>day</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Sunday, the first day of the week</li></ol><p>Literally ‘day after the Sabbath’.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suckle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dajjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft, kneadable material, <i>especially:</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dough</li><li>clay</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daigaz</i> (“soft, kneadable material”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participation</li><li>community</li><li>partnership</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dailō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdɛːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to share, distribute, divide, portion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dailijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, portion</li><li>share</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dailiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdɛː.moːˌnaː.riːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) one possessed by the devil</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valley</li><li>ditch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dala-</i>, akin to English <i>dale</i>, Dutch <i>dal</i>, Swedish <i>dal</i>, and German <i>Tal</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdalaθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down, downwards</li><li>below</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌻</i> (dal, “valley”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸</i> (-þ, location adverb suffix), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dalą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdalaθa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>below, beneath</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌸</i> (dalaþ).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from below</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌸</i> (dalaþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Dalmatia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Dalmatia</i> or Ancient Greek <i>Δαλματία</i> (Dalmatía).</p></html>:/ˈdam.ja.nus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Δαμιανός</i> (Damianós); compare English <i>Damian</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daniel, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Δᾱνῑήλ</i> (Dānīḗl), from Biblical Hebrew <i>דָּנִיֵּאל</i> (Daniyél).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>callousness, hardness (of heart)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (daufs, “hardened”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>callousness, hardness (of heart)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (daufs, “hardened”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duhtēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰugh₂tḗr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast, banquet</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dying, death</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dauþjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, murder</li><li>to put to death, execute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daudijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (dauþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈdɔːθubliːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) marked for death</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (dauþus, “death”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. What suffix?</i></p></html>:/ˈdɔː.θus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dauþuz</i>. Contains the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daudaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell, odour, scent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dauniz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰowh₂-nis</i>, from the root <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i>. Cognates include Icelandic <i>daunn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing, immersion in water</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) baptism</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daupīniz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (daupjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/ˈdɔːpjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, immerse</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to baptize</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daupijaną</i>. The sense “to baptize” is a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>βαπτίζω</i> (baptízō).</p></html>:/ˈdɔːpjands/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baptist, baptizer</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durą</i>, related to <i>&#42;durz</i>. Germanic cognates include Old High German <i>tor</i> (German <i>Tor</i> (“gate”)), Old English <i>dor</i> and <i>duru</i> and Old Norse <i>dyrr</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwer-</i> (“door, gate”).</p><p>Indo-European cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्वार्</i> (dvār), Ancient Greek <i>θύρα</i> (thúra) and Latin <i>foris</i>, <i>forum</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) female porter or gatekeeper</li></ol><p>Feminine equivalent of <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (daurawards). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter, gatekeeper</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durawardaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) female porter or gatekeeper</li></ol><p>Feminine equivalent of <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (daurawards). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>double gate</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;durz</i>.</p></html>:/dɔːfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hardened, hard-hearted, callous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;daubaz</i> (“deaf”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewbʰ-</i> (“to whisk, be obscured”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Δαυείδ</i> (Daueíd), Hebrew <i>דּוד</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διάβολος</i> (diábolos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slanderer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διάβολος</i> (diábolos, “slanderer”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌳𐌹𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌿𐍃</i> (diabaulus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) deacon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>διάκονος</i> (diákonos).</p></html>:/ˈdiɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shape, mold, form from earth or clay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;diganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>fingō</i> and Sanskrit <i>देग्धि</i> (dégdhi).</p></html>:/ˈdiɡans/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthen, made of clay</li></ol></html>:/ˈdiɣriː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) abundance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;digrį̄</i>, from <i>&#42;digraz</i> (“big, thick”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i> (“to knead, shape”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;diupį̄</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍀𐍃</i> (diups) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;diupiþō</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍀𐍃</i> (diups, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;deupaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdiu̯s/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild animal, beast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;deuzą</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>dēor</i> (modern English <i>deer</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>apart, asunder, dis-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twiz-</i>, and doublet of <i>𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃-</i> (twis-). Since the form lacks the expected effects of Grimm's law, it has often been conjectured to have been borrowed from or influenced by Latin <i>dis-</i>; however it doesn't normally appear appended to roots borrowed from Latin, but instead shows correspondences with other Germanic terms prefixed with <i>&#42;twiz-</i>. The voiced onset can thus be explained as irregular lenition due to the unstressed syllable. The exact details, however, are unclear. Cognate to German <i>zer-</i>.</p></html>:/disˈdɛːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide, split up</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dailjan)</p></html>:/disˈdriu̯san/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall upon</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (driusan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of emotion</i>) to take complete possession of, to overcome, to seize</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (habān).</p></html>:/disˈhniu̯pan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break asunder</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hneupaną</i>.</p></html>:/disˈhnupnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐌷𐌽𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (dishniupan) + <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, causative suffix).</p></html>:/disˈhuljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to cover</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (huljan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌹</i> (dissigqai)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌺𐍂𐌹𐍄𐌽𐍉𐌳𐌰</i> (disskritnōda)</li></ol></html>:/disˈniman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to possess</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman)</p></html>:/disˈsin.kʷan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to set, to go down (of a celestial body interrupting the actions of the day)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌰𐌽</i> (sigqan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, take hold</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (sitan).</p></html>:/disˈskɛːdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to disperse</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (skaidan)</p></html>:/disˈskriːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, rip, rend</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skreitan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skrītaną</i>.</p></html>:/disˈskritnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be torn, rent, ripped</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌺𐍂𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;disskritans) (past participle of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌺𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (disskreitan)) + <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html>:/ˈdistahiːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispersion, diaspora</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (distahjan, “to scatter”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins)</p></html>:/disˈtahjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter</li><li>to waste</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (tahjan)</p></html>:/disˈtɛran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst</li><li>to leaven</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;tairan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌸</i> (distairiþ)</li></ol></html>:/disˈtɔrnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;distaurans) (past participle of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (distairan)) + <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html>:/disˈwilwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (wilwan).</p></html>:/disˈwinθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, grind to powder, pulverize</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;winþjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winþijaną</i> (“to winnow”), a variant of <i>&#42;windwōną</i>. The sense ‘crush, grind to powder’ found at Luke 20:18 is a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>λικμάω</i> (likmáō).</p></html>:/ˈdiswiss/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-) plus an uncertain element. Streitberg calls it a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀνάλυσις</i> (análusis), but it is unclear what word of the shape <i>wiss</i> could correspond to <i>λύσις</i> (lúsis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dewaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰew-</i>.</p></html>:/du/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to, towards</li></ol><p>Isolated in Gothic among the Germanic languages. Probably related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tō</i> somehow, but the exact etymology is unclear. If this is a non-Germanic loan in Gothic that was not transmitted through Proto-Germanic, its use would prove the existence of the verb <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ginnan) as simplex in Gothic (see <i>𐌳𐌿𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (duginnan)).</p><p>Lehmann argues that “Among attempted etymologies the most likely takes du as the proclitic of PGmc <i>tō</i> in verbs like <i>*du-ginnan</i>; cf <i>ga-</i> (J Schmidt 1883 ZVS 26:24; Delbrück 1907 IF 21:355f; Rolffs 1908:46ff). Less likely sources, G Schmidt 1962:240 assumes non-Gmc origin; Voyles 1967 JEGP 66:173-74 derives <i>du</i> and <i>und</i> from PIE <i>n̥-dʰ-</i> + unidentified vowel, possibly <i>u</i>.”</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix indicating movement towards, or arrival&#47;presence at, something or -place.</li></ol><p>The preposition <i>𐌳𐌿</i> (du) used as a prefix, of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, come up to</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍄𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (atgaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run up to (somewhere or something)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (atrinnan).</p></html>:/duatˈsniwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw to the shore, to come to anchor</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍄-</i> (at-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (sniwan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>attested spelling, perhaps a misspelling, of expected <i>𐌳𐌿𐌰𐍄𐍃𐌽𐌴𐍅𐌿𐌽</i> (duatsnēwun)</li></ol></html>:/ˈduɣan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be useful, to be of value</li><li>to fit, to be suitable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duganą</i>.</p></html>:/duɡaˈwindan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entangle</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;windan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windaną</i>.</p></html>:/duˈɡinnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin</li></ol><p>Either from <i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ginnan) (a Gothic formation) or inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;duginnaną</i>. There are no other Germanic cognates with the same prefix which could be used to support the Proto-Germanic verb of the second etymology, but the unprefixed verb of the first etymology (<i>&#42;ginnaną</i>) is not attested in any Germanic language (which all except Gothic have descendants of the prefixed form <i>&#42;biginnaną</i>, having the same meaning as this verb).</p><p>The element <i>𐌳𐌿</i> (du) is not attested in any other Germanic languages, probably being an archaic inheritance from Proto-Germanic that was lost early on in other Germanic languages. The archaic nature of the prefix might seem to point to a Proto-Germanic inheritance for this verb, but its clear productivity in Gothic renders this pointer uncertain.</p></html>:/duˈɡinniθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌳𐌿𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌹𐌸</i> (duginniþ)</li></ol></html>:/duhˈθeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌳𐌿𐌸𐌸𐌴</i> (duþþē, “wherefore, therefore”)</li></ol></html>:/duˈθeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌳𐌿𐌸𐌸𐌴</i> (duþþē, “wherefore, therefore”)</li></ol></html>:/duˈθiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌳𐌿𐌸𐌸𐌴</i> (duþþē, “wherefore, therefore”)</li></ol></html>:/duθˈθeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wherefore</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿 𐌸𐌴</i> (du þē, “to which [purpose], for which [reason]”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative reconstruction of <i>𐌳𐌿𐌻𐌲𐍃</i> (dulgs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) creditor</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿𐌻𐌲</i> (dulg) or <i>𐌳𐌿𐌻𐌲𐍃</i> (dulgs, “debt”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰</i> (&#42;haitja, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haitijô</i>), the latter an agent noun equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dulgaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdulθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep a feast, to celebrate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dulþijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌳𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (dulþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast, celebration</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dulþiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dumb, mute, speechless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dumbaz</i>. Cognate with English <i>dumb</i>, German <i>dumm</i>, Dutch <i>dom</i>.</p></html>:/duˈrinnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run to, come up to</li></ol><p><i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan)</p></html>:/duˈstoːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, start</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;stōdjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōdijaną</i>.</p></html>:/duˈwakan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;intransitive) to watch, keep alert</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿-</i> (du-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (wakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐌿</i> (du) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌴</i> (ƕē).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, pull, carry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;draganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌺</i> (dragk)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌹𐌳𐌰</i> (dragkida)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drankiz</i>. Cognates include Dutch <i>drank</i>, German <i>Trank</i>, Old English <i>drænc</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdrankjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drink, to give to drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drankijaną</i>. A causative of <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (drigkan), its cognates include the English <i>drench</i>, Dutch <i>drenken</i>, and German <i>tränken</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdrɛːvjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, bother, badger</li><li>to drive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;draibijaną</i>, from the root of <i>𐌳𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (dreiban, “to drive”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drachma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>drachma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>δραχμή</i> (drakhmḗ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumb, scrap of food</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;druhsnō</i>, zero-grade variant of <i>&#42;drōhsnō</i> (“dregs, sediment”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) warfare</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (drauhtinōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-assus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wage war</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;drauhts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (-inōn), the former deriving from <i>&#42;druhtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) warfare</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (drauhtinōn) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (witōþ).</p></html>:/ˈdriːβan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive, force to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drībaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreybʰ-</i> (“to drive, push”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (drigkan)</li></ol></html>:/ˈdrin.kan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drinkaną</i>. Compare Vandalic <i>drincan</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdriu̯ɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wage war</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreuganą</i> (“to serve, be a retainer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrewgʰ-</i> (“to serve one's tribe”).</p></html>:/ˈdriu̯.san/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreusaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈdriu̯.soː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cliff, drop, steep slope</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (driusan, “to fall”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inebriation, drunkenness</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) drinking-bout</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐍂𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (drugkans) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intoxicated, drunk, drunken</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drunkanaz</i>, the past participle of <i>&#42;drinkaną</i>. The Proto-Germanic participle likely already had the added meaning of "intoxicated".</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound</li></ol><p>From or related to the Proto-Germanic verb <i>&#42;drunjaną</i> (“to rumble”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fall</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;druziz</i> (“a fall, decline”), a zero-grade derivation from the same root as <i>&#42;dreusaną</i> (“to fall”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturbance, trouble, tumult</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/become restless, to be/become agitated</li><li>to be/become troubled</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drōbuz</i> (“disturbed, troubled”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html>:/ˈdroːbjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trouble, worry, upset</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cause trouble, incite a riot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drōbijaną</i> (“to disturb”), from <i>&#42;drōbuz</i> (“turbid, disturbed, troubled”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolish talk</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (dwals) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (dwals, “foolish”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be mad</li></ol><p>Either a Gothic innovation, from <i>&#42;𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;dwalms) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn) or from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwalmōną</i>. <i>&#42;𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;dwalms) is related to <i>𐌳𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (dwals, “foolish”)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwalaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwel-</i>, <i>&#42;dʰewel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge, to evaluate</li><li>to deem, to consider</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgement, insight</li><li>distinction</li><li>notability, prestige</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōmaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰóh₁mos</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>in that case, then, thereby</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) that, who, which; generic relative particle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ī</i>.</p></html>:/ˈiːθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>for which reason, wherefore</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan).</p></html>:/ˈiːkɔ.ni.oː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Konya (a city in Turkey), the ancient Iconium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰκόνιον</i> (Ikónion).</p></html>:/ˈiːsarn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron</li><li>fetters, chains</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īsarną</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;īsarnom</i> (“iron”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ēsh₂r̥no-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron shackle or chain</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (eisarn) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌹</i> (bandi).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of iron</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;īsarnīnaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (eisarn, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌵𐌰𐌸</i> (qaþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈkʷɛː.noːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, wail, lament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwainōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) stake, sharp stick, thorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kweru</i> (“sharp stick”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷéru</i> (“spit, spear”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meekness, gentleness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌵𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (qairrus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, patient, calm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwerruz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌵𐌴𐌸𐌿𐌽</i> (qēþun) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌵𐌴𐌽𐍃</i> (qēns)</li></ol></html>:/kʷeːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēniz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷḗn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, speak, converse, tell a story</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kweþaną</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷet-</i>. In the Gothic Bible it is used to translate Ancient Greek <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to say”). Compare Old Norse <i>kveða</i>, Old High German <i>quedan</i>, and Old English <i>cweþan</i> (modern English <i>quethe</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌹𐌸</i> (qiþiþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (qiþa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pregnant woman</li></ol><p>From an adjective <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌿𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;qiþuhafts, “pregnant”), from <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (qiþus, “womb”) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (hafts, “laden with”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly, stomach</li><li>womb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kweþuz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈkʷiman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwemaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷem-</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>cuman</i> and Latin <i>veniō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) female</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌵𐌹𐌽𐍉</i> (qinō, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds, “born of, coming from”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female being</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a silly or foolish woman</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌵𐌹𐌽𐍉</i> (qinō, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (-ein, diminutive suffix).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) female</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌵𐌹𐌽𐍉</i> (qinō, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) female</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌵𐌹𐌽𐍉</i> (qinō, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html>:/ˈkʷi.noː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwenǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷḗn</i>.</p></html>:/kʷi.us/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alive, lively</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwiwaz</i>, <i>&#42;kwikwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, ruination</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (qistjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwistijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming, arrival</li><li>presence</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) parousia</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kumiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisture</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌵𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;qramms) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html>:/za.ka.ri.as/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zacharias, a Hebrew priest, the father of John the Baptist.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ζαχαρίας</i> (Zakharías), from Biblical Hebrew <i>זְכַרְיָה</i> (Zəḵaryā).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌶𐌰𐍇𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (zaxariins)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌶𐌰𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌰𐍃</i> (zakarias)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) epithet of Simon the Zealot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ζηλωτής</i> (zēlōtḗs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌴𐌳𐌿𐌽</i> (habaidēdun) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈhabaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“to grasp”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a song of praise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hazjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/haz.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hazjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hang, suspend</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hang, be suspended; <i>figuratively</i>: to hang on every word</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanhaną</i>.</p><p>From a form closely related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hangāną</i>, or directly from the same with a later alteration influenced by the above strong verb.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Hebrew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἑβραῖος</i> (Hebraîos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manner, mode, fashion, way</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haiduz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-eyed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haihaz</i>.</p></html>:/hɛː.θi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncultivated) field (land area free of woodland, cities, and towns&#59; open country)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haiþī</i> (“heath”). Cognates include English <i>heath</i>, Dutch <i>heide</i>, German <i>Heide</i>, Old Norse <i>heiðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wild</li><li>pertaining to a field or heath</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌹</i> (haiþi) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks). The origin of the <i>-w-</i> is uncertain, perhaps by analogy with e.g. <i>𐌾𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (judaiwisks) and <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐌹</i> (aiwiski).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female heathen, heatheness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌹</i> (haiþi, “heath”).</p></html>:/ˈhɛː.laxs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) holy, sacred</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailagaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (hails) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags). Attested in only one instance, as a runic inscription on the Ring of Pietroassa.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heal another</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (hails) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/hɛls/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole</li><li>healthy, well</li><li>sound</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hail! greetings!</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hailaz</i> (“whole, healthy, sound”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haimaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóymos</i>. However, the Germanic word is reconstructed as a masculine a-stem, the inflection and gender do not match.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(owned) land</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌼𐍃</i> (haims) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍉𐌸𐌻𐌹</i> (&#42;ōþli), a form related to <i>&#42;ōþalą</i> with an added suffix <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). Compare Old High German <i>heimōdil</i>, Bavarian <i>Hoamatl</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attested&#32;only in plural</i>) heresies</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>αἱρέσεις</i> (hairéseis), plural of <i>αἵρεσις</i> (haíresis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌿</i> (hairu)</li></ol></html>:/ˈhɛr.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herd (group of animals)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herdō</i>. Cognate to English <i>herd</i>, German <i>Herde</i> and Old Norse <i>hjǫrð</i>, and Sanskrit <i>शर्ध</i> (śardha, “host, troop”).</p></html>:/ˈhɛr.diːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herdsman (a person who tends to livestock)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;Christianity) pastor (someone with spiritual authority over a group of people)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hirdijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowels</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herþrą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhɛ.rus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword (weapon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heruz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhɛr.toː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hertô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱḗr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hajaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kéHy-os</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keHy-</i>.</p></html>:/hɛː.tan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to address, call</li><li>to name</li><li>to promise</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be called</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haitaną</i>.</p></html>:/hɛː.ti/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, command</li></ol><p>From a form close to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haisiz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contend, contest, strive against</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍆𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (haifsts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conflict, rivalry, contestation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haifstiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak, coat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hakulaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhal.bɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspect (any specific feature, part, or element of something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbō</i> (“half, side; direction”). Cognate to Old English <i>healf</i> and Old High German <i>halba</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhalbs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the half (consisting of a half)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold</li><li>to take care of, tend, feed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haldaną</i> (“to watch, look after”).</p><p>Cognate to Old English <i>healdan</i> (“to hold”). More cognates: see there.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haldiz</i>, comparative of an adjective corresponding to Old High German <i>halto</i> (“much”). According to Duden, ultimately related to <i>&#42;halþaz</i> (“inclined, sloping”). Also compare Old Norse <i>heldr</i> (“rather”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hardly ever, scarcely</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;halis) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅</i> (aiw). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Whence *halis?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in vain, vain, futile</li></ol><p>Uncertain etymology; seemingly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halkaz</i>. Some have linked this to <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐌿𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (ushulōn), Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;halh</i> and Old English <i>healoc</i>, which would suggest a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i>.</p><p>There are no apparent non-Germanic cognates. Has been cited as a possible source of Old Church Slavonic <i>хлакъ</i> (xlakŭ), but according to Lehmann this proposed borrowing is 'dubious semantically'.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock, stone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halluz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the underworld</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) hell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haljō</i>. Cognates include English <i>hell</i>, Dutch <i>hel</i>, German <i>Hölle</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) neck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halsaz</i>. Cognates include Dutch <i>hals</i>, German <i>Hals</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) neck</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (hals) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰</i> (&#42;agga); the latter is from <i>&#42;angô</i>. Cf. Swabian <i>Halsanke</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame, limp</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haltaz</i>.</p></html>:/hamfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maimed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamfaz</i>. Compare Old Saxon <i>hāf</i>, Old High German <i>hamf</i>.</p></html>:/ˈha.na/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rooster, cock (male chicken)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanô</i>. Cognates include Dutch <i>haan</i>, German <i>Hahn</i>, Old English <i>hana</i> and Crimean Gothic <i>ano</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handugį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐌲𐍃</i> (handugs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handugaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (handus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌲𐍃</i> (-ugs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>handwrought, made by hand, handmade</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuwurhtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a crowd, gathering</li><li>(<i>military</i>) a troop, cohort, maniple</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hansō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌸</i> (haubiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haubudą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;káput-</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>hǫfuð</i> (Swedish <i>huvud</i>), Old High German <i>houbit</i> (German <i>Haupt</i>), Old English <i>hēafod</i> (English <i>head</i>).</p></html>:/ˈhɔːbiθwunds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wounded in the head</li></ol><p><i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌸</i> (haubiþ) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundaz</i> (from the root of <i>&#42;wundō</i> (“a wound”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>highly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs, “high”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, “adverb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height</li></ol><p><i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p><p>Non-lemma form.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to elevate, lift up</li><li>to praise, exalt, honour, glorify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;hauhaz</i>. See also <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs, “high”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honour, glory, exaltation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hauhjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride, haughtiness, hubris</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (hauhhairts, “proud, haughty”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-hearted, haughty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhahertaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs, “high”) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍉</i> (hairtō, “heart”). Compare also Dutch <i>hooghartig</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>haughty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs, “large, great”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (*þūhts, “seeming [to be]”), which is related to <i>𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þugkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhiþō</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs, “high”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>higher; comparative degree of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰</i> (hauhaba)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highest height</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (hauhists) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highest; superlative degree of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs)</li></ol></html>:/hɔːh.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to elevate, lift up</li><li>to praise, exalt, honour, glorify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;hauhaz</i>. See also <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍃</i> (hauhs, “high”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauhaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>high</i>, Dutch <i>hoog</i>, German <i>hoch</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowliness, humility</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (haunjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) humility</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauniþō</i>; compare French <i>honte</i> and Italian <i>onta</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (hauns, “humble”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html>:/hɔːn.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make humble, humble, abase</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haunijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low, humble</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a door, especially of wickerwork</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurdiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhɔ.ri/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coal (smouldering material)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) (a fire of) burning charcoal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurją</i> (“fire”) (Lehmann 1986), whence also Old Norse <i>hyrr</i> (“fire”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hurną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“horn”). Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚺᛟᚱᚾᚨ</i> (horna) (Old Norse <i>horn</i>), Old English <i>horn</i>, more distantly with Latin <i>cornū</i>, <i>cervus</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korva</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hornist, trumpeter</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (haurn, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound a trumpet or horn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (haurn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) something heard; a message or report</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauzīniz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/hɔːs.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear, listen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hauzijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear</li></ol><p>Apparently a variant of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. why this variant, and are there other class 2 cognates? both hausjan and hausjon translate akouein. any difference in meaning?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐌱𐌰</i> (harduba)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harder: comparative degree of <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (hardus)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hardizô</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (hardus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>harshly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (hardus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardheartedness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (hardus) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍉</i> (hairtō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>σκληροκαρδία</i> (sklērokardía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harduz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kert-</i>, <i>&#42;kret-</i> (“strong; powerful”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kóryos</i>. The <i>-jis</i> in the nominative singular results from morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the expected outcome of <i>*-jaz</i> in Gothic following a consonant would otherwise have been <i>*-is</i>. Other examples of this development include <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis), <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis) and <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis).</p></html>:/ˈhataːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hatāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌰𐍄𐌹𐍃</i> (hatis) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hate, wrath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hataz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hatjaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈha.wi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass, hay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hawją</i> (“hay”), derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hawwaną</i> (“to hew”). Cognates include Old English <i>hīeg</i>, Old Saxon <i>houwi</i>, Old High German <i>hewe</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift, raise, hold up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;habjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂pyé-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“to grasp”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make bound, to attach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haftijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bound, laden with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haftaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inner) chamber</li></ol><p>Pokorny derives it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kot-</i> (“dwelling space”), comparing words such as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kutą</i> (“shed, hut, stall”), Old Norse <i>kytja</i> (“small cottage”), Ancient Greek <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “cup, pint”); however, the relation and origin of all of these words is unclear. Also compare the cognates listed at Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kotьcь</i> (“cottage, paddock”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fever</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hitǭ</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;hitjō</i> ~ <i>&#42;hitjǭ</i> (“heating”); compare <i>&#42;haitį̄</i> (“heat”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>hita</i>, Old High German <i>hizza</i>, Old English <i>hitt</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodman, master of the house</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌷𐌴𐌹𐍅</i> (&#42;heiw) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰</i> (frauja). The former comes from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hīwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>my God! (said by Jesus on the cross)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>ἠλί</i> (ēlí), itself a transliteration of Aramaic <i>אלהי</i>, from <i>אלהא</i> with first person singular pronoun as possessor.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here, in this place</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hē₂r</i>. Germanic cognates: see there.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herod, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἡρῴδης</i> (Hērōídēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hither, here (to this place)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hidrê</i>. Cognate to Old Norse <i>heðra</i> and Old English <i>hider</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌷𐌹𐌳𐍂𐌴</i> (hidrē)</li></ol></html>:/hilms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helmaz</i> (“helmet”). Cognates include English <i>helm</i>, Dutch <i>helm</i>, German <i>Helm</i>, Old Norse <i>hjalmr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+genitive</i>) to help</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;helpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born of heaven, heavenly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌹𐌼𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (himins, “heaven”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds, “born of, coming from”). Cognate with Old English <i>heofoncund</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heaven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heminaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhindana/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) beyond</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hindanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative or dative</i>) behind, beyond</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinder</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Old High German <i>hintar</i> (whence German <i>hinter</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass by, go (away)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (hindar) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html>:/ˈhindarwiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) deceitful, insincere</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (hindar, “behind, beyond”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;weis, “wise”).</p></html>:/ˈhindarwiːsiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) deceit, guile</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (hindarweis, “deceitful, insincere”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei, “-ness”).</p></html>:/ˈhindumists/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outer, outermost</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hindumistaz</i>; cognate with English <i>hindmost</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude, mass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heuhmô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌷𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌼𐌰</i> (hiuhma)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wail</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;heufaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>to one person</i>) come (here)! hither!</li></ol><p>Extremely uncertain; many different etymologies have been proposed (for which see Lehmann 1986, who calls the etymology "massively disputed"), but most agree on at least some link to either <i>𐌷𐌴𐍂</i> (hēr) or the stem of <i>&#42;𐌷𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;his). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. don't replace this with a single ety unless there has been recent research since Lehmann which goes into this; as Lehmann's lemma demonstrates, there are multiple plausible explanations.</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) appearance, form</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hiwją</i>. Cognate with English <i>hue</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlahjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍆𐍃</i> (hlaifs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill (<i>genitive plural</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to slope, incline”). Compare English <i>lean</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈhlɛːw/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb, grave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaiwą</i> (“tomb, grave”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱloy-wo-m</i>, ultimately from the root <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to incline”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grave</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍅</i> (hlaiw) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍃𐌽𐌰</i> (-asna).</p></html>:/ˈhlɛːfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaibaz</i>; compare English <i>loaf</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare, trap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlammiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lot</li><li>inheritance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlautaz</i>.</p></html>:/hlas/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerful</li></ol><p>Possibly altered from <i>&#42;𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (&#42;hlahs), from <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hlahjan); otherwise unknown. There are no known Germanic cognates.</p></html>:/ˈhlasiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheerfulness</li></ol><p><i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐍃</i> (hlas) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) the feast of Tabernacles; Sukkot.</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍂𐌰</i> (hleiþra, “hut, tent”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;stakjan, “to stick, fasten”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins); a calque of Ancient Greek <i>σκηνοπηγία</i> (skēnopēgía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spare</li><li>to take good care of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlībijaną</i>, whence also Old Norse <i>hlífa</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhliː.du.ma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left (west side of the body when one is facing north)</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) left (left hand&#59; the left side)</li></ol><p>Contains the comparative suffix <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma). The root is ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to lean, incline”), with a sense of "crooked, slack". Compare Old Irish <i>clé</i> (“left”) and Latin <i>clīvius</i> (“inauspicious”) from the same root. The origin of the <i>𐌳</i> (d) is unclear, but Lehmann (1986) suggests derivation from a formation similar to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlīdō</i> (“slope”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌷𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌿𐌼𐍉𐌽𐌰</i> (hleidumōna)</li></ol></html>:/ˈhliːθra/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hut, tent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlīþrō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱléy-treh₂</i> (“shelter, covering”), from <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to shelter, cover”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-treh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booth, tent</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hleumô</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhli.ɸan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to steal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlefaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klep-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhliɸ.tus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) thief</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hleftuz</i>, or directly from <i>𐌷𐌻𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌽</i> (hlifan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌻𐌿𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (hlūtrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity <i>(of heart)</i>; sincerity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌻𐌿𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (hlūtrs, “pure”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clean, pure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlūtraz</i> (“clear, clean, pure”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlewH-</i> (“clean”). Cognate with Dutch <i>louter</i>, German <i>lauter</i>, Old High German <i>hlūttar</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowliness, abasement, humility</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hnaiwjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lower</li><li>to abase</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaiwijaną</i>, variant form of <i>&#42;hnaigijaną</i>, or from <i>𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐍃</i> (hnaiws) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low, lowly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaiwaz</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈhnaskʷus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnaskuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;knes-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhniːwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incline, bend downwards, decline</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnīwaną</i> (“to bow”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stake, sharp stick, thorn</li></ol><p>From the same Indo-European root as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hneupaną</i> (“to tear”) (whence <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃𐌷𐌽𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (dishniupan)). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈhuŋɡrijan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to hunger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hungrijaną</i> (“to hunger”). Compare Old English <i>hyngran</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhuɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, mean, believe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind, reason</li><li>country estate?, farm property</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hugiz</i>.</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure, hoard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huzdą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hoard, store up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huzdijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accumulate, save up, stockpile</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewk-</i>, the root also underlying <i>𐌷𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌼𐌰</i> (hiuhma, “mass, multitude, heap”), with the suffix <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger</li><li>famine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunhruz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gracious, kind, clement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulistrą</i>, cognate to English <i>holster</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, wrap, veil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huljaną</i>, compare Dutch <i>hullen</i>, German <i>hüllen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cave, cavern</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>&#42;hulą</i>, with a suffix of unclear origin or meaning. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈhund/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hundred (count of hundred)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i>. Doublet of <i>𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (kintus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (hundafaþs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a centurion, officer commanding 100 soldiers</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (hund) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;faþs), calque of Koine Greek <i>ἑκατοντάρχης</i> (hekatontárkhēs), itself a calque of Latin <i>centuriō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhunds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hundaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwn̥tós</i>, derived from <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i> (“dog”). Cognates include Old English <i>hund</i>, Old Norse <i>hundr</i> and Old High German <i>hunt</i>, and Ancient Greek <i>κύων</i> (kúōn), which it translates (along with derived <i>κυνάριον</i> (kunárion, “little dog”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captivity, capture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunþaz</i>, from the strong verb <i>&#42;hinþaną</i> (“to reach for”). Cognates include Old English <i>hūþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering, sacrifice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hunslą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altar</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌻</i> (hunsl, “sacrifice”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (staþs, “place”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌻</i> (hunsl, “sacrifice”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan); translates Ancient Greek <i>σπένδω</i> (spéndō). Compare English <i>housel</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hupiz</i> (“hip”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrainį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (hrains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purification, cleansing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hrainjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure-hearted, goodhearted</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (hrains, “pure”) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍄𐍉</i> (hairtō, “heart”), a calque of Latin <i>mundus corde</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse, purify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrainijaną</i>; compare <i>&#42;hrainisōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clean</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrainiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhrɛː.waˌduː.boː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) turtledove</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraiwą</i> (“corpse, carcass”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌿𐌱𐍉</i> (*dūbō, “dove”, from <i>&#42;dūbǭ</i>).</p></html>:/ˈhramjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crucify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hramjaną</i> (“to put into a frame”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a staff</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrungō</i>. Cognate with English <i>rung</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhruːk/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) crow (cry of the rooster)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Lehmann compares Old Norse <i>hraukr</i> (“cormorant”) and Old English <i>ġehrūxl</i> (“noise”) and derives it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ker-</i> with a g-extension, with Ancient Greek <i>κραυγή</i> (kraugḗ, “cry”) deriving similarly.</p></html>:/ˈhruːk.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crow (to make the sound of a rooster)</li></ol><p><i>𐌷𐍂𐌿𐌺</i> (hrūk) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious</li><li>triumphant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōþīgaz</i>; equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌷𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;hrōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōpijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry, clamour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōpaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrōtą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an aromatic plant, hyssop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ὕσσωπος</i> (hússōpos), ultimately from a Semitic source.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōhô</i>.</p></html>:/ˈhoːloːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse falsely</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōlōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐍉𐍂𐍃</i> (hōrs, “adulterer”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit adultery</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌷𐍉𐍂𐍃</i> (hōrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (-inōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer, fornicator, whoremonger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōraz</i> (“adulterer”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teng-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thanks</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθadiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whither, to where (<i>relative</i>)</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þadrê</i> (“thither”), suffixed with <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) silence, muteness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þahān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains, “to keep quiet”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be silent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þagjaną</i> (“to be silent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tak-</i> (“to be silent”), cognate Latin <i>taceō</i> (“to be silent”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) clay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanhǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tenk-</i> (“to thicken, be solid”), related to <i>&#42;þinhaną</i> (“to thrive”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thence, from there</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (þar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html>:/ˈθaθroː.iː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from whence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (þaþrō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html>:/ˈθaθroːh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(from) thence</li><li>since that time</li><li>afterward, after that, then</li><li>then (followed by <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan))</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (þaþrō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</p></html>:/ˈθaθroːθθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then, afterward</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>𐌸𐌰𐌸𐍂𐍉𐌷</i> (þaþrōh) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) through</li><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) by</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þerhw</i> whence also Old English <i>þerh</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurhw</i> whence English <i>through</i>, Dutch <i>door</i> (“through”) and German <i>durch</i> (“through”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work through, to keep on working</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (arbaidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry through</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html>:/θɛrhɡaˈliːkoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to transfer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷-</i> (þairh-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉𐌽</i> (galeikōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉𐌳𐌰</i> (þairhgaleikōda)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through, by</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see through</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh, “through”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be awake or keep watch through the night</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (wakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>persist, continue on, persevere</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷</i> (þairh, “through”) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “remain, abide”).</p></html>:/ˈθɛr.koː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye (hole at the end of a needle through which thread is passed)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thessalonica</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Θεσσᾰλονῑ́κη</i> (Thessălonī́kē).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Thessalonian</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Thessalonians, two epistles of the New Testament</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Θεσσαλονικεύς</i> (Thessalonikeús).</p></html>:/θan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then, at that time, when</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yet more, further, longer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍃</i> (mais).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>since, thereafter</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;seiþs). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. -seiths = ?</i></p></html>:/ˈθandeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>while, as long as</li><li>because, since</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þandê</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌴</i> (þandē)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whatever house</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌴</i> (þanē), alternative form of (or scribal error for) <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (þanei, masculine accusative singular of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌴𐌹</i> (saei)), with <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌴𐌹</i> (gardei, accusative singular of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards) + <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei))</p></html>:/ˈθannu/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, in consequence, consequently, hence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌿</i> (nu).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in a dichotomous question</i>) or (instead)</li><li>(<i>after a comparative</i>) than</li><li>Introduces the result of a conditional clause.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þau</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>though</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þauh</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿</i> (þau) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>even though</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐌷</i> (þauh) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (jabai).</p></html>:/ˈθɔr.βan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need</li><li>to need to, to be required to, to have to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurbaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθɔr.niːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of thorn</li></ol><p><i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃</i> (þaurnus, “thorn”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”). Compare Old English <i>þyrnen</i> and Old High German <i>durnīn</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθɔr.nus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorn (any shrub or small tree that bears thorns)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurnuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurpą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make thirsty</li><li>to be thirsty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurzijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (þaursus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacking water, dry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þursuz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθɔr.stiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurstuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurftiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (þaurban) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p><p>A past participle.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (þaurfts)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (þaurfts) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza).</p></html>:/θar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there, in that place</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þar</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, want</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þarbō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary</li><li>needy, having need</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þarbaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þarfr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (þar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;of cloth) unprocessed, unrefined, new</li></ol></html>:/ˈθaruh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there also, and there</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (þar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</p></html>:/θatˈɛːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the one thing, only this/that</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only (after <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni, “not”))</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍄𐌰</i> (þata) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽</i> (ain), neuter of <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains). Compare Old English þæt an</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌸𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (þatainei, “only”)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍄𐌰</i> (þata) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (ains) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei). Compare Old English þæt an, Old Norse ateins and Icelandic aðeins</p></html>:/ˈθataʍah/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>whatever, whatsoever (followed by <i>𐌸𐌴𐌹</i> (þei))</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍄𐌰</i> (þata) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰</i> (ƕa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that</li><li>(<i>after a negation</i>) [not] as though</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌰𐍄𐌰</i> (þata) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) (the person/thing) who, that, which</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰</i> (sa) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html>:/θeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>𐌾𐌰𐌷 𐌸𐌴</i> (jah þē) and <i>𐌽𐌹 𐌸𐌴 𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌹𐍃</i> (ni þē haldis)</li></ol><p>An old instrumental of <i>𐍃𐌰</i> (sa); cognate with Old English <i>þȳ</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθeː.iː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>in order that</li><li>because</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌴</i> (þē) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that, so that</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) that</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þī</i>, earlier <i>*tey</i>, a locative or instrumental formation to the base of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i>. Related to Latin <i>sic</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθiːhan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prosper</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þinhaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tenk-</i> (“to be suitable”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occasion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þinhsą</i>, related to <i>&#42;þingą</i>.</p></html>:/θiːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>your, yours, thy, thine (singular)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þīnaz</i>, whence also Old English <i>þīn</i> (English <i>thine</i>, <i>thy</i>), Old High German <i>dīn</i> (German <i>dein</i>), Old Norse <i>þínn</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθiː.ʍoː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunder (sound caused by lightning)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (þius). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌸𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌴𐌹</i> (þizēei)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to pull, stretch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þinsaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθiu̯bi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiubiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (þiufs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly, furtively, covertly</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌱𐌹</i> (þiubi). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (þiufs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people, tribe</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the heathen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudō</i>, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewtéh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingdom</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) royal court or palace</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (þiudans) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudanaz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐍃</i> (-ns) (compare <i>𐌺𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (kindins)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reign</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (þiudans) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government</li><li>kingdom, empire</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus). Compare Old English <i>þēodnes</i> (“connection, assembly”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) in a manner characteristic of heathens or gentiles</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;þiudisks, “pertaining to a nation or nations”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, suffix forming adverbs from adjectives), calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐθνικῶς</i> (ethnikôs). The first element can be analyzed as <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda, “people, nation”) + <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks). It is unattested in the limited corpus that survives, but likely existed in the Gothic language as an inherited word from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiudiskaz</i>. Compare <i>𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍉</i> (iudaiwiskō, “in a manner characteristic of Jews”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boon, a good thing</li></ol><p>Apparently substantivized from the neuter singular of <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;þiuþs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiudijaz</i> (“good, favorable”). See also the related terms below.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þiuþjan) or <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (þiuþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodness</li><li>blessing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þiuþjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blessing</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;þiuþs, “good, kind”, from <i>&#42;þiudijaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bless</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;þiuþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan); the first element seems to derive from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudjaz</i> (“good, kind; friendly”), but this derivation is not unproblematic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evangelize, bring good tidings</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (þiuþ) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (spillōn), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐαγγελίζεσθαι</i> (euangelízesthai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant boy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (þius, “servant”) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌿𐍃</i> (magus, “boy”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þewaz</i>.</p></html>:/θiu̯ɸs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeubaz</i>, cognate with Proto-Norse <i>&#42;ᚦᛖᚢᛒᚨᛉ</i> (&#42;þeubaʀ) (attested in <i>ᛗᚨᚱᛁᚦᛖᚢᛒᚨᛉ</i> (mariþeubaʀ)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>especially, particularly, above all</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐍃</i> (þis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (-hun).</p></html>:/θis.ˈʍa.ðuh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to wherever, whithersoever</li></ol><p><i>𐌸𐌹𐍃</i> (þis) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐌸</i> (ƕaþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</p></html>:/ˈθiswaduh ˈθadiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whithersoever, to wherever</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>whoever, whosoever, anyone that</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐍃</i> (þis) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐌶𐌿𐌷</i> (ƕazuh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wherever</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌹𐍃</i> (þis) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐍂𐌿𐌷</i> (ƕaruh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servitude (<i>accusative singular</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (þius), but the suffix is unclear. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bondwoman, maid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þiwī</i>; feminine equivalent of <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (þius). Cognate with Old Norse <i>þý</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tender</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to frighten, scare</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) flight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flugiz</i> (“flight”).</p></html>:/ˈθliuhan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee, escape</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fleuhaną</i> (“to flee”).</p></html>:/θuː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>thou, you (second-person singular personal pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þū</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;túh₂</i>, compare Ancient Greek <i>σύ</i> (sú), Old Church Slavonic <i>тꙑ</i> (ty) and Sanskrit <i>त्वम्</i> (tvam).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌴𐌹𐌸</i> (þugkeiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳</i> (þugkjand)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seem, appear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þunkijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) you, who...</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌿</i> (þū) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conscience</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þunhtuz</i>. From the same root as <i>𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þugkjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience</li><li>suffering</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Passion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (þulān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains). The sense "Passion" is a clipping of the phrase <i>𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃 𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (þulains xristaus, “suffering of Christ”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, endure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þulāną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθuː.sun.di/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thousand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þūsundī</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>þūsend</i> and Old Norse <i>þúsund</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tribune, an officer commanding a thousand soldiers</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌿𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌹</i> (þūsundi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;faþs, “lord, master, commander, ruler”), calque of Ancient Greek <i>χιλίαρχος</i> (khilíarkhos), itself a calque of Old Persian <i>&#42;hazārapatiš</i> (“commander of thousand [men]”). Compare with Old English <i>þusendealdormann</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌸𐌿𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌾𐍉𐍃</i> (þūsundjōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trumpet</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌸𐌿𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;þuts) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (haurn). The first element may be related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeutǭ</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to trumpet, blow a horn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐌿𐍄𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (þuthaurn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þragjaną</i> (“to run”). Compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;tregess</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθram.stiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) grasshopper, locust (insect)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contentiousness</li></ol><p>The second element of the compound is <i>𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌸𐌴𐌹</i> (balþei, “boldness”). The first element is closely related to Icelandic <i>þrasa</i> (“to quarrel, to bicker”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consolation, solace</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐍂𐌰𐍆𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þrafstjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to console, comfort</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to cheer up</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd, throng</li><li>to oppress, trouble</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrinhwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) difficulty, distress</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þreihan) +&lrm; <i>-𐍃𐌻</i> (-sl), the latter from <i>&#42;-slą</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>þrøngsl</i> (“straits, distress”).</p></html>:/ˈθriːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrīz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tréyes</i>. Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>tria</i> and Old Norse <i>þrír</i>, and Koine Greek <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈθriːs ˈtiɡ.jus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirty (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrīz tigiwiz</i> and cognate to Old Norse <i>þrír tigir</i>. Contrast Crimean Gothic <i>treithyen</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθrid.ja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the third</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þridjô</i>. Close in formation to Koine Greek <i>τρίτος</i> (trítos, “third”), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈθri.ja ˈhun.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) three hundred (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “three hundreds”. Compare Old English <i>þrēo hund</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thresh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þreskaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leprosy</li></ol><p>From a Proto-Germanic compound, which Orel reconstructs as <i>&#42;þrūtezfellą</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>blæcþrustfel</i> (“leprosy”). The first element is usually considered to be related with <i>&#42;þrūtaną</i> (“to swell”). The second element is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fellą</i> (“hide, skin”). Compare <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌻𐌹</i> (faurafilli, “uncircumcision”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leprous</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to train, drill, exercise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þrōþijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈθwahan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wash</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwahaną</i>; cognate with Old English <i>þwēan</i> and Old Norse <i>þvá</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a washing, a bath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwahlą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, wrath, ire</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌷𐍃</i> (þwairhs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwerhaz</i> (“cross, adverse”). Cognates include English <i>thwart</i>, Dutch <i>dwars</i>, Swedish <i>tvär</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌷𐌻</i> (þwahl)</li></ol></html>:/ˈθwastiθa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stability, security</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌸𐍅𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;þwasts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θυμίαμα</i> (thumíama).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thomas, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Θωμᾶς</i> (Thōmâs), from Aramaic <i>תאומא</i> (“Thomas”), from <i>תאמא</i> (“twin”).</p></html>:/i.aˈiːrus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jair, a Biblical character.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Iaīrus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰάϊρος</i> (Iáïros), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יָאִיר</i> (yāʾîr, literally “he will light up”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jericho</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰεριχώ</i> (Ierikhṓ), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרִיחוֹ</i> (yərīḥō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jerusalem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰερουσαλήμ</i> (Ierousalḗm), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרוּשָׁלַיִם</i> (Yərūšālayim).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌹𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌼</i> (iairusalēm)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἱεροσόλυμα</i> (Hierosóluma), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרוּשָׁלַיִם</i> (Yərūšālayim).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌹𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐍅𐌼𐍉𐍃</i> (iairusaulwmōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌰𐌺𐍉𐌱𐌿𐍃</i> (iakōbus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jacob, the Biblical character</li><li>James, the son of Zebedee, Biblical character</li><li>James, the son of Alphaeus, Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰάκωβος</i> (Iákōbos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (ibai)</li></ol></html>:/ˈibɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Interrogative particle&#58; starts a yes-or-no question.</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>lest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ibai</i>, variant of <i>&#42;jabai</i> (whence <i>𐌾𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (jabai)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountainside, hillside, descent (<i>dative singular</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of equal substance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ebnalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌹𐌱𐌽𐍃</i> (ibns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of equal beauty or shape</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌱𐌽𐍃</i> (ibns) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (skauns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌱𐌽𐍃</i> (ibns, “even”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-assus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>even</li><li>plain, flat, level</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ebnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>backward, back</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abuhaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌹𐍃</i> (igqis)</li></ol></html>:/ˈiŋkʷar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>of you two (second-person dual possessive), your</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inkweraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>A prefix meaning "again", "back", "anew", equivalent to <i>re-</i>.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idi-</i>, <i>&#42;idi</i>, <i>&#42;ida</i> (“back, backwards, again”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;éti</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>et-</i>, German dialectal <i>it-</i> (“again, back”), Icelandic <i>ið-</i> (“again, back”), English <i>ed-</i> (“again, back”), Welsh <i>ad-</i>, Welsh <i>ed-</i> (“again, back”), Latin <i>et</i> (“and”), Latin <i>at</i> (“but, moreover”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌹𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌴𐌳𐌿𐌽</i> (iddjēdun) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌹𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌰</i> (iddja) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repentance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌳𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍉𐌽</i> (idreigōn) or a related form + <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regret, repent</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, disgrace</li><li>insult</li><li>curse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idiwītą</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌹𐌳-</i> (id-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄</i> (&#42;weit). Cognates include Old English <i>edwīt</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse, revile, reprobate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idiwītijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌹𐌳𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄</i> (idweit) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan). Compare Old English <i>edwītan</i>, <i>edwītian</i>, Old High German <i>itawīzen</i>, <i>itawīzōn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jesus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰησοῦς</i> (Iēsoûs), from Hebrew <i>ישוע</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌹</i> (izei)</li></ol></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, she, it, they; 3rd person personal pronoun (third-person personal pronoun)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌹𐌴𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (iēsus)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;iz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;izi</i>.</p><p>Abbreviation.</p></html>:/ˈiziː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌴𐌹</i> (saei, “that, which, who”, masculine nominative singular)</li><li>alternative form of <i>𐌸𐌰𐌹𐌴𐌹</i> (þaiei, “that, which, who”, masculine nominative plural of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌴𐌹</i> (saei))</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐍃</i> (is) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html>:/ˈizwar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>your (second-person plural possessive)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;izweraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, however, though</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;idi</i>.</p></html>:/ik/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>first person singular personal pronoun; I</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ek</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;eǵh₂óm</i>, compare Sanskrit <i>अहम्</i> (ahám), Ancient Greek <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ), Latin <i>ego</i> and Old Church Slavonic <i>азъ</i> (azŭ).</p></html>:/ˈikiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) I, who...</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌺</i> (ik) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html>:/ˈiku/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>whether I</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌺</i> (ik, “I”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿</i> (-u, interrogative particle)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌹𐌼𐌼𐌰</i> (imma) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/in/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) in, within, among</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) on, during</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) into</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) on account of, because of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in</i>.</p></html>:/in ˈfrumistjam/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first of all</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐν πρώτοις</i> (en prṓtois)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>in-, en-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;in</i>.</p></html>:/inˈaɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten, order or warn sternly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;agjan).</p></html>:/ˈinahiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobriety, soundness of mind</li></ol><p><i>𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (inahs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html>:/ˈinahs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌷𐌰</i> (aha).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke, make jealous or angry</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (aljanōn).</p></html>:/inˈbranː.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to inflame (to set on fire)</li></ol><p><i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;brannjan)</p></html>:/ˈinɡaˌliːkoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to change</li></ol><p><i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉𐌽</i> (galeikōn)</p></html>:/inˈɡardja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that is in one's house</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of one's household</li></ol><p><i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poison</li></ol></html>:/inˈɡramjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gramjan).</p></html>:/inˈdroːbnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become troubled or disordered</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐍉𐌱𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (drōbnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌲𐌵𐌹𐍃</i> (igqis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretext, excuse</li><li>occasion</li></ol><p>Apparently contains the suffix <i>-𐌹𐌻𐍉</i> (-ilō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌺𐌹𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;kilþs) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kelþaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈinkunja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>countryman</li></ol><p><i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (kuni) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja)</p></html>:/inˈliuhtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlighten</li><li>to make see, cause to see</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (liuhtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchange, substitution</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (inmaidjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform, change</li><li>to exchange</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maidjan). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to exchange”); compare Latin <i>mūtō</i>, <i>mūtuus</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Immanuel</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἐμμανουήλ</i> (Emmanouḗl).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in (in the direction of the speaker)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;inn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within, inside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;innai</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from inside, from within</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (inna, “inside”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html>:/ˈinnakunds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of one’s household</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (inna) +&lrm; <i>-𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-kunds), thus literally “born within”.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from inside, from within</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;innanē</i>, cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᛁᚾᚨᚾᚨ</i> (inana).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring or carry in(side)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍄𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (atbairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or enter inside</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍄𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (atgaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ingress, entrance into</li></ol><p>A derivation from <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐍄𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (innatgaggan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs) following the pattern of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganhtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to usher in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍄𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (attiuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, go in</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;innganganą</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, go in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (galeiþan).</p></html>:/inˈniman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, accept, take in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html>:/inˈniu̯.ji.θa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal</li><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) the Feast of Dedication; Hanukkah</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa), calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐγκαίνια</i> (enkaínia).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inner, interior</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip past, to sneak in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍆𐍃𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (ufsliupan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽</i> (inn) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (wairpan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) without, except</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;enu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌽𐌿</i> (inu)</li></ol><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌹𐌽</i> (in) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐌿</i> (inu) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽</i> (in) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html>:/inˈrɔhtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to groan</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) + a Germanic root of uncertain etymology, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krewh₂-</i> (“blood”) and thus related to <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌳𐌿𐌱𐍉</i> (hraiwadubō, “turtledove”, literally “corpse dove”).</p></html>:/inˈriːraːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quake</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (reirān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to spoil completely, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (riurjan).</p></html>:/inˈsaxts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrative, presentation</li><li>declaration, view</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;insahtiz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (insakan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/inˈsɛːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐌰𐌽</i> (saian).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let down with cords</li></ol><p><i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;sailjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sailijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look into, to see into</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present a case, expound, explain</li><li>to recount</li><li>to designate, put before</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away, dispatch (with a task)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sandjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imminent, to be at hand</li><li>to persist, insist, urge</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swinþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/inˈtandjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to burn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (tandjan).</p></html>:/inˈtund.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to burn up (catch fire and burn until destroyed)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (tundnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graft in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐍂𐌿𐍃𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;trusgjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. origin of trusgjan?</i></p></html>:/inˈwaɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move (excite to action)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to be troubled</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wagjan).</p></html>:/inˈwandjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, subvert</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worship</li><li>to salute</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;weitan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wītaną</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reject, disown, deny</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;widan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wedaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injustice, wickedness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (inwinds, “unjust, perverse, corrupt”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turned aside, perverse, unjust, unrighteous</li></ol></html>:/inˈwisands/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>past (having already happened&#59; in the past)</li></ol><p>Present participle of an unattested verb of <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to be, remain”)</p></html>:/ˈinwitoːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the law, law-abiding</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌽-</i> (in-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (witōþ), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἔννομος</i> (énnomos).</p></html>:/inˈfiːnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be moved with compassion, have compassion</li><li>to be tender</li></ol><p>Unknown; there are no Germanic cognates. Lehmann suggests a connection to Latin <i>pius</i> (“pious”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewH-</i> (“to be clean, pure”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌹𐌴𐍃𐌿</i> (iēsu)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌹𐌴𐍃𐌿𐌰</i> (iēsua)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judea</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰουδαία</i> (Ioudaía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Judea</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌰</i> (iudaia) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌽𐌳</i> (land).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰουδαῖος</i> (Ioudaîos), or possibly from Latin <i>iūdaeus</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Jewishly, in a manner characteristic of Jews</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (judaiwisks, “Jewish, pertaining to Jews”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, suffix forming adverbs from adjectives), calque of Koine Greek <i>Ἰουδαϊκῶς</i> (Ioudaïkôs). Compare <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍉</i> (þiudiskō, “in a manner characteristic of heathens or gentiles”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to live as a Jew, to be Jewish</li></ol><p>Calque of Koine Greek <i>ἰουδαΐζειν</i> (ioudaḯzein). Formed from <i>𐌾𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (judaiwisks, “Jewish”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn, “denominative suffix”). See <i>𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍉</i> (iudaiwiskō) for more.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judas Iscariot (disciple of Jesus)</li><li>Judah (one of the Hebrew patriarchs)</li><li>Judah (a tribe of Israel)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰούδας</i> (Ioúdas).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌹𐌴𐍃𐌿𐌹𐍃</i> (iēsuis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) crowd, throng, multitude</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upwards, up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;upp</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uppai</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from above</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌰</i> (iupa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew, afresh, from the beginning</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌰</i> (iupa) and <i>𐌹𐌿𐍀</i> (iup). The literal meaning of this adverb was likely 'from above', while the meaning 'anew' is a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>ἄνωθεν</i> (ánōthen). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. suffix?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌹𐌴𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (iēsus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>better; comparative degree of <i>𐌲𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (gōþs)</li></ol><p>Possibly ultimately related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wésus</i> (“good”). Compare <i>𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (iusila).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recovery, easing</li></ol><p>Possibly ultimately related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wésus</i> (“good”). Compare <i>𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (iusiza). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, she, it, they; 3rd person personal pronoun (third-person personal pronoun)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐌹𐌴𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (iēsus)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;iz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;izi</i>.</p><p>Abbreviation.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be</li><li>to remain, abide</li><li>to feast, make merry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (“to be, remain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i>. Compare Latin <i>Vesta</i> (“goddess of the hearth”), Sanskrit <i>वसति</i> (vásati, “dwell”). Some forms are suppletive and instead derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésti</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (“to consume, feast”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to graze”). The usage of the adverb <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) to qualify this verb is very common (three out of the four total attestations of <i>wisan</i>); the resulting phrase <i>waila wisan</i> (in various conjugated forms) seemingly represents a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐφραίνεσθαι</i> (euphraínesthai), which it translates. However, the same Greek verb may also be translated simply as <i>wisan</i> without the adverb <i>waila</i> (Luke 15:24).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isaac, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰσαάκ</i> (Isaák), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יִצְחָק</i> (Yiṣḥāq).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Israel (a Biblical region of Western Asia roughly coextensive with the modern State of Israel, known in the Bible as the Land of Israel and considered the ancestral homeland of the Jewish people)# (<i>historical</i>) Israel (an ancient kingdom in Western Asia, roughly coextensive with the modern State of Israel and the Land of Israel)# (<i>historical</i>) Israel (an ancient kingdom that occupied the northern part of the Land of Israel and modern State of Israel, as distinct from Judah)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰσραήλ</i> (Israḗl).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Israelite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰσρᾱηλῑ́της</i> (Isrāēlī́tēs).</p></html>:/ˈitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;etaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>etan</i>.</p></html>:/ˈiftuma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>next, following</li></ol><p>Alteration or variant of <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (aftuma, “aftermost”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftumô</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aftai</i> (“after, behind, in back”). Equivalent to <i>𐌰𐍆𐍄𐌰</i> (afta, “behind”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma, superlative suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>𐌹𐍉𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌴𐍃</i> (iōhannēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>John, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωάννης</i> (Iōánnēs), from Hebrew <i>יוחנן</i> (Yōḥānān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Joses (Biblical character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωσῆς</i> (Iōsês).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Joseph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωσήφ</i> (Iōsḗph), from Hebrew <i>יוסף</i> (Yōsēph).</p></html>:/ˈka.bal.laː.riːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) horseman, rider; hostler?</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>caballārius</i>.</p></html>:/ˈkɛːsar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesar, emperor</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Caesar</i>, likely via Ancient Greek <i>Καῖσαρ</i> (Kaîsar). Borrowed separately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaisaraz</i>, which is a Northwest Germanic form.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax paid to the Roman emperor</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌺𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂</i> (kaisar) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌳</i> (gild).</p></html>:/ˈkal.boː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) heifer (young cow)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalbǭ</i>, a feminizing ōn-stem extension of <i>&#42;kalbaz</i> (“calf”). Cognate with Old High German <i>kalba</i> (archaic German <i>Kalbe</i>).</p></html>:/ˈkalds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold (having a low temperature)</li><li>cold (of the weather)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaldaz</i> (“cold”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fornication, adultery, lechery</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐌾𐍉</i> (kalkjō, “whore”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus).</p></html>:/ˈkalk.joː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) whore, harlot</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalkjǭ</i> (“whore”). Perhaps borrowed from Latin <i>calx</i> (“lime”) and remodeled after the genitive <i>calcis</i>, as Heinertz explains “[...] slaves for sale had their feet covered with lime or chalk. — Terms for ‘whore’ may be taken from ‘slave, captive in war’”, which is reinforced by Lehmann.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kannijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colossian</li><li>(<i>in the dative plural</i>) Colossians, an epistle of the New Testament</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Κολοσσαεύς</i> (Kolossaeús).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buffet, slap</li></ol><p>An iterative/intensive derivation from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaupōną</i> (“to trade, buy”), signalling repeated or forceful action. In several other Germanic languages iterative forms became the regular past of the simple verb (such as Old High German <i>slagazen</i> &lt; <i>slagon</i>, Old Norse <i>cúpa</i> &lt; <i>kaupa</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trade, barter</li><li>to do business</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaupōną</i>, from Latin <i>caupo</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple gift, offering</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>κορβᾶν</i> (korbân), from Biblical Hebrew <i>קרבן</i> (korbán).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple treasury</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>κορβονᾶς</i> (korbonâs), ultimately from Biblical Hebrew <i>קרבן</i> (korbán).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (kaurus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (kaurus, “heavy”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html>:/ˈkɔrn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn, grain (harvested seeds of the main cereal plant grown in a given region, such as wheat or barley)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kurną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵr̥h₂nóm</i>. Cognates include Crimean Gothic <i>kor</i>, Old English <i>corn</i> and Old High German <i>korn</i>, and Latin <i>grānum</i>.</p></html>:/ˈkɔr.noː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kernel (single seed or grain, especially of corn or wheat)</li></ol><p>An n-stem derivation of <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽</i> (kaurn, “wheat, grain”). Compare Old High German <i>cherno</i>&nbsp;m (“kernel, grain”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden</li><li>to oppress</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (kaurus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burdensome</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kuruz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷréh₂us</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taste</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kauzijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get a haircut</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;capillāre</i>, from <i>capillus</i>. Possibly (according to Dennis Green) borrowed from Latin by Goths serving in the Roman army; Roman-style short, military haircuts would have been a novelty to them to be referred to with a loanword (contrasted with native <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (skaban)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, concern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karō</i>. Cognates include English <i>care</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dungeon, prison</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>carcer</i>, likely specifically from a Vulgar Latin variant form <i>&#42;carcara</i>. Parallel borrowing with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;karkārī</i> (“dungeon, prison”).</p></html>:/ˈka.roːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care, to be concerned</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;karōną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌰</i> (kara) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar, pot, vessel</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) belongings of a household, utensils, furniture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kazą</i> (“vessel”). The second sense seems to be a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>σκεῦος</i> (skeûos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kazjô</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌺𐌰𐍃</i> (kas, “jar, pot, vessel”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja). Cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kaʀjō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈka.tils/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;katilaz</i>, usually considered to come from Latin <i>catillus</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, warranty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cautio</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprout, germinate</li><li>to bud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kīnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li><li>upper room</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Celtic language, probably Gaulish <i>cēlicnon</i> (“tower”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb, uterus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kilþį̄</i>, a form related to the etymon of English <i>child</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>governor (of the Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>From (a form closely related to) Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kinþiz</i> with the suffix <i>-𐌽𐍃</i> (-ns), as in <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (þiudans).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek</li><li>chin, jaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kinnuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small coin, cent</li></ol><p>From Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;centus</i> (“a type of coin”), derived (perhaps via <i>centenionalis</i>) from <i>centum</i>. Doublet of <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (hund).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove, test</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;keusaną</i>. Cognates include English <i>choose</i>, Dutch <i>kiezen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sound a cymbal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌺𐌻𐌹𐍃𐌼𐍉</i> (klismō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈklis.moː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) cymbal</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knewą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kneel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knussijaną</i>, from <i>&#42;knussuz</i>, ultimately a derivate of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race, people</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;knōdiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A group of people eating together</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cubitus</i>.</p></html>:/ˈkuk.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kukjaną</i>. Perhaps cognate with English <i>kiss</i>, Dutch <i>kussen</i>, German <i>küssen</i> and Icelandic <i>kyssa</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>arise! (transliteration of an Aramaic saying by Jesus)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>κούμι</i> (koúmi), itself a transliteration of an Aramaic term.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetter, chain</li></ol><p>Unclear. Near-cognates are found in Old English <i>cynewiþþe</i> and Old High German <i>kunawid</i>, but these do not correspond exactly to the Gothic form or to each other. Has also been connected to Sanskrit <i>गुण</i> (guṇa, “string”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunþiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (kunþs, “known”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kin, family, race</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunją</i> (“kin, family”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to produce, beget”).</p></html>:/ˈkunːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, to be familiar with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kunnaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵneh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, proof</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kustuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌺𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (kiusan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek (person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>graecus</i> (“Greek”). The devoicing from <i>g-</i> to <i>k-</i> has a parallel in <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌺𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (marikreitus), which derives from Ancient Greek <i>μαργαρίτης</i> (margarítēs), Latin <i>margarīta</i>. Compare Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;Krēkō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈkriu̯s.tan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnash, grind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kreustaną</i> (“to crush, grind, strike, smash”). Cognate to Old Swedish <i>krysta</i> (“to crackle”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gnashing, grating</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌺𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (kriustan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quirinius or Cyrenius, Roman governor of Syria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Κυρήνιος</i> (Kurḗnios).</p></html>:/ˈlaŋɡamoːðiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longanimity, patience, forbearance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (laggs) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (mōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), calque of Ancient Greek <i>μακροθυμία</i> (makrothumía) (possibly via Latin <i>longanimitas</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langį̄</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (laggs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/laŋɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;langaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay, set, put, deposit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lagjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hospitably, charitably</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (laþōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, invite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laþōną</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calling</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (laþōn, “to call, invite”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calling</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (laþōn, “to call, invite”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to berate, revile</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lahaną</i>, but this does not explain why the Gothic verb reduplicates. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remainder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laibō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legion</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>λεγεών</i> (legeṓn), from Latin <i>legiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump around, to jump for joy, to dance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌸</i> (laikiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laikaz</i>, <i>&#42;laikiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) a reading (a specific passage to be read in whole)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lēctiō</i> (“reading”). The date of borrowing is uncertain: it is only attested as a gloss, specifically in manuscripts that were produced in Ostrogothic Italy, but it may have been used by Gothic scribes and scholars before the conquest of Italy in the late fifth century.</p></html>:/ˈlɛː.saː.riːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher, master</li></ol><p>Most likely from <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍉</i> (&#42;laisō, “teaching”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-areis).</p><p>Alternatively, from <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laisjan, “to teach”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis, “-er”), although this suffix is more commonly attached to nouns than to verbs.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (laisāreis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled at teaching</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laisjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p><p>Alternatively, and more likely, derived from an unrecorded <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍉</i> (&#42;laisō, “teaching, doctrine”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laizō</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs), since this suffix is more commonly attached to nouns than to verbs.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching, doctrine</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laisjan, “to teach”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/ˈlɛːsjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach (according to Wright, it takes the accusative case for both the thing taught and the person taught)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to learn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laizijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow, pursue</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laistijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>track, footprint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laistiz</i>, <i>&#42;laistaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leys-</i> (“to trace, track”).</p></html>:/ˈlɛ.wa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) lion (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lewô</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>leō</i>. Saskia Pronk-Tiethoff reconstructed the term in 2013 as <i>*liwa</i> before being aware of its attestation (which was discovered as a genitive plural <i>laiwanē</i> in the <i>Gothica Bononiensia</i>, first published around that very time), attributing the Slavic forms to a Gothic intermediary based on a hypothetical <i>i</i>-vocalism as she thought would be expected from a Gothic term derived from <i>leō</i>. The form <i>laiwa</i> is not an entirely certain reading due to the poor legibility of the manuscript; the correct reading may still be <i>liwa</i>, but Falluomini (2017) favours the form with <i>-ai-</i>.</p><p>Some Gothic given names attested in Latinized form may also contain this term. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. this does not look like the regular outcome expected from a direct borrowing of leō</i></p></html>:/ˈlamb/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheep (woolly ruminant)</li><li>lamb (young sheep)</li></ol><p>To the same root as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lambaz</i> (“lamb”), though the Gothic word is an <i>a</i>-stem, not an <i>s</i>-stem.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;landą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lendʰ-</i> (“land, heath”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laugnijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) proportions of the body</li></ol></html>:/ˈlɔ.hat.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lighten, flash with lightning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luhatjaną</i>, whence also Old High German <i>lohazan</i> (“to gleam”). Equivalent to the zero grade of the root of <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌸</i> (liuhaþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-atjan).</p></html>:/ˈlɔ(ː)h.mu.ni/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning (flash of light)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any blaze of fire from heaven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> + <i>-𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (-muni), a variant of <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni). The latter form suffixed to the same root in the o-grade may have yielded Middle English <i>lēvene</i> (whence English <i>levin</i>), for which reason Lehmann (1986) assumes an o-grade in the Gothic word as well. Close in formation to Old English <i>lēoma</i> (“radiance, lustre”) and Latin <i>lūmen</i> (“light”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (lauhmuni)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward</li><li>wages</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;launą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unthankful, ungrateful</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌽</i> (laun) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌲𐍃</i> (&#42;wargs). The latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wargaz</i> (“criminal, outlaw”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty, devoid</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) -less, lacking, devoid of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lausaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐌰𐌹</i> (lausawaurdai)</li></ol></html>:/ˈlɔːsawɔrdiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vain jangling</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (lausawaurds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ματαιολογίᾱ</i> (mataiologíā).</p></html>:/ˈlɔːsawɔrdi/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vain babbling</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (lausawaurds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>κενοφωνίᾱ</i> (kenophōníā).</p></html>:/ˈlɔːsawɔrds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talking vainly, speaking idly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (-waurds) (see <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd)). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ματαιολόγος</i> (mataiológos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fasting</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌵𐌹𐌸𐍂𐍃</i> (lausqiþrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) empty-bellied</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus, “empty”) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (qiþus, “belly”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. whence the -r-?</i></p></html>:/ˈlɔːshandja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty-handed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus, “empty”) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (handus, “hand”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make loose, loosen</li><li>to set free, deliver <i>(from something)</i></li><li>(<i>of property</i>) to collect, claim, exact</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lausijaną</i> (“to make loose, set free”).</p></html>:/ˈlɔːfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf (part of a plant)</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) foliage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laubą</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>lēaf</i>, Old High German <i>loub</i> and Old Norse <i>lauf</i>. The Germanic cognates are neuter, and Lehmann believes that this term in the sense of "foliage" is neuter as well and contrasts with the masculine "leaf", comparing the relationship between those two to that between Latin <i>vallum</i> and Latin <i>vallus</i>.</p></html>:/ˈlasiws/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feeble, weak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lasiwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, sloth, laxness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌰𐍄𐍃</i> (lats) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delay, retard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;latjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lazy, slothful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lataz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light (in weight)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;linhtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cider, fermented drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līþu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body</li><li>corpse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līką</i> (“body, corpse”). Cognate with English <i>lich</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approval</li><li>purpose</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (leikān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains).</p></html>:/ˈliːkaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līkāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carnal, corporeal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺</i> (leik, “body”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (lēkeis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (lēkinōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lītilaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līhwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doctor, physician, leech</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēkijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>healing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (lēkeis, “physician”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus). The word is only attested as the genitive singular <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (leikinassaus) (with a diphthong <i>ei</i> instead of long <i>ē</i> in the first syllable), but that is assumed to have been a spelling variant based on its apparent etymology.</p></html>:/ˈleːkinoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēkinōną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌻𐌴𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (lēkeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (-inōn). Cognate with Old Norse <i>lækna</i>, Old High German <i>lāchinon</i> (> Middle High German <i>lāchenen</i>).</p></html>:/ˈleː.tan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lētaną</i> (“to let, allow, leave alone”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occasion, opportunity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to betray</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēwijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li></ol><p><i>𐌻𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (libān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;libjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyp-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie (down), to rest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ligjaną</i> (“to lie”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;légʰyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;legrą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈli.θus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) limb, member</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liþuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why (said by Jesus on the cross)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>λεμα</i> (lema), itself a transliteration of Aramaic <i>למא</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovely</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (liufs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks). Diachronically, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leubalīkaz</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>lieflijk</i>, German <i>lieblich</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage, wedlock</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leugō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, tell a falsehood</li><li>to marry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuganą</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leugāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, tell a falsehood</li><li>to marry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuganą</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leugāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lie (<i>accusative singular</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (liugan, “to lie”). The attested form is accusative singular and may have belonged to an identical neuter a-stem or a masculine a-stem <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;liugns).<i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) false prophet, pseudoprophet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌽</i> (liugn, “falsehood”) +&lrm; <i>𐍀𐍂𐌰𐌿𐍆𐌴𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (praufētus, “prophet”), calque of Koine Greek <i>ψευδοπροφήτης</i> (pseudoprophḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌽</i> (liugn, “lie”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (-waurds, “-speaking, -telling”) (see <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd)), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ψευδολόγος</i> (pseudológos). While adjectival in form (and while the suffix <i>-waurds</i> generally produces adjectives), this word is only attested in substantivized form.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌽</i> (liugn, “falsehood”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leudaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌸</i> (liuhaþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌳𐌰</i> (liuhada)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness, radiancy, illumination</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, luminous</li></ol><p>From a verb related to <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌸</i> (liuhaþ, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, noun-forming suffix).</p><p>From <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌸</i> (liuhaþ, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuhadą</i>. Related to <i>&#42;leuhtą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give light, shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;liuhtijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) song</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuþą</i> (“song”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌸𐍉𐌽</i> (liuþōn, “to sing”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leuþōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit, dishonesty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍄𐍃</i> (liuts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypocritical, dishonest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leutaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dear, beloved</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leubaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>lief</i>, Dutch <i>lief</i>, German <i>lieb</i>.</p></html>:/ˈlisan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather</li><li>to collect</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to know</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lesaną</i> (“to gather, collect”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lizaną</i>, following Kroonen. Or, per Lehmann, Köbler and others (see references), a Gothic innovation by analogy with the preterite-present verb <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunning, crafty, sly</li></ol><p><i>𐌻𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (lists) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cunning plan, a scheme, a plot</li><li>a wile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;listiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrisy, insincerity</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercessory prayer</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Ancient Greek <i>λιτή</i> (litḗ), <i>λιτανεία</i> (litaneía) or some closely related word. Contains the Gothic deverbal suffix <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), but semantically difficult to reconcile with <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;litjan) (found in <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸𐌻𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (miþlitjan)) meaning "feign, dissemble". <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (*lubān, possibly related to <i>&#42;lubō</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) skilled in witchcraft or poison-mongering</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌱𐌹</i> (&#42;lubi, “poison”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;leis, “knowledgeable, familiar, skilled”) (Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lubją</i> + <i>&#42;līsaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorcery, witchcraft, poison-mongering</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌿𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (lubjaleis, “skilled in witchcraft or poison-mongering”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). Translates and possibly calques Ancient Greek <i>φαρμακεία</i> (pharmakeía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face, countenance</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐍃</i> (lūkās)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp, light</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lucerna</i> (“lamp”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp stand, candlestick</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (lukarn) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌸𐌰</i> (&#42;staþa), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staþô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luke, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Λουκᾶς</i> (Loukâs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ransom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lunaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lust, desire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lustuz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈlustusama/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>longed for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lustusamaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌻𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (lustus) +&lrm; <i>-𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (-sama).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;transitive) to lust, desire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lustōną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌻𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (lustus) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lutōną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈluf.tus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air (the apparently open space above the ground)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luftuz</i> (“roof, sky, air”). Contains the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus). Cognate to Old English <i>lyft</i>&nbsp;f (“air, sky”) and Old Norse <i>lopt</i>&nbsp;n (“attic, air”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) palm of the hand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lōfô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virginity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (magaþs, “girl, virgin”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, maiden</li><li>virgin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;magaþs</i> (“girl, virgin, maiden”).</p></html>:/ˈmaɣan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be able, may, can</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;magʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;megʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(little) boy</li></ol><p>Diminutive from <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌿𐍃</i> (magus). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. gothic formation or pgmc?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maguz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerful, mighty</li><li>able</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtīgaz</i>. Morphologically, <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (mahts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs). Compare English <i>mighty</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, power, ability</li><li>a mighty deed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mahtiz</i> (“might”). Equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (magan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/ˈma.θa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maggot (larva that eats decomposing organic matter)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly, especially an assembly of the people (as opposed to a council, <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ))</li><li>the place of assembly</li><li>speech</li><li>market, marketplace</li><li>things sold at market, provisions, supplies</li><li>sale</li><li>the time of market: midday</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþlą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, discourse (<i>accusative singular</i>)</li></ol><p>The only attested form precludes a definite ascription of this word to a single lemma. There are two options:</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak publicly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maþlijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌻</i> (maþl) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to change, transform, corrupt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maidijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greater, more; comparative degree of <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maizô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍃</i> (mais) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍃</i> (mais) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mihstuz</i>. Contains the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift, present</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiþmaz</i>. Cognate with English <i>mathom</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) wrinkle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mailą</i>.</p></html>:/mɛmbrana/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) parchment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>membrāna</i>; according to Miller, via Koine Greek <i>μεμβράνα</i> (membrána).</p></html>:/mɛːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greatest, most; superlative degree of <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maistaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈmɛːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, hew</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;maitaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌺𐌹𐌳𐍉𐌽𐌾𐌰</i> (makidōnja)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedonia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μᾰκεδονῐ́ᾱ</i> (Măkedonĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Macedonian</li></ol><p>Possibly a back-formation, if it was initially just the nominative plural <i>𐌼𐌰𐌺𐌹𐌳𐍉𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (makidōneis) which was borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μᾰκεδόνες</i> (Măkedónes). Alternatively, it may have been borrowed via Latin <i>mācedoni</i>. Compare <i>𐍂𐌿𐌼𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (rumōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malmaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈma.loː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth (insect)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>𐌼𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (malan, “to grind”) with the sense of "grinding one", from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i>, in which lies also the origin of Old Norse <i>mǫlr</i> (“moth”) and Old High German <i>miliwa</i> (“mite”) (whence German <i>Milbe</i>).</p></html>:/ˈmamːoː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mammon</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>μαμωνᾶς</i> (mamōnâs), from Aramaic <i>מָמוֹנָא</i> (māmōnā).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance, plenty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌳𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (-dūþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude, especially of people</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;managį̄</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more (numerous), additional; comparative degree of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>most; superlative degree of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become abundant, to abound</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags, “much, many”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to multiply, increase</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many</li><li>much (a large amount of)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;managaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manifold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;managafalþaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (manags, “many”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (-falþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manslayer, homicide, murderer</li></ol><p>From the combining form of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna, “man, human”) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maurþrjan, “to murder”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”). It is likely that a noun <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍂𐌾𐌰</i> (&#42;maurþrja) (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murþrijô</i>) existed on which this word was based (making the derivation <i>manna</i> + <i>*maurþrja</i>), but this cannot be verified.</p></html>:/ˈma.nɔː.li/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) human-image, human-figure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manauliją</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌴𐌸</i> (manasēþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (manasēþs)</li></ol></html>:/ˈma.naˌseːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the world (human collective existence)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna, “man”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;sēþs, “seed”), literally “seed of man”. For the semantics, one can compare Latin <i>saeculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌹</i> (manasēdai)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (manasēþs)</li></ol></html>:/ˈman.liː.ka/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an image, likeness (of a person); a portrait</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manalīkô</i>. Equivalent to the combining form of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a). A translation of the New Testament Greek <i>εἰκὼν</i> (eikṑn, “image”).</p></html>:/ˈman.na/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li><li>human, person</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mann-</i>; cognate to Old English <i>mann</i> (English <i>man</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anyone</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna) +&lrm; <i>-𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (-hun).</p></html>:/ˈma.nːisks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manniskaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks). Cognate with Old English <i>mennisċ</i>, Old Frisian <i>menniska</i>, <i>manniska</i>, Old Saxon <i>mennisk</i>, <i>mennisko</i>, Old High German <i>menniska</i>, <i>mennisk</i>, Old Norse <i>mennskr</i>, <i>mennska</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity, human nature</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (mannisks) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (-ōdus), which suffix is ultimately from <i>&#42;-ōþuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparedness, readiness, preparation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (manwus, “prepared”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (manwus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>preparedly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (manwus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready, prepared</li></ol></html>:/ˈmɔːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to remind</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Connected by Pokorny with Ancient Greek <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos, “speech, story”) and Old Church Slavonic <i>мꙑсль</i> (myslĭ, “thought”), from a possible Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mewHdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murginaz</i> (“morning”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murþrą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈmɔr.θri.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murder, kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murþrijaną</i> (“to murder”).</p><p>A prefixed form of this word <i>may</i> be attested in the <i>Gothica Bononiensia</i> fragment, which has <i>gamaur</i> (folio 1 recto, line 19) with the ending of the verb lacking due to a lacuna. Given the existence of this verb with <i>-r-</i>, this fragment may point to the existence of <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gamaurþrjan). The narrowness of the lacuna however seems to favor a shorter form, perhaps from <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gamaurþjan), corresponding to a simplex verb <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;maurþjan) from <i>&#42;murþijaną</i>, which otherwise has no known reflexes (prefixed or otherwise) in Gothic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mourn, be anxious</li><li>take care for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;murnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽 𐌰𐌸𐌰</i> (maran aþa)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>maranatha</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>μαράνα θά</i> (marána thá) or <i>μαράν αθά</i> (marán athá), from Aramaic <i>מרנא תא</i> (“Lord, come!”) or <i>מרן אתא</i> (“our lord has come”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mari</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;móri</i>. Compare English <i>mere</i>, German <i>Meer</i>, Dutch <i>meer</i>, Old Norse <i>marr</i>, Latin <i>mare</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stumbling-block, impediment, hindrance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (marzjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impede, hamper, hinder, impair</li><li>to disturb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;marzijaną</i>, whence English <i>mar</i>, Dutch <i>marren</i>, among others.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Martha, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μάρθα</i> (Mártha), from Aramaic <i>מַרְתָּא</i> (martā).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌰</i> (marja)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) pearl</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>μαργαρίτης</i> (margarítēs), possibly via Latin. Apparently modified by folk-etymological association with <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (marei, “sea”); although the second element <i>-kreitus</i> is less readily explained, its devoicing may result from the same process that yielded <i>𐌺𐍂𐌴𐌺𐍃</i> (krēks, “Greek”) (from Latin <i>graecus</i>) with its devoiced initial consonant.</p><p>The sole attested form, <i>marikreitum</i>, has been surmised to be a misspelling of <i>*marikreitōm</i>, dative plural to a feminine noun <i>*marikreita</i>, which would support a borrowing from Latin <i>margarīta</i> instead a direct derivation from the Greek form. This, however, remains uncertain.</p><p>Compare Old High German <i>merigrioz</i>, Old Saxon <i>merigriota</i>, <i>merigrīta</i>, Old English <i>meregrot</i>, <i>meregrēot</i>, all meaning "pearl" and folk-etymologically adapted to their languages' respective reflexes of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mari</i> (“sea”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (marei, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍅𐍃</i> (saiws, “lake, sea”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, region, coast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;markō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marcellus of Ancyra, a fourth-century bishop.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Marcellus</i>, possibly misspelled due to the influence of the <i>-ius</i> in <i>𐍃𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (sabaillius), which is attested directly adjacent to this name in the <i>Skeireins</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mark, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μάρκος</i> (Márkos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mary, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μαρία</i> (María), from Aramaic <i>מרים</i> (maryam).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) martyr</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>martyr</i> (per Lehmann) or directly from Ancient Greek <i>μάρτυρ</i> (mártur).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐍄𐍅𐍂𐌴</i> (martwrē)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Matthew, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ματθαῖος</i> (Matthaîos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food bag, knapsack, scrip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;matibalgiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌰𐍄𐍃</i> (mats, “food”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌲𐍃</i> (balgs, “bag”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, have a meal, dine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;matjaną</i>. Cognate with Vandalic <i>matzia</i> (“to eat”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;matiz</i>. Cognate with English <i>meat</i>, Danish <i>mad</i>, and Swedish <i>mat</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, lass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mawī</i> (“girl”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(little) girl</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mawilǭ</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌰𐍅𐌹</i> (mawi) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌻𐍉</i> (-ilō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēgaz</i> (“kin”).</p><p>Cognate to Old English <i>mǣġ</i> and Old Norse <i>mágr</i>.</p></html>:/miːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>my, mine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mīnaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈmeːkiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sword</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēkijaz</i>&nbsp;m. Cognate with Old English <i>mēċe</i> and Old Norse <i>mækir</i>. Koine Greek <i>μάχαιρα</i> (mákhaira), which it translates, is a false cognate.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>a point in time, <i>especially</i> an appointed or fixed time.</li><li>a long time</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) writing, scripture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēlą</i>. In the Gothic Bible translated by Ulfilas, the Greek word χρόνος (khrónos), meaning “time” or “duration,” is rendered as 𐌼𐌴𐌻 (mēl). This Gothic term carries the sense of a fixed period or appointed time.</p><p>By contrast, in the Latin Vulgate, Jerome consistently translates <i>χρόνος</i> as tempus, the standard Latin word for “time.”</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a measure of grain, perhaps equivalent to the Roman modius</li></ol></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>a point in time, <i>especially</i> an appointed or fixed time.</li><li>a long time</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) writing, scripture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēlą</i>. In the Gothic Bible translated by Ulfilas, the Greek word χρόνος (khrónos), meaning “time” or “duration,” is rendered as 𐌼𐌴𐌻 (mēl). This Gothic term carries the sense of a fixed period or appointed time.</p><p>By contrast, in the Latin Vulgate, Jerome consistently translates <i>χρόνος</i> as tempus, the standard Latin word for “time.”</p></html>:/ˈmeːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēlijaną</i> (“to mark”).</p></html>:/ˈmeː.na/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the Moon (sole natural satellite of the Earth)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēnô</i>&nbsp;m, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗh₁n̥s</i>. Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>mine</i>, Old English <i>mōna</i>&nbsp;m and Old Norse <i>máni</i>&nbsp;m.</p></html>:/ˈmeː.noːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>month (period into which a year is divided)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēnōþs</i> (“month”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proclamation</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) preaching</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mērjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame, renown</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēriþō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐌼𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;mērs, “famous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html>:/ˈmeː.ri.la/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌼𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;mērs, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (-ila).</p></html>:/meːr.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proclaim</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to preach, evangelize</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mērijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) preacher</li></ol><p>Substantivized form of <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (mērjands), present participle of <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mērjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>dish</li><li>vessel, trough</li></ol><p>From Vulgar Latin <i>mēsa</i>, from Latin <i>mēnsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a middle wall, a fence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;waddjus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deluge, cataclysmic flood</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis, “middle”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (*sweipān, “to sweep[?]”) (from a Germanic derivate of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ksweybʰ-</i>) + <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈmið.jis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i>. The <i>-jis</i> in the nominative singular results from morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the expected outcome of <i>*-jaz</i> in Gothic following a consonant would otherwise have been <i>*-is</i>. Other examples of this development include <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (harjis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis) and <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis).</p></html>:/ˈmid.junˌɡards/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inhabited world, ecumene</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midjagardaz</i> (“Middle Earth”). Akin to Old English <i>middangeard</i>, Old Saxon <i>middilgard</i>, Old High German <i>mittilgart</i>, Old Norse <i>Miðgarðr</i>. Not exactly equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis, “middle”) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards, “yard”); the initial element is possibly rather a variant of <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (miduma) or a suffixed form of <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle, midst</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;medumō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mediate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;medumōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward, recompense, payment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mizdō</i>. Cognate with English <i>meed</i>.</p></html>:/miθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) together with</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>along, together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midi</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix indicating that the prefixed action is performed together, similar to co-. Verbs with this prefix frequently govern the dative case.</li></ol><p>See <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸</i> (miþ, “with”). Compare German <i>mit-</i>.</p></html>:/miθanaˈkumbjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recline at a table for a meal together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌼𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (anakumbjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toil or struggle together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (arbaidjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or come along</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die together (with)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (gadauþnan).</p></html>:/miθɡaˈkʷiu̯jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken together</li></ol><p><i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaqiujan)</p></html>:/miθɡaˈliːkoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to imitate or follow together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉𐌽</i> (galeikōn). The substantivized present participle of this word (its only attestation) is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>συμμιμητής</i> (summimētḗs, “fellow imitator”).</p></html>:/miθɡaˈsatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make sit together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasatjan).</p></html>:/miθɡaˈswiltan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to die with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (gaswiltan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to build up together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gatimrjan).</p></html>:/miθɡaˈtiu̯.han/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to carry away with (take possession of the mind)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (gatiuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to abide together with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (gawisan, “remain, abide”).</p></html>:/miθˈkwiθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be in conformity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;with dative) to come along</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman). Compare Dutch <i>meekomen</i>, German <i>mitkommen</i>.</p></html>:/ˈmiθθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>meanwhile</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸</i> (miþ) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (miþþanei, “while, as”)</li></ol></html>:/miθˈθaniː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>while, as</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (miþþan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to reign alongside</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (þiudanōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter together (with someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (inngaleiþan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>συνεισέρχεσθαι</i> (suneisérkhesthai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to send someone along as a companion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (insandjan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>συναποστέλλειν</i> (sunapostéllein).</p></html>:/miθˈkɔːrjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to burden together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kaurjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to live with someone or something</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (liban, “to live”).</p></html>:/miθˈlitjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to feign or dissemble with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;litjan, “to feign, dissemble”), from <i>𐌻𐌹𐍄𐌰</i> (lita) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (matjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to receive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman). Compare Dutch <i>meenemen</i>, German <i>mitnehmen</i>.</p></html>:/miθurˈrɛːsjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to raise up together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍂𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (urraisjan).</p></html>:/miθurˈriːsan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be risen with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍂𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (urreisan).</p></html>:/miθusˈhramjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crucify with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ushramjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprout up with, spring up with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (uskeinan).</p></html>:/miθˈroːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to talk with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (rōdjan)</p></html>:/miθˈsatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to move, shift, remove</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan).</p></html>:/miθˈskalkinoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to serve with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (skalkinōn)</p></html>:/miθˈstandan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to stand with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html>:/miθˈsoːkjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to question with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sōkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) to testify together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (weitwōdjan).</p></html>:/miθˈwisan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be with, stand with</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awareness, conscience</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;wissei), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wissį̄</i>.</p></html>:/miθˈwitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, be conscious of, be aware of</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan); a calque of Ancient Greek <i>σύνοιδα</i> (súnoida).</p></html>:/miθˈfaɡinoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice with, rejoice in [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (faginōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to capture together</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌸-</i> (miþ-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (frahinþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greatly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnitude, greatness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils) +&lrm; <i>-𐌳𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (-dūþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness, majesty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, of great reputation or thinking oneself to be great</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils, “large, great”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (*þūhts, “seeming [to be]”), which is related to <i>𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þugkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become large or great</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils, “large, great”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make large, enlarge, magnify</li><li>to praise, glorify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mikiljaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (mikils, “large, great”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, suffix forming factitive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>large, great, big</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mikilaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meǵh₂los</i>, from <i>&#42;meǵh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compassion, mildness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mildiþō</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;mildeis, “mild”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html>:/ˈmilh.ma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud (visible mass of water droplets suspended in the air)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;melhmô</i>. Compare Swedish <i>moln</i>.</p></html>:/ˈmi.liθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) honey (sweet substance produced by bees)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mili</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mélit</i>. Cognate to and translation of Koine Greek <i>μέλι</i> (méli).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to serve in the (Roman) army</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mīlitō</i> (“to serve in the army”). Attested in Wulfila's Bible translation dating to the third quarter of the fourth century, it was according to Dennis Green likely borrowed sometime from the early third century onwards (when contact between Goths and Romans intensified due to the Goths reaching the Dacian frontier in their migrations) to denote the semantic difference between Gothic-style warfare (for which there existed a native word <i>𐌳𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (drauhtinōn)) and the kind of army service Goths would have experienced (as auxiliaries or otherwise) in the Roman army.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;meluks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂melǵ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) meat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mimzą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗms</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, decrease</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (mins, “less, fewer”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smaller, less; comparative degree of <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (leitils)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;minnizô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smallest, least; superlative degree of <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (leitils)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;minnistaz</i>.</p></html>:/mins/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>less, fewer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;minniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>mis-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missa-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌰𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (missadēþs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misdeed, transgression</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missadēdiz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌰-</i> (missa-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;dēþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissidence, discord, dissension, division</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌰-</i> (missa-, “mis-”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diverse, various</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;missalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌰-</i> (missa-) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html>:/missaˈtɔːjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transgress</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌰-</i> (missa-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taujan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reciprocally, the one the other, one to another</li></ol></html>:/ˈmitadjoː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (mitaþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐍉</i> (-jō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (mitaþs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;metaþs</i>, a t-stem derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure”).</p></html>:/ˈmitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure, gauge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;metaną</i> (“to measure”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fathom, deliberate, consider, think over</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;metōną</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, opinion, deliberation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (mitōn, “to consider, think over”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, opinion, deliberation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (mitōn, “to consider, think over”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meekness, gentleness</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌼𐌿𐌺𐍃</i> (*mūks) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (mōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). The first element is otherwise unattested in Gothic, but derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mūkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust, dirt, soil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;muldō</i> (“dirt, soil”). Related to Dutch <i>moude</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthy, made of dust</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐌿𐌻𐌳𐌰</i> (mulda, “dust”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html>:/ˈmunan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reckon, to suppose</li><li>to think, to consider</li><li>to intend, be about to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;munaną</i>.</p><p>Possibly a verbalization of 𐌼𐌿𐌽𐍃 (muns, “intention, purpose”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goal, aim, objective</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mundrį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;mundrs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay attention, observe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mundōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;munþaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ment-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparedness, readiness</li><li>purpose, intention</li><li>preparation, provision, precaution</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;muniz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, mind”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) angry</li><li>wrathful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌼𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (mōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mood, temper</li><li>(<i>especially</i>) anger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌼𐍉𐍃𐌴𐍃</i> (mōsēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moses, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Μωσῆς</i> (Mōsês), from Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōše).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax, tribute, customs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōtō</i> (“tax, toll”), of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publican, tax collector, customs officer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐍉𐍄𐌰</i> (mōta, “tax, customs”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax booth, publican's office</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌼𐍉𐍄𐌰</i> (mōta, “tax”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (staþs, “place”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nebuchadnezzar</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ναβουχοδονόσωρ</i> (Naboukhodonósōr), from Biblical Hebrew <i>נְבוּכַדְנֶאצַּר</i> (nəḇûḵaḏneʾṣṣár), a corruption of earlier <i>נְבוּכַדְרֶאצַּר</i> (<i>nəḇûḵaḏreʾṣṣár</i>), from Akkadian <i>𒀭𒀝𒃻𒁺𒋀</i> (/⁠Nabû-kudurri-uṣur⁠/).</p></html>:/ˈnadrs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) viper, adder (venomous snake)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nadraz</i>. Compare Old English <i>nǣdre</i>, whence English <i>adder</i>. Formed to the same root as Latin <i>natrix</i> (“water snake”).</p></html>:/ˈna.kʷa.ðiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nudity, nakedness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nakwadį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌽𐌰𐌵𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (naqaþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈna.kʷaθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naked, nude</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nakwadaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazareth (the largest city in the modern Northern District, Israel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ναζαρέθ</i> (Nazaréth), probably from the Hebrew <i>נָצְרַת</i> (Natz'rat).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Nahshon, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ναασσών</i> (Naassṓn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>נַחְשׁוֹן</i> (Naḥšon).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supper</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (nahts) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍄𐍃</i> (mats). Compare Old English <i>ǣfenmete</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nahts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to grudge</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>While it is evidently a compound with second element <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (swaran, “to swear”), the origin of the first element 𐌽𐌰𐌹- is obscure:</p><p>See Lehmann 1986: 265; 332-333 for a more complete overview.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) blasphemy</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;naitjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). The verb (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naitijaną</i>) is attested in prefixed form as <i>𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ganaitjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to name, call, designate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;namnijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌽𐌰𐌼𐍉</i> (namō, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌽𐌰𐌼</i> (nam) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈna.moː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name (word or phrase indicating a particular person, place, class or thing)</li><li>name (authority)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;namô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁nómn̥</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>nama</i> and Old Norse <i>nafn</i>, and Koine Greek <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma), which it translates.</p></html>:/nɔ.βɛm.bɛr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The month November.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>November</i>. Attested (with difficulty: the manuscript is a palimpsest) in the 5th/6th century AD Gothic Calendar. The existence of this word is contested due to its relative illegibility by Landau (2006); cf. the discussion at <i>𐌾𐌹𐌿𐌻𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (jiuleis), the only other known possibly-attested month in Gothic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shackle, manacle, chain, fetter</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (nauþs) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌹</i> (bandi).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necessity, need</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (nauþs) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (þaurfts); possibly from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naudiþurftiz</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>nauðþurft</i>, German <i>Notdurft</i>, Dutch <i>nooddruft</i>, Old Saxon <i>nōdthurft</i>, Old Frisian <i>nēdthreft</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noah, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Νῶε</i> (Nôe), with added -l- likely by analogy with Hebrew names such as <i>Daniel</i> ending in <i>אֵל</i> (el, “god”), from Biblical Hebrew <i>נֹחַ</i> (Nōaḥ).</p></html>:/nɔh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yet, still</li><li>(<i>with a negative</i>) not yet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nuh</i>. Cognate with German <i>noch</i>.</p></html>:/ˈnɔhθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>still, yet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nuhþan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌷</i> (nauh) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan). Compare Old High German <i>noh danne</i> (“yet”), Old Saxon <i>nohthan</i> (“yet”), Dutch <i>nochtans</i> (“nevertheless”) and the similarly formed German <i>dennoch</i> (“anyhow”).</p></html>:/ˈnɔhθanuh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>still, yet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (nauhþan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naudijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (nauþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, compulsion, duress</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;naudiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead person, corpse, cadaver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nawiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈnar.dus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) nard (<i>Nardostachys jatamansi</i>)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>νάρδος</i> (nárdos) (according to Lehmann (1986) by way of Latin <i>nardus</i>), from Phoenician (compare Hebrew <i>נֵרְדְּ</i> (nērd), Aramaic <i>נִרְדָּא</i> (nirdā) and Akkadian <i>𒆠𒆗</i> (lardu [HIRIM])), from Sanskrit <i>नलद</i> (nálada).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nazīniz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (nasjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nazjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saviour (substantivized from the above participle)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Messiah</li></ol><p>Substantivized form of <i>𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (nasjands), present participle of <i>𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (nasjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;natją</i>. Cognates include English <i>net</i>, Dutch <i>net</i>, Old Norse <i>net</i>, West Frisian <i>net</i>, German <i>Netz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make wet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;natjaną</i>; equivalent to <i>&#42;𐌽𐌰𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;nats, “wet”, from <i>&#42;nataz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, suffix forming factitive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>no (negation particle)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nē</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. cognates?</i></p></html>:/ˈneːθ.la/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing needle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēþlō</i>, derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₁-</i> (“to sew”). Cognates include Old English <i>nǣdl</i>, Old High German <i>nādala</i>, Old Saxon <i>nāthla</i> and Old Norse <i>nál</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nī</i>, earlier <i>*nei</i>. For the formation compare <i>𐌾𐌰</i> (ja) and <i>𐌾𐌰𐌹</i> (jai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hate, envy, malice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nīþą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈniːwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to grudge</li></ol><p>If not a ghost word, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nīwaną</i> (“to grudge”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌽𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌴𐌼𐌿𐍃</i> (nikaudēmus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near, close</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhw</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) close to, near</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearer, closer; comparative degree of <i>𐌽𐌴𐍈</i> (nēƕ)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈneːʍjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēhwijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌽𐌴𐍈</i> (nēƕ, “near, close”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbour</li></ol><p>From a form belonging or related to <i>𐌽𐌴𐍈𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (nēƕjan, “approach”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent noun”), although the exact derivation is unclear. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. simply nehwan + -ja would yield *nehwja or the like; the -und- wants explanation</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ne</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;né</i> (“not”).</p></html>:/ni θeː ˈhaldis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nevertheless</li></ol><p>Literally, “not the more”.</p></html>:/ˈniba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌽𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (nibai, “if not, though not”)</li></ol></html>:/ˈnibɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if…not, though…not, unless, except</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (ibai)</p></html>:/ˈnid.wa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rust, corrosion (the act of corroding)</li></ol><p>Contested; Lehmann lists several possibilities. Possibly to be connected with the first element of Old Norse <i>niðfǫlr</i>, in which case the Old Norse word might mean "rust-yellow". Perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nidwō</i> (“going down, sinking, downfall”), or from a compound involving the same first element as Old English <i>neowol</i>, or perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ney-</i> (“to be aroused, shine”) (whence latin Latin <i>niteō</i> (“to glitter, shine”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and not, neither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nehw</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nekʷe</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help, assist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;neþaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nét-e-</i>, related to Proto-Tocharian <i>&#42;nete</i> (“might, strength”). Perhaps related to <i>&#42;niþjaz</i> (“relative”). Compare German <i>Gnade</i>, Dutch <i>genade</i>.</p></html>:/ˈniθθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and not, neither</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌹𐌷</i> (nih) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinsman, a male relative</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nítyos</i>; compare Sanskrit <i>नित्य</i> (nitya, “own, innate, native”). The <i>-jis</i> in the nominative singular results from morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the expected outcome of <i>*-jaz</i> in Gothic following a consonant would otherwise have been <i>*-is</i>. Other examples of this development include <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (harjis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis), <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis) and <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kinswoman, a female relative</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niþjǭ</i>, from the same source as <i>&#42;niþjaz</i> (whence <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nicodemus, a Pharisee in the New Testament</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Νῑκόδημος</i> (Nīkódēmos).</p></html>:/ˈniman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take, accept, receive, assume</li><li>to catch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nemaną</i> (“to take”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nem-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼</i> (nim) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not…?</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni) + <i>-𐌿</i> (-u).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visitation, inspection</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;niuhsjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). The verb is attested in prefixed form as <i>𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (biniuhsjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childishness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌺𐌻𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (niuklahs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childish</li></ol><p>Contains the suffix <i>-𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (-ahs). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈniu̯n/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newun</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i>. Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>nyne</i> and Old Norse <i>níu</i>, and Koine Greek <i>ἐννέᾰ</i> (ennéă), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈniu̯n ˈhun.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) nine hundred (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “nine hundreds”. Compare Old English <i>nigon hund</i>.</p></html>:/ˈniu̯n.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the ninth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newundô</i>. Cognate to Old Norse <i>níundi</i>, and close in formation to Koine Greek <i>ἔνατος</i> (énatos), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈniu̯n.teː.hund/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ninety (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;newuntēhundą</i> of obscure formation. Akin in some way to Old English <i>hundnigontiġ</i>, Old Saxon <i>antniguntig</i> and Old High German <i>niunzog</i>.</p></html>:/ˈniu̯n.teː.hund jah ˈniu̯n/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety-nine (cardinal number)</li></ol></html>:/ˈniu̯jasatiθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) novice</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (satiþs). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>νεόφῡτος</i> (neóphūtos).</p></html>:/ˈniu̯.ji.θa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niujis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>new</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;niwjaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néwyos</i>. The <i>-jis</i> in the nominative singular results from morphological levelling with the oblique forms, as the expected outcome of <i>*-jaz</i> in Gothic following a consonant would otherwise have been <i>*-is</i>. Other examples of this development include <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (harjis), <i>𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (niþjis), <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (aljis) and <i>𐌼𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (midjis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to reach, obtain</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to enjoy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;neutaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈnist/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>isn't, contraction of <i>𐌽𐌹</i> (ni) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐍃𐍄</i> (ist)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and so, so then, consequently</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>then, therefore</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌽𐌿</i> (nu) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html>:/ˈnu.nu/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>now then, therefore, consequently</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>𐌽𐌿</i> (nu, “now”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who catches;</li><li>fisherman</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of <i>𐌽𐌹𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (niutan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) stern, poop</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>yes (confirmation particle)</li><li>thus, so</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ja</i>.</p></html>:/ˈja.βɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>expressing a factual condition, with indicative verbs</li><li>expressing a potential or hypothetical condition, with a present subjunctive in the protasis, and with an imperative or subjunctive in the apodosis</li><li>expressing a counterfactual condition, with past subjunctives in both protasis and apodosis</li></ol><li>whether</li><li>either</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jabai</i>. Doublet of <i>𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (ibai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌰𐌴𐌹𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (iaeirus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and</li><li>also</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jahw</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌾𐌰</i> (ja) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌾𐌰𐌸𐌸𐌴</i> (jaþþē, “whether”)</li></ol></html>:/ˈjaθθeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if, and if</li><li>whether</li><li>(<i>when doubled</i>) whether…or</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌰𐌷</i> (jah) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌴</i> (þē).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>yes, yea</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jai</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>over there, yonder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jainar</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thither, to there</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jend-</i>, <i>&#42;jand-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;i-</i> (“a pronomial stem”). Akin to Old English <i>ġeond</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thither, to there</li></ol><p>Akin to English <i>yonder</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thither-turned; turned towards that place; thitherward</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌳</i> (jaind) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (-wairþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from there</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (jains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html>:/jɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that over there, yon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jainaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>yon</i> and German <i>jener</i>.</p></html>:/jɔː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>whether</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌰</i> (ja) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿</i> (-u).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li><li>a long time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jērą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) yoked animal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic. Compare Old English <i>geoht</i>, Old High German <i>kiioht</i>. It is here assumed that the gender of the Gothic word was identical to that of the Old English, i.e. neuter, but the sole attested form <i>𐌾𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌰</i> (jiuhta) (a dative singular, found in the <i>Gothica Bononiensia</i>) may as well point to a masculine a-stem lemma <i>&#42;𐌾𐌹𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;jiuhts).</p><p>The word is related to <i>𐌾𐌿𐌺</i> (juk, “yoke”), with the <i>-h-</i> in this word being the result of Primärberührung.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrath</li><li>strife, quarrel, argument</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌾𐌹𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (jiukan, “to fight”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, quarrel, argue</li><li>to overcome</li></ol></html>:/ˈjiu̯.liːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) "Yulemonth" (possibly November, December, or both)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jiulijaz</i> (“Yulemonth”), a suffix-stressed <i>-ja</i>-extension of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jehwlą</i> (“Yule”) (by way of Verner's law) and cognate to Old Norse <i>ýlir</i> (“month lasting from late November to late December”) and possibly Old English <i>giuli</i> (“December and January”) (Schaffner 2001, Lehmann 1986).</p></html>:/ju/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>already; yet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ju</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (juggs) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;lauþs, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laudaz</i> (“man”)).</p></html>:/juŋɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jungaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂yuHn̥ḱós</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (iudaius)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Jewish</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (judaius), <i>𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (iudaius, “Jew”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks, suffix forming adjectives), calque of Koine Greek <i>ἰουδαικός</i> (ioudaikós).</p></html>:/ˈjuːziː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>who (<i>with the second-person plural pronoun as antecedent</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (jūs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>younger; comparative degree of <i>𐌾𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍃</i> (juggs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;junhizô</i>, comparative of <i>&#42;jungaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>ǿri</i>.</p></html>:/ˈjuθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now, by now, already</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌾𐌿</i> (ju) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (þan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) yoked pair of animals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;juką</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yugóm</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke, burden</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌾𐌿𐌺</i> (juk). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth (quality or state of being young)</li></ol><p>Uncertain; the sole attested form, <i>jundai</i> can point to multiple possible nominative singular forms, each with a different etymology.</p></html>:/juːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you, ye (second-person plural personal pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jūz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yúHs</i>.</p></html>:/ˈjut/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you two, the two of you (second-person dual personal pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;jut</i>. Akin to Old English <i>ġit</i> and Old Norse <i>it</i> (whence Icelandic <i>þið</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iota, jot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἰῶτα</i> (iôta), ultimately from a Semitic source.</p></html>:/ˈu.βi.zwa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porch, portico</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubiswō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly, evilly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (ubils) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad, evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubilaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈubiltoːjis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malefactor, evildoer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (ubils) +&lrm; <i>-𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (-tōjis)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak evil, malign</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌻𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (ubilwaurds, “evil-speaking, maligning”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, “verbal suffix”). According to Köbler, a calque of Ancient Greek <i>κακολογεῖν</i> (kakologeîn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abusive, maligning, railing, speaking ill</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (ubils, “evil”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (-waurds) (see <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd, “word”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌹𐍃</i> (ugkis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) (<i>when prefixed to a word with initial vowel</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;anan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ananą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzētô</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (itan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌿𐍃</i> (us) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐍉𐍆</i> (ushōf) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌹𐌳𐌳𐌾𐌰</i> (usiddja) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at leisure</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (*ūhts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs); the former derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>seasonably, opportunely</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (ūhteigs, “at leisure”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, suffix forming adverbs from adjectives).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) dawn, daybreak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhtwǭ</i>.</p></html>:/ˈul.ban.dus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camel (animal)</li></ol><p>The exact derivation is uncertain.</p><p>Cognate with Old Norse <i>úlfaldi</i>, Old English <i>olfend</i>, <i>olfenda</i>, Old High German <i>olbento</i>, Old Saxon <i>olbundeo</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>un-; negation prefix.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;un-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unagandz</i>, from <i>&#42;un-</i> + the present participle of <i>&#42;aganą</i>. Contrast the attested Gothic present participle <i>𐍉𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (ōgands).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fearlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃</i> (agis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unholy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (airkns, “holy”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) without shame</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;aiwisks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>invisibly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (anasiuns, “visible”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, adverb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) not uncovered, unrevealed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (andhuliþs), the past participle of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (andhuljan). Compare Dutch <i>ononthuld</i> (“unrevealed”), German <i>unenthüllt</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) undisputed, unopposed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (andsakans), the past participle of <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (andsakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) indisputable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) + a word related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (andsakan, “to dispute”) of uncertain derivation. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (bairands), the present participle of <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unborn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;baurans), the past participle of <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌽</i> (barn, “child”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (-ahs, “-ic, -ish, -y”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unleavened bread</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐍄</i> (beist) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unleavened</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;beistjōþs, “leavened”), formed as if it were the past participle of a hypothetical verb <i>&#42;𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;beistjōn, “to leaven”), from <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐍄</i> (beist, “yeast”).</p></html>:/ˈunˌbi.a.ri/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;derogatory) beast (violent&#47;antisocial person)</li></ol><p>Ultimately unknown, though the first element appears to be <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unsearchable; inscrutable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;bilaistiþs), past participle of an unattested verb <i>&#42;𐌱𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;bilaistjan, “to seek out, examine, search”), from <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laistjan). Likely calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀνεξιχνίαστος</i> (anexikhníastos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) uncircumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄</i> (bimait).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) uncircumcised</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (bimaitans).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless, unprofitable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌺𐍃</i> (brūks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to be incontinent or unrestrained</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (gahabān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) lack of self-control; acrasia</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐍉𐌱𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (gahōbains), likely a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀκρασία</i> (akrasía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfit, reprobate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌺𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (gakusans).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disbelief, faithlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (galaubeins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distrust</li><li>to disbelieve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌱𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (galaubjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of little value</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (galaufs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) unrighteousness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹</i> (garaihtei), possibly a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀνομία</i> (anomía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseen, invisible</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (gasaiƕans, “seen”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) homeless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;gastōþs), from which <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (gastōþān) is also derived. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrestrained</li><li>disorganized, unruly</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;tass). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Where is *tass from?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>untidily, unrulily, disorderly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (ungatass) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) disorderly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌴𐍅𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (gatēwiþs), the past participle of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌴𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gatēwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmoved, unshaken</li><li>immobile</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (gawagiþs), the past participle of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gawagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreproachable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;gafairinōþs), past participle of <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;gafairinōn), unattested perfective counterpart with the prefix <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) to the attested verb <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (fairinōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irreproachable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;gafairinōnds), present participle of <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;gafairinōn), unattested perfective counterpart with the prefix <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) to the attested verb <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (fairinōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disobedient, unruly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍈𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐍃</i> (gaƕairbs, “amenable, persuadable”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) unto, until, up to</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;und</i> (“until”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a midday meal, noonmeat; lunch</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;undaurns) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍄𐍃</i> (mats); the first element is from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;undurniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) under, beneath (stationary)</li><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) under (direction)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>under, beneath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;under</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lowest, undermost</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (undar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the lowest or basest man</li></ol><p>Substantivized from the weak masculine nominative singular of an adjective <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂𐌻𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;undarleis, “lowest”), of which the first element is <i>𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (undar). According to Pokorny, the second element is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₃l-</i> (“to bow”), see also Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;olīnā</i> (“angle”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) under</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;underô</i>.</p></html>:/undɡriːpan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) seize, grasp, lay hold of</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (und) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (greipan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immortality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (diwan, “to die”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide, grant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (und) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rēdan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to allot, grant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (und) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unsayable, unspeakable, that which cannot be said</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;qēþs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēdiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wifeless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌴𐌽𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;qēniþs), which is formed as if the past participle of a verb <i>&#42;𐌵𐌴𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;qēnjan, “to marry”), from <i>𐌵𐌴𐌽𐍃</i> (qēns, “wife”). The word thus translates roughly to 'unwived'.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacking, not having</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (habānds).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illness, weakness, infirmity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (unhails) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwell, sick</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (hails).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not made by hand</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌿𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (handuwaurhts).</p></html>:/ˈunhindarwiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sincere, unpretending</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍂𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (hindarweis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil, demon, evil spirit, monster</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhulþô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-devil, evil spirit</li></ol><p>Feminine equivalent of <i>𐌿𐌽𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌸𐌰</i> (unhulþa). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) admitting of no truce or conciliation; implacable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (&#42;hunslags, “pertaining to sacrifice; conciliatory?”), from <i>𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌻</i> (hunsl, “sacrifice, offering”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags). Possible calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἄσπονδος</i> (áspondos), which this word translates and which was derived similarly, linking truce and conciliation to religious offerings.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncleanness, impurity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (unhrains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impurity, uncleanness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (unhrains, “impure, unclean”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unclean</li><li>(<i>of speech</i>) vulgar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unhrainiz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (hrains).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escape</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unþafleuhaną</i>; compare unprefixed <i>𐌸𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þliuhan). The prefix <i>unþa-</i> does not occur in any other attested Gothic word, and, seeing as this verb is inherited, there is no indication of its productivity in Gothic (although the corpus is too small to be certain there did not exist other words with the same prefix, which might include non-inherited Gothic formations that could indicate the prefix' continued productivity).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-people, non-nation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda, “nation, people”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (þiuþ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸</i> (unþiuþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan) or <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þiuþjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwashed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;þwahans, “washed”), past participle of <i>𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þwahan, “to wash”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unburdensomeness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;kaureins), from <i>𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (kaurus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careless, negligent</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌰</i> (&#42;karja), from <i>𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌰</i> (kara).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an uncia (unit of area measure equivalent to roughly 2,400 square ft.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>uncia</i>. As the term is only attested in a sixth-century document from Ostrogothic Italy, it may well have been a late borrowing, but there is no way to confirm this (as the absence of evidence is not evidence of absence).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (unkunþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unkunþaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (kunþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknowing, ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (kunnands).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐌻𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (unlēþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐌽𐌻𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (unlēþs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unlēdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unmarried</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (liugaiþs), the past participle of <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (liugān, “to marry”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unlying</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (liugands), present participle of <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (liugan, “to lie”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sincere, honest, unhypocritical</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍄𐍃</i> (liuts).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unloved</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (liufs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apathy, listlessness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unlustuz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (lustus, “desire, lust”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak</li><li>unable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtīgaz</i>; cognate with Old English <i>unmihtiġ</i>, Old High German <i>unmahtīg</i>, Dutch <i>onmachtig</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (mahteigs, “powerful, mighty”) or <i>𐌿𐌽𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (unmahts, “inability, weakness”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inability, incapacity</li><li>infirmity, weakness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unmahtiz</i> (“might”), whence English <i>unmight</i>, Dutch <i>onmacht</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (mahts).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) untamed, wild, savage</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐍃</i> (&#42;manariggws, “tamed”), from the combining form of <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰</i> (manna, “human”) + <i>&#42;𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐍃</i> (&#42;riggws), of uncertain origin. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐌽𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌰𐌹</i> (unmanariggwai)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unprepared, unready</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌿𐍃</i> (manwus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) not mild; unkind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;mildeis, “mild”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mildijaz</i>. Possibly a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἄστοργος</i> (ástorgos). Some sources instead give a lemma form <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;unmilds) (the attested form is ambiguous).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless, foolish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unnutiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unseasonably, inopportunely, untimely</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍉</i> (ūhteigō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneducated</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (uslaisiþs), the past participle of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (uslaisjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfathomable, indescribable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (usspillōþs), the past participle of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (usspillōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) not spurning or rebuffing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐌿𐍆𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (ufbrikands).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incorruption, imperishableness, immortality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (unriureis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incorruptible, imperishable, immortal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (riureis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspeaking, mute</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (rōdjands, “speaking”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>undisputably, incontrovertibly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;sahts, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sahtaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseeing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (saiƕands, “seeing”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsalted</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌻𐍄𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;saltans, “salted”), past participle of <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌻𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;saltan, “to salt”), from <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐍄</i> (salt, “salt”).</p></html>:/ˈunsar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>our (first-person plural possessive)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unseraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil, wickedness, iniquity</li></ol><p><i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌴𐌻𐍃</i> (unsēls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (unsēljins)</li></ol></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil, wickedness, iniquity</li></ol><p><i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐌴𐌻𐍃</i> (unsēls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil, wicked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unsēliz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌴𐌻𐍃</i> (sēls).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impious, lawless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;sibjis), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sibjaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumult, rioting</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌿𐍄𐌹</i> (&#42;suti). The latter element is of unclear origin. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to continue, not cease</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (sweiban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dishonour, denigrate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-, dis-”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (swērān, “to honour”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishonour</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (unswērs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishonour, disgrace</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (unswērs, “dishonourable”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without honor, dishonorable, despised</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (swērs).</p></html>:/ˈunswikunθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unknown, not well recognized</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swikunþs).</p></html>:/ˈunswikunθoːza/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (unswikunþs)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (unswikunþs, “unknown”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza, comparative suffix).</p></html>:/ˈun.tals/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undisciplined, untrained, uneducated, unruly, disobedient</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;tals, “disciplined, obedient, trained”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>temporal</i>) until, as long as</li><li>(<i>causal</i>) because, for, as</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) reckless, overconfident, rash</li></ol><p>The first element is <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;untils, “unfitting”), from <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;tils) (compare its attested antonym <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (gatils)). The second element is <i>&#42;𐌼𐌰𐌻𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;malsks), a reflex of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malskaz</i> (“haughty”). Viewed in terms of it constituent elements, the compound can thus be analysed as having a literal meaning of "unsuitably haughty".</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrue, disloyal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;untriwwiz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐍃</i> (triggws, “true, loyal”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) uncrooked, blameless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (*wāhs, “crooked”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanhaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unworthily</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;unwairþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba); the former adjective is inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unwerþaz</i>. See also <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (wairþs) and <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spotlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼𐍃</i> (unwamms, “unblemished, spotless”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unblemished, spotless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;wamms, “unclean, blemished”), from <i>𐍅𐌰𐌼𐌼</i> (wamm, “spot, blemish”).</p></html>:/ˈunwɔrstws/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idle (averse to work or labor)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅</i> (waurstw).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlearned, ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;weis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unexpectedly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐍃</i> (wēns, “hope, expectation”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. there appears to be a suffix -iggs acting adverbially as an intermediate step (*unweniggs + -o, where *unweniggs would mean unexpected), but that is a noun suffix (see the entry at *-𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍃), not an adjective suffix. What gives?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unwillingness, displeasedness</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;unwērs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). A lemma <i>*unwēreins</i> may be thought to underlie the sole attested form of this word (the accusative singular), but this is dismissed by Streitberg and subsequent scholars.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be displeased or annoyed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wērjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) uncertain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unwissaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;wiss), past participle of <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorant person, fool</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan, “to know”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknowing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (witands).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance, folly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌹</i> (&#42;witi). The latter element derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witją</i>, whence English <i>wit</i> also derives. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) discontent, unhappy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (*wunānds), past participle of <i>&#42;𐍅𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (*wunān), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungrateful</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌲𐍂𐍃</i> (fagrs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blameless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (fairina).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unblamably, blamelessly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (fairinōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unsearchable; inscrutable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;fairlaistiþs), past participle of an unattested verb <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;fairlaistjan, “to seek out, examine, search”), from <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laistjan). Likely calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀνεξιχνίαστος</i> (anexikhníastos).</p></html>:/ˈunˌfɔːrs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gossipy, tattling</li><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) tattler</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) + the root of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (gafaurs, “well-behaved, sober”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwitting, without forethought, unintentional</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;faurweis, “with forethought, knowingly, intentionally”), from <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;weis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncomprehending</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (fraþjands, “understanding”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-abasement, neglect of one's own needs</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐌽𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;unfreidjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins) or from <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;freideins), from <i>𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (freidjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folly, foolishness, ignorance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (unfrōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐌽𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌳𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (unfrōdans)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish, ignorant, unwise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (frōþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unquenchable, inextinguishable</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍀𐌽𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;ƕapnands), present participle of <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍀𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ƕapnan), for which see <i>𐌰𐍆𐍈𐌰𐍀𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (afƕapnan). This word is likely a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἄσβεστος</i> (ásbestos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ceaseless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐌽-</i> (un-) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (ƕeila).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) (<i>when prefixed to a word with initial r-</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (raisjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to emerge</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rannjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deem, judge, decide, determine</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rēdan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzrīsaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;reisan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forth, go out</li><li>to come out, rise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzrinnaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan). Compare Old High German <i>irrinnan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) rising or getting up</li><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) resurrection</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzristiz</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>urrist</i> and Old English <i>ǣrist</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;uncertain definition) outcast? reprobate? depraved?</li></ol><p>Uncertain. There do not appear to be Germanic cognates. Lehmann mentions two etymologies given by early philologists, but "neither proposal [is] cogent" in his view. The first theory holds that the word is, "with loss of <i>w-</i>", to be related to Sanskrit <i>वृणक्ति</i> (vṛṇakti, “to turn”) and Ancient Greek <i>ἔργω</i> (érgō), both said to be from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to turn, bend”). The other theory similarly claims loss of <i>w-</i>, noting that the word should be properly reconstructed as <i>*uz-wrugks</i>, but relates it instead to Ancient Greek <i>ῥέμβω</i> (rhémbō), similarly said (by this etymologist) to be derived from Indo-European <i>*wer-</i>. With neither etymology being particularly persuasive, the provenance of the second element of this word remains elusive. In any case, the first element is the prefix <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become larger</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌿𐌼𐍃</i> (rūms) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html>:/ˈurˌruns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rise (of a celestial body&#58; appearance of moving from behind the horizon) (<i>particularly of the Sun</i>)</li><li>(by extension,&#32;uncountable) the east (compass point)</li><li>toilet, latrine (fixture used for urination and defecation) (translating <i>ἀφεδρών</i> (aphedrṓn))</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) departure (death)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-, “out-”) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (runs, “run”). The sense of "east", may be a semantic loan from Koine Greek <i>ἀνατολή</i> (anatolḗ).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzrunsiz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-, “out-”) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runsiz</i> (“stream, course”).</p></html>:/us/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>out of, from, away from, proceeding from [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz</i>. Cognate to German <i>er-</i> and <i>ur-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>out-, up-</li><li>In verbs, can indicate perfectivity (a finished action).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uz-</i> (“up, out”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust, weary, bruise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌲𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (agljan).</p></html>:/usˈaɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;agjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to exert oneself, to persevere at a task</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;aiwjan), likely from <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐍃</i> (aiws, “eternity”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan). Translates Ancient Greek <i>κοπιᾶν</i> (kopiân).</p></html>:/usˈalθans/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) outdated, old-fashioned</li></ol><p>Formally a past participle of an unattested verb from <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌻𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (alþeis, “old”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear away, remove</li><li>to bring forth, produce</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html>:/ˈusbalθiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bickering, incessant disputing or wrangling</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌸𐌴𐌹</i> (balþei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sweep out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;baugjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baugijaną</i> (“to bend, curve”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌳𐌹</i> (usbauhtēdi)</li></ol></html>:/usˈbiːdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to await</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (beidan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience, long-suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌽𐍃</i> (usbeisns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient, long-suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌽𐍃</i> (usbeisns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html>:/ˈusbiːsns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longsuffering</li><li>expectation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (usbeidan, “to await”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍃𐌽𐍃</i> (-sns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to pray for, request, wish</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bidjan). Compare German <i>erbitten</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beat severely, scourge</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌻𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (bliggwan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supplication, imploration, prayer</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍃𐌱𐌻𐍉𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;usblōtan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), from <i>𐌱𐌻𐍉𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (blōtan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy</li><li>to redeem</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bugjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;braidjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;braidijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off, become broken off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (brikan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan); the vocalism reflects a zero-grade form as in the past participle of <i>brikan</i>, *<i>brukans</i>. Possibly a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐκκλᾶσθαι</i> (ekklâsthai). De Vaan suggests that the term could be compared to Latin <i>frūniscor</i> which may also show a nasal infix and which belongs to a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;bʰruHg-</i>. However, this is unlikely and only tenable if the general view that <i>usbruknan</i> is related to <i>brikan</i> is abandoned, as the scholarly consensus (e.g. Lehmann, Kroonen and De Vaan himself) instead relates <i>brikan</i> to the root <i>&#42;bʰreg-</i>, whence Latin <i>frangō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>go out, go forth</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html>:/us.ˈɡɛːs.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, to scare</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gaisijaną</i>, which is related to <i>&#42;gaistaz</i>. Compare Old English <i>gæstan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in awe, to be frightened or astonished</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gīsnaną</i>, which is related to <i>&#42;gaistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reimburse, repay, give up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzgebaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (giban).</p></html>:/ˌusˈɣil.dan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repay, recompense, reward</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gildan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be poured out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) + a zero-grade form (possibly the past participle) of <i>𐌲𐌹𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (giutan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (graban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exhausted, faint, weary</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐍂𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰</i> (&#42;grudja). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/usˈdɔːdiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diligence, vigor, zeal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (usdauþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endeavor, strive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (usdauþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diligently</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (usdauþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, suffix forming adverbs from adjectives).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more diligent or earnest; comparative degree of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (usdauþs)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (usdauþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza).</p></html>:/usˈdɔːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earnest, diligent, vigorous</li></ol></html>:/usˈdriːβan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive out, expel</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (dreiban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌱𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (usdribeina)</li></ol></html>:/usˈdriu̯.san/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (driusan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a rough road</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (usdriusan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/usˈkʷiθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report or proclaim widely, to spread (the word)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to kill, annihilate, destroy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy utterly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (qistjan).</p></html>:/usˈhaːhan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to hang oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (hāhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) needy, wanting, lacking</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰</i> (&#42;haista). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to provoke, challenge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzhaitaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html>:/usˈhɔːh.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be exalted, glorified</li></ol><p>Fientive in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) corresponding to <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ushauhjan).</p></html>:/usˈhɔːh.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to elevate, lift up</li><li>to exalt</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hauhjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, lift up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hafjan). Cognate with Old High German <i>irheffen</i>.</p></html>:/usˈhinθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take captive, capture</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hinþan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinþaną</i> (“to reach for, catch”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap forth, jump to one's feet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hlaupan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaupaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to turn away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (hneiwan). Translates, and perhaps calques, Ancient Greek <i>ἐκκλίνω</i> (ekklínō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to hollow out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulōną</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>ūzholо̄n</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean out, cleanse</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hrainjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crucify</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hramjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off</li></ol><p><i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐍂𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hrisjan), the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrisjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌷𐍉𐍆𐌿𐌽</i> (ushōfun)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to pull or draw away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (þinsan). Compare Old High German <i>irdinsan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (usþulān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer through, endure, tolerate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (þulān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to harass, torment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzþreutaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌸𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;þriutan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodily exercise or training, fitness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌸𐍂𐍉𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (usþrōþjan, “to drill, to train”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to drill, train</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐍉𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (þrōþjan).</p></html>:/usˈθwahan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash off</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍅𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þwahan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (kannjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprout up, germinate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (keinan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test</li><li>to reject, eliminate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (kiusan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-known, manifest</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (kunþs), as if the past participle of a verb <i>&#42;𐌿𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;uskunnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay (down) upon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlagjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to train, teach thoroughly, educate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (laisjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow, permit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlaubijaną</i> (“to allow”). Cognate with Dutch <i>oorloven</i>, German <i>erlauben</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvation, redemption, liberation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (uslausjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to liberate, redeem</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to rid oneself of something, to divest oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzlausijaną</i>; compare Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;uʀlausijan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lausjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, depart</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclude</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man with palsy</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening</li></ol><p>Corresponding to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luką</i>; compare <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (uslūkan, “to open, unlock”)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open, unlock</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;lukan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lūkaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become open or unlocked</li></ol><p>Fientive verb in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) corresponding to <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (uslūkan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salvation</li></ol><p>Morphologically, apparently from <i>&#42;𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;uslunjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), the former being a hypothetical prefixed form of a hypothetical verb <i>&#42;𐌻𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;lunjan, “to save”), but none of these verbs are attested. Cognates are required for verification of origin. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌿𐍃𐌻𐌿𐍄𐍉</i> (uslutō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive, seduce</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌿𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (lutōn).</p></html>:/usˈmɛːtan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, excise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (maitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be or become abundant, to abound</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌲𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (managnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become known, become public</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;mērnan), fientive verb in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) corresponding to <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mērjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to proclaim, make known</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mērjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>behaviour, conduct, way of life</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (usmitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behave, to conduct or comport oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (mitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out, remove</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uznemaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look up</li><li>to (re)gain one's sight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzsehwaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”). Cognate with Old English <i>āsēon</i>, Old High German <i>irsehan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to explain or communicate (something to someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lodge</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (saljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sandjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engendering, begetting, semination</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ussatjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set or send forth</li><li>to beget, create</li><li>to plant</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read (aloud), recite</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (siggwan, “recite, sing”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>particularly, especially</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (sinþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (sitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear or cut off, separate</li><li>to awaken, return to one's senses</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzskarjaną</i>; by surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skarjan), from <i>&#42;skarjaną</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>ascirian</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vigilant, watchful</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍅𐍃</i> (&#42;skaws).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌿𐌸</i> (ussuggwuþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report, tell</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (spillōn).</p></html>:/usˈstaŋɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to pluck out</li></ol><p>Corrected by some editors to <i>usstiggan</i> on the assumption that it comes from <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stinganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise, stand up, emerge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzstandaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resurrection</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (usstandan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascend, go up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (steigan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profligacy, unbridledness</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;usstiurs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). May be a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀσωτία</i> (asōtía), which it translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) profligately, unbridledly</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌿𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;usstiurs, “profligate, unbridled”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba). May be a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἀσώτως</i> (asṓtōs), which it translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek out</li><li>to examine</li><li>to judge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzsōkijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sōkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌲</i> (usstaig)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proof, revelation, showing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ustaiknjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, point out, reveal</li><li>to designate, render</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taiknjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌹𐌸</i> (usstandiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end, completion, fulfilment</li><li>perfection</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (ustiuhan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuhtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌰𐌹</i> (usstassai)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead or drive out</li><li>to finish, fulfil</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (tiuhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (usstōþ)</li></ol></html>:/usˈwaɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke</li><li>to shake, excite, toss about</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mature, to become adult</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wahsjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, growth</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;wahsts, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahstiz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast away, eject</li><li>to expel, reject</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzwerpaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (wairpan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to wake someone up, to rouse</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to toss about, to move to and fro</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;walugjan). The word is generally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i>, possibly <i>&#42;welw-</i>; compare <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;walwjan), Latin <i>volvō</i>. The precise route of derivation from this root is not quite certain. Possible derivations for <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;walugjan) include:</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collapse, downfall, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (uswaltjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overthrow, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-, “out-”) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waltjan, “to tumble, turn about”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diversion, deception</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (uswandjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/usˈwandjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away, deviate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wandjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>righteousness, justice</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>righteous, just</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (uswaurkjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to operate</li><li>to perfect, finish, complete</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uzwurkijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waurkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castoff, rejection, expulsion</li><li>miscarriage, untimely birth</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (uswairpan, “to throw away, reject, expel”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (uswaurhts)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profane</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (weihs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without hope, despairing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐍃</i> (wēns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave, pleat, braid, twine</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;windan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disconnected, loosened, detached</li></ol><p>Contains the prefix <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rootlessness, disconnectedness, looseness, detachedness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (uswiss) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blameless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (fairina).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) departure? evacuation?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic. Compare German <i>Ausfahrt</i>, Dutch <i>uitvaart</i>, Swedish <i>utfart</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (usfilhan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (filhan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightened, scared</li><li>astonished</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌰</i> (&#42;filma).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fright, horror</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;filmei, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felmaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fulfillment, completion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (usfulljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/usˈɸullnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become full, to fill up</li><li>to become fulfilled</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (fullnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fulfill</li><li>to accomplish, complete, bring to completion</li><li>to fill, to supply (something which is lacking)</li><li>to comprehend, encompass, comprise; to be comprehensive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fulljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌽𐍉𐌳𐌴𐌳𐌿𐌽</i> (usfullnōdēdun)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to tempt</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (fraisan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to instruct, make wise</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍃-</i> (us-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍄𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;fratwjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fratwijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, nourishment</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍃𐍆𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;usfōdjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), from <i>𐍆𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fōdjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out of (in the direction of the speaker)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūt</i>. Germanic cognates: see there.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtai</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from outside</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍄𐌰</i> (ūta) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outside</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+genitive</i>) outside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ūtanē</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear out, carry away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍄</i> (ūt) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (bairan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, exit</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍄</i> (ūt) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) under (going beneath)</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) under (located beneath)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>under</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ub</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix indicating location underneath or an action&#47;movement forth from below or outwards from within&#59; often yields aspectually perfective verbs.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uba-</i>; equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍆</i> (uf) used as a prefix.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bound by an oath</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (aiþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis). Perhaps a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἔνορκος</i> (énorkos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) over (moving across)</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) over, above (positioned above)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>over, above</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uber</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upér</i>, from <i>&#42;upo</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>over-, super-, exceedingly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (ufar).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abound</li><li>to exceed, surpass</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (ufarassus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance (used positively)</li><li>excess (used negatively)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (ufar, “over”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-assus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>go too far</li><li>transgress</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to pour to overflowing, to overpour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ubergeutaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌹𐌿𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (giutan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) high priest</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰</i> (gudja). Translates Ancient Greek <i>ἀρχιερεύς</i> (arkhiereús), of which the Gothic might be a calque.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to get dressed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌼𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;hamōn), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to over-exalt; puff up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hauhjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disobedience</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ufarhausjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). The unattested verb is a prefixed variant of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan). Compare also English <i>overhear</i>, German <i>überhören</i> (“to disregard, ignore”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to become raised up high, to become exalted</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hafnan), fientive verb in <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan) corresponding to <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarhafjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to raise up above, exalt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic; compare Old English <i>oferhebban</i>, Old High German <i>ubarhevan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐍆𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hafjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born of heaven, heavenly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌹𐌼𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (himinakunds).</p></html>:/ufarˈhliːθrjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to rest [<i>with</i> <b>𐌰𐌽𐌰</b> (ana) <small>‘upon’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍂𐌰</i> (hleiþra, “tent”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐπισκηνόω</i> (episkēnóō, “to be quartered in, dwell upon”), from <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ, “tent”).</p></html>:/ufarˈhuɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be exalted above measure</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hugjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to surpass</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þeihan).</p></html>:/ufarˈlaɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;transitive) to lay upon, overlay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberlagjaną</i>; cognate with Old English <i>oferleċġan</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to go across, pass over</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;leiþan).</p></html>:/ˈufarmɔːdiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) forgetfulness, oblivion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maudjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscription, superscription</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarmēljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιγραφή</i> (epigraphḗ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscription, superscription</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarmēljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιγραφή</i> (epigraphḗ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to inscribe</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιγράφω</i> (epigráphō).</p></html>:/ufarˈmitoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) forget (<i>meaning uncertain</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (mitōn), but the interpretation and reading of this word, which occurs only once, is disputed. Another interpretation is that this in fact represents two words, the preposition <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (ufar) and the (accusative singular of the) noun <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (mitōns), yielding a meaning of "above the intention"; cf. Lehmann (1986).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forget</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (&#42;munnōn). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) beyond</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (jaina), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὑπερέκεινα</i> (huperékeina); apparently an adverbially used neuter plural (see <i>𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (jains)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sprinkling</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ufarranjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), from <i>&#42;𐍂𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rannjan). Possibly a calque of Ancient Greek <i>περίρρανσις</i> (perírrhansis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌳𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skadwjan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐπισκιάζειν</i> (episkiázein). Compare English <i>overshadow</i>, Dutch <i>overschaduwen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first fruits</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂</i> (ufar) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;skafts), a deverbal noun from <i>&#42;skapjaną</i> (or possibly a deverbal noun directly from a hypothetical verb <i>&#42;𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ufarskapjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascend or climb over</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberstīganą</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (steigan). Compare German <i>übersteigen</i>, Old English <i>oferstīgan</i>, Dutch <i>overstijgen</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perjurer, oathbreaker</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌰</i> (&#42;swara), the latter from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarô</i> (“swearer”). Alternatively derived from <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (ufarswaran) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forswear</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (swaran).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sprinkle over</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐍂𐌿𐍃𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;trusnjan). According to Pokorny, the latter is ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”), thus related to <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿</i> (triu).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow exceedingly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wahsjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be superior, exceed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to be”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) overfullness, overabundance, surfeit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberfullį̄</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (ufarfulls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overfill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberfullijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fulljan) or <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (ufarfulls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) full to overflowing; overfull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uberfullaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂-</i> (ufar-) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+genitive</i>) over (moving across)</li><li>(<i>+dative</i>) over (positioned above)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make swollen, inflate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;bauljan); ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. cognates?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow up, inflate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌻𐌴𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;blēsan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;blēsaną</i> (“to blow”).</p></html>:/ufˈbrikan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spurn, rebuff</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (brikan).</p></html>:/ufˈbrin.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be scorched (dried, damaged, burnt by exposure to sunlight or heat)</li></ol><p><i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (brinnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to gird oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gairdan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gerdaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up, unearth, excavate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (graban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, immerse</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to baptize</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (daupjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to raise up</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (habān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufhausjan, “to obey”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to obey, heed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hausjan, “to hear”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌻𐍉𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hlōhjan). The latter derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlōgijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subjugation, subjection</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufhnaiwjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subjugate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hnaiwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry forth, cry out, shout out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐍂𐍉𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hrōpjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strive, strain (to attain something)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þanjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>The second element is taken to be <i>𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (harjis), more specifically the stem -𐌷𐌰𐍂𐌹. The cause of the loss of final -(𐌹)𐍃 is unclear; the best explanation might simply be a dialectal change. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding, knowledge</li></ol><p>From the past stem <i>ufkunþ-</i> of <i>𐌿𐍆𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (ufkunnan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get to know, discover, perceive, find out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (kunnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fail, fall short</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (ligan).</p></html>:/uf.ˈmeːl.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superfluity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆</i> (uf) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐍉</i> (-jō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch or reach out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rakjaną</i> (“to stretch”).</p></html>:/ufˈsankʷ.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to swallow up (to consume, devour)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌵𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (saggqjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneak in</li><li>to sneak away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (sliupan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, butcher</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (sneiþan).</p></html>:/uɸˈstrɔː.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew or spread under something</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (straujan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swelling, bloating</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) vanity, being puffed up</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐌿𐍆𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;ufswalljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), the former unattested verb being a compound of <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) + <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swalljan), from <i>&#42;swallijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, groan</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐍉𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swōgan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōganą</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>often, ofttimes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ufta</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍄𐍉</i> (auftō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;of women) married, having a husband</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂</i> (wair). Some read two words, <i>uf waira</i>, "under a husband". Likely a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὕπανδρος</i> (húpandros).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exclaim, cry out</li></ol><p>From <i>𐌿𐍆-</i> (uf-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐍉𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wōpjan).</p></html>:/ˈpɛː.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak</li><li>shirt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;paidō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Pentecost</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>πεντηκοστή</i> (pentēkostḗ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peter, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πέτρος</i> (Pétros).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍀𐌿𐍂𐌰</i> (paurpura)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>purpura</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dyed purple</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍀𐌿𐍂𐌰</i> (paurpura) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (-ōþs), as if the past participle of a class 2 weak verb. A literal translation is thus 'purpled'.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Post-Wulfilan Gothic</i>) (Christian) priest, clergyman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παπᾶς</i> (papâs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Comforter; the Holy Ghost</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παράκλητος</i> (paráklētos).</p></html>:/pa.ras.kɛ.weː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Friday</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>παρασκευή</i> (paraskeuḗ), from <i>παρασκευάζω</i> (paraskeuázō, “to prepare”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Easter</li><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) Passover</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>πάσχα</i> (páskha), from Aramaic <i>פסחא</i> (pasḥa), from Hebrew <i>פֶּסַח</i> (pesaḥ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍀𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰</i> (paska)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paul, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Παῦλος</i> (Paûlos).</p></html>:/ˈpiː.kaˌbaɡms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) palm tree</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>&#42;𐍀𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;peiks, “sharp point”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌲𐌼𐍃</i> (bagms), the first part from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pīkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of nard</i>) pure, undiluted</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>πιστικός</i> (pistikós) with Gothic adjectival suffix <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>platea</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πλατεῖα</i> (plateîa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of cloth, a patch</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html>:/ˈplinsjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plęsati</i> (early <i>&#42;plensati</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money pouch, wallet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pungaz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>pungr</i> (Icelandic <i>pungur</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pound</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pundą</i>, from Latin <i>pondus</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presbytery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πρεσβῠτέρῐον</i> (presbŭtérĭon).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍀𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍉𐍂𐌹𐌰𐌿𐌽</i> (praitōriaun)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍀𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍄𐍉𐍂𐌹𐌰𐌿𐌽</i> (praitōriaun)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretorium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>πραιτώριον</i> (praitṓrion), from Latin <i>praetōrium</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Christianity) prophetess</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>προφῆτις</i> (prophêtis).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) prophet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>προφήτης</i> (prophḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) prophet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>προφήτης</i> (prophḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophecy</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>προφητεία</i> (prophēteía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prophesy</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>προφητεύειν</i> (prophēteúein).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍀𐍂𐌰𐌿𐍆𐌴𐍄𐌴𐍃</i> (praufētēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) psalm</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ψαλμός</i> (psalmós).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rabbi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ῥαββονί</i> (rhabboní), from Biblical Hebrew <i>רַבּוֹנִי</i> (rabbōnī, “my master”), alternative form of <i>רַבִּי</i> (rabī́).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rabbi, teacher</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ῥαββί</i> (rhabbí), from Aramaic <i>רבי</i> (“rabbi, spiritual teacher”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law, decree, ordinance</li><li>opinion, judgment, advice, counsel</li><li>task, responsibility</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raginą</i>. Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>&#42;ᚱᚨᚷᛁᚾᚨ</i> (&#42;ragina).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advisor, counsellor</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (raginōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule, to be a ruler (with dative&#58; over)</li><li>to advise, give counsel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raginōną</i>, from <i>&#42;raginą</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽</i> (ragin) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongue</li><li>speech, language</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razdō</i> (“voice, language, speech, sound”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;res-</i> (“to shout, speak”). Cognate with Old English <i>reord</i> (“voice, speech”), Old High German <i>rarta</i> (“sound, voice, harmony”), Old Norse <i>rǫdd</i> (“voice, speech”), Old English <i>reord</i> (1st and 3rd person singular preterite of <i>rǣdan</i> (“to read”)). More at <i>rede</i>, <i>read</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;razną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reckon, count</li><li>to consider, evaluate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rahnijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to offer</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>𐌿𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ufrakjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) easier</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍂𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;raþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza). The etymology of the positive degree of this adjective is unclear. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account, number</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raþjǭ</i>.</p></html>:/ˈrɛːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, determine, interpret</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raidijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>correctly, rightly</li><li>straightly, straightforwardly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (raihts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>truly, indeed (<i>difficult-to-translate discourse marker</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (raihts, “straight, correct”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight</li><li>correct</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rehtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raizijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) idiot, fool</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>ῥακά</i> (rhaká), from Aramaic <i>ריקא</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raudaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick, pluck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raupijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈrɔːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cane (plant with a simple and flexible stem, or the stem thereof)</li><li>cane (stem of such a plant adapted for use as a tool)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>cane (rod or stick used for corporal punishment)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rauzą</i>, of obscure origin. Cognate to Old High German <i>rôr</i> (whence German German <i>Rohr</i>) and close in formation to Old Norse <i>reyrr</i> (“reed”). Compare also English <i>rush</i>, which may be related.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) mile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rastō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈriː.kiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kingly, royal, regal, noble</li><li>mighty, powerful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīkijaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈriː.ki/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dominion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīkiją</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (reiks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i) or a substantivization of the neuter singular of <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (reikeis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to rule, govern</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (reiks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (-inōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greatest, most powerful, foremost; superlative degree of <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (reikeis)</li></ol></html>:/riːks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, lord</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;Gnosticism) archon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīks</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;rīxs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃rḗǵs</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>rēx</i> (“king”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble, shiver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rīrāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trembling</li><li>an earthquake</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (reiran, “to tremble, quake”).</p></html>:/ˈriɡn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain (condensed water from a cloud)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;regną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈriɡn.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make rain, to let it rain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rain [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rignijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌽</i> (rign) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍂𐌹𐌵𐌹𐍃</i> (riqis)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍂𐌹𐌵𐌹𐍃</i> (riqis, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dark; darken</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rikwizjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍂𐌹𐌵𐌹𐍃</i> (riqis, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>रजस्यति</i> (rajasyáti).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rekwaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁régʷos</i>.</p></html>:/ˈriˌkan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to heap up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rekaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃réǵeti</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calm, tranquility</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;remaz</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>रमते</i> (ramate).</p></html>:/ˈrinːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li><li>to flow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rinnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) brook, runnel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rinnǭ</i>. Cognate with Old High German <i>rinna</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruption, disintegration, perdition, ruination</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (riureis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corruptible, perishable, mortal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;riurijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to spoil, ruin, destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;riurijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room, space</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūmą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rome (a major city in Italy)</li><li>Rome, Ancient Rome (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa; in full, Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Rūmō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) roomy, spacious</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūmaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Roman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūmōniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret, mystery</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūnō</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>rūn</i> (English <i>roun, rune</i>), Old Saxon <i>rūna</i>, Old High German <i>rūna</i> (German <i>Raun</i>), Old Norse <i>rún</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running</li><li>course</li><li>flux, flow, issue</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running</li><li>course</li><li>flux, flow, issue</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;runiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, talk</li><li>to preach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōdijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courtyard</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōhsniz</i>, possibly from <i>&#42;rōkijaną</i> (“to attend to, care for”) + <i>&#42;-(s)niz</i> (nominal suffix), literally, “protected thing”, though the form would have to be analogical (one would expect &#42;𐍂𐍉𐌺𐌽𐍃 (&#42;rōkns)). See also Old English <i>neorxnawang</i>.</p></html>:/sa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>that, that one</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that (<i>singular</i>), those (<i>plural</i>)</li><li>the (translating the Ancient Greek article)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sa</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sabellius, an early third-century theologian.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sabellius</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine linen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sabanum</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Saturday, the Sabbath day</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>σάββατον</i> (sábbaton), from Hebrew <i>שבת</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsan.kʷa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;uncountable) the west (compass point)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sankwą</i>&nbsp;n (“sinking”). Cognate to Old Norse <i>sǫkk</i>&nbsp;n (“sinking”). The only attested sense of this word, "west", may be a semantic loan from Koine Greek <i>δυσμή</i> (dusmḗ); compare <i>𐌿𐍂𐍂𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (urruns).</p></html>:/ˈsankʷ.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to plunge (to thrust into water, or into any substance that is penetrable&#59; to immerse) [<i>with</i> <b>𐌹𐌽</b> (in, + accusative) <small>‘into something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sankwijaną</i> (“to cause to sink”). Cognate to Old English <i>senċan</i> and Old Norse <i>søkkva</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song, singing</li><li>recitation of a book</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sangwaz</i>. It is not clear why the Gothic noun is an i-stem.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (saþs)</li></ol></html>:/ˈsa.iː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) (the person/thing) who, that, which</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰</i> (sa) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>that particular</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that particular</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰</i> (sa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full, sated</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sadaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>behold</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly an apocopated form of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈</i> (saiƕ), second person singular imperative of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (saiƕan, “to see”). Alternatively, it has been derived from the pronoun <i>𐍃𐌰</i> (sa): either with a deictic -𐌹 (<i>-i</i>) (comparable to and of the same origin as the Greek deictic iota) or as a locative form.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēaną</i> (“to sow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₁-</i> (“to sow”).</p></html>:/ˈsɛhs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehs</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;s(w)éḱs</i> (which see for more). Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>seis</i> and Old Norse <i>sex</i>, and Koine Greek <i>ἕξ</i> (héx), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈsɛhs ˈtiɡ.jus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sixty (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehs tigiwiz</i> and cognate to Old Norse <i>sex tigir</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsɛh.sta/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the sixth</li></ol><p>Altered form of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehtô</i> (whence Old Norse <i>sétti</i> and Old High German <i>sehto</i>) with the <i>*s</i> inserted by analogy with <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (saihs, “six”). Identical in formation to Old English <i>sixta</i> and Old High German <i>sehsto</i>, and close to Koine Greek <i>ἕκτος</i> (héktos), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈsɛːnjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to tarry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sainijaną</i>; compare <i>&#42;sainōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain</li><li>sorrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sairą</i>, cognate with Old English <i>sār</i> (“sore, ache”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soul</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saiwalō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake, sea</li><li>marshland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saiwiz</i>.</p></html>:/sɛ.ʍan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sehwaną</i> (“to see”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to see, notice”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute, quarrel, argue</li><li>to rebuke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakaną</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>sāgiō</i>, Old English <i>sacu</i> (whence English <i>sake</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) garb worn for penitence or mourning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sakkuz</i>, from Latin <i>saccus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack, bag; sackcloth”), from Semitic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarrel, argument</li><li>strife</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐍉</i> (-jō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quarrelsome</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (sakan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌻𐍃</i> (-uls).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salbōną</i>, cognate to English <i>salve</i>, Dutch <i>zalven</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment, salve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌻𐌱𐍉𐌽</i> (salbōn, “to anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) jesting, banter</li></ol><p>Uncertain; no Germanic cognates are known. Has been tentatively linked to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sel-</i> (“jump”), whence Latin <i>saliō</i> (“to jump”), Ancient Greek <i>ἅλλομαι</i> (hállomai, “to jump”). May contain the same suffix found in <i>𐌷𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍂𐌰</i> (hleiþra).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodgings, guest room</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saliþwō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;sals, “dwelling”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (-þwa, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lodge</li><li>to be guest</li><li>to pay tribute, make an offer</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;sals, “house; dwelling”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saljaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saltą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saltaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>same</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consensus, agreement, concurrence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (sama, “together”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samaþa</i> (“together”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same kin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (sama, “same”) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌹</i> (kuni, “kin”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) as large, as extensive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (sama) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;lauþs, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laudaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>identical, having the same form</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samalīkaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (sama) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>likewise, similarly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (samaleiks, “identical”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍉</i> (samaleikō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samanai</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) of the same soul</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (sama) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌰</i> (saiwala), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>σύμψυχος</i> (súmpsukhos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) of one mind, unanimous</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌼𐌰</i> (sama, “same”) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌹</i> (fraþi, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, make agreeable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sandijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickness, illness, disease</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;suhtiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (siukan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way, manner</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrifice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sauþiz</i>, related to <i>&#42;sauþaz</i>, whence also Old Norse <i>sauðr</i>. Akin to English <i>seethe</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsɔː.il/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sun (star)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i>, and cognate to Old English <i>sōl</i> and Old Norse <i>sól</i>&nbsp;f, though reconstructing a Proto-Germanic form of this etymon is difficult; it may possibly have been a consonant stem Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōel</i> (from an earlier *<i>sōwel</i> with unexpected full-grade, whence the Gothic form) or Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōl</i> (from an earlier *<i>sōwul</i> with zero-grade, whence the Old English (remodeled as an <i>a</i>-stem) and Old Norse form (remodeled as a feminine <i>ō</i>-stem); one can see Ringe 2017 and Kroonen 2011 and 2013 for details. In the Indo-European root lies also the origin of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌽𐍉</i> (sunnō), as well as Koine Greek <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios), which both of the Gothic terms translate.</p><p>The term was an <i>l-</i>/<i>n-</i>stem in Proto-Indo-European, and Kroonen (2013) considers it likely that the Gothic term was too, as well as the Proto-Germanic form (which he reconstructs as a neuter heteroclitic stem <i>&#42;sōel</i>), thus viewing the dative form <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌹𐌽</i> (sunnin) as the regular dative form of the present lemma as part of a declension similar to the one of <i>𐍆𐍉𐌽</i> (fōn). Otherwise, <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌹𐌽</i> (sunnin) (attested twice in Mark) has generally been seen as a dative form of a neuter <i>an</i>-stem whose nominative form would be identical to the nominative of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌽𐍉</i>&nbsp;f (sunnō) (see e. g. Kroonen 2011, Lehmann 1986, Streitberg s. 132, and especially Ringe 2017 with ref.).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solomon, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σολομών</i> (Solomṓn), from Hebrew <i>שלמה</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>column, pillar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sauliz</i>, an ablaut counterpart to zero-grade <i>&#42;sūliz</i>. Cognates include English <i>sile</i>, Dutch <i>zuil</i>, German <i>Säule</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syrian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σύρος</i> (Súros).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief, sorrow</li><li>worry, care, concern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;surgō</i>. Cognates include English <i>sorrow</i>, Dutch <i>zorg</i>, German <i>Sorge</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry</li><li>to grieve, be sorrowful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;surgāną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐌰</i> (saurga) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌽</i> (-ān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syrian woman</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (saur) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌹</i> (-ini).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sarwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Satan</li><li>Satan (a person regarded as particularly malignant, detestable, or evil)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Σατανᾶς</i> (Satanâs), <i>Σατάν</i> (Satán), from Hebrew <i>שָׂטָן</i> (Satán).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (satana)</li></ol></html>:/ˈsatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;satjaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsaʍazuh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>whoever, whosoever (followed by <i>𐍃𐌰𐌴𐌹</i> (saei) or <i>𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌹</i> (izei))</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌰</i> (sa) +&lrm; <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>late</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīþuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simon, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σίμων</i> (Símōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Genitive form of the reflexive pronoun.</li></ol></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>his/her/its/their own (third person reflexive possessive)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selfish</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (seins, “his, her, its/their”, reflexive possessive determiner) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;gairns, “desirous, eager”, from <i>&#42;gernaz</i>).</p></html>:/siːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>his/her/its/their own (third person reflexive possessive)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (sinteins)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodness, gentleness, kindness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌴𐌻𐍃</i> (sēls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good, kind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēliz</i> (“happy, fortunate, kind, good”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌺𐌸𐌰𐌽𐌹</i> (sibakþani)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>forsaken me (said by Jesus on the cross)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Ancient Greek <i>σαβαχθανι</i> (sabakhthani), itself a transliteration of an Aramaic term.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relationship, kinship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sibjō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsi.bun/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebun</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septḿ̥</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>seofon</i> and Crimean Gothic <i>sevene</i>, and Ancient Greek <i>ἑπτᾰ́</i> (heptắ).</p></html>:/ˈsi.bun.teː.hund/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seventy (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sebuntēhundą</i> of obscure formation. Akin in some way to Old English <i>hundseofontiġ</i>, Old Saxon <i>antsiƀunta</i> and Old High German <i>sibunzo</i>.</p></html>:/siŋɡʷan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing</li><li>to recite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;singwaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sengʷʰ-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsin.kʷan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink (descend into liquid)</li><li>to set (of a heavenly body&#58; to disappear below the horizon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinkwaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sengʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;segaz</i>. Cognates include German <i>Sieg</i>, Dutch <i>zege</i>, Old Norse <i>sigr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prize, spoils</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌹𐍃</i> (sigis, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌽</i> (laun, “reward”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌻𐌾𐍉</i> (sigljō) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal (on a message)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sigillum</i> (“seal”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom; habit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siduz</i>. May contain the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice, meditate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sidōną</i>.</p></html>:/sik/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Accusative form of the reflexive pronoun.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sek</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shekel (ancient Levantine currency)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σίκλος</i> (síklos), <i>σίγλος</i> (síglos) (directly or via Latin <i>siclus</i>), from Hebrew <i>שֶׁקֶל</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>self</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyewitness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌱𐌰</i> (silba, “self”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;siuneis, “one who sees(?)”); compare <i>𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (siuns, “sight, vision, face”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voluntary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selbawiljaz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>selfwille</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌱𐌰</i> (silba) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (wilja) with an adjectival suffix.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amazement, astonishment</li></ol><p>From or closely related to <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (sildaleiks).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, marvelous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seldalīkaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;silds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks). Cognate with Old English <i>sellīċ</i> (English <i>selly</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be marvelled at, to be admired</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (sildaleiks, “amazing, marvelous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, suffix forming fientive verbs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marvel, to wonder (at), be amazed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (sildaleiks, “marvellous, wondrous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, marvelous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seldalīkaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;silds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks). Cognate with Old English <i>sellīċ</i> (English <i>selly</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silubrą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of silver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;silubrīnaz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌹𐌻𐌿𐌱𐍂</i> (silubr, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once, sometime in the past</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;simlê</i>. Distantly related to Latin <i>semel</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsi.naː.pis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustard (plant, <i>Sinapis alba</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sināpis</i> (<i>sināpi</i>), from Ancient Greek <i>σίνᾱπι</i> (sínāpi).</p></html>:/ˈsiniːxs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of people</i>) old</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sénos</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinīgaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sénos</i> (“old”). Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἕνος</i> (hénos), Sanskrit <i>सन</i> (sána), Old Armenian <i>հին</i> (hin) and Latin <i>senex</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time, occurrence (as in: <i>five times</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sinþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eldest; superlative degree of <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (sineigs)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (sineigs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html>:/ˈsin.tiːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sintīnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (sinteins, “daily”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sintīnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sick</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seukaną</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick, weak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seukaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickness, illness, disease</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siukį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌹𐌿𐌺𐍃</i> (siuks) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick, weak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seukaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face</li><li>appearance, sight</li><li>vision, perception</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siuniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siwjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower, disciple, student</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Green (1998), from a continental Celtic etymon <i>*sepānios</i> ("follower"), an agent noun from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to follow”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to be a disciple or student (to someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌹𐍀𐍉𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (sipōneis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan), calque of Ancient Greek <i>μαθητεύειν</i> (mathēteúein).</p></html>:/sis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Dative form of the reflexive pronoun.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;siz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sitjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsitls/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat (place in which to sit)</li><li>throne (seat of a ruler)</li><li>bird's nest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;setlaz</i> (“seat”). Cognate with Old English <i>setl</i> (“sitting, seat”) and Old High German <i>sezzal</i> (“seat”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave, to shear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skabaną</i>. Note also <i>𐌺𐌰𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (kapillōn), a borrowing.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaduz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌿𐌻𐌰</i> (skaþula)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrongdoing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþaz</i>, <i>&#42;skaþiz-</i>, from <i>&#42;skaþjaną</i> (“to damage, harm, hurt”), whence <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skaþjan).</p></html>:/ˈskaθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm, do wrong, injure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>injurious</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaþulaz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skaþjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌻𐍃</i> (-uls).</p></html>:/ˈskɛːdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, divide, put asunder</li><li>to set at variance, cause dissension between</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;reflexive</i>) depart from, get divorced from</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaidaną</i>, Verner variant of <i>&#42;skaiþaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skey-t-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery, bondage, servitude</li><li>service to a god; worship</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐍃</i> (skalks, “slave, servant”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus), or <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (skalkinōn, “serve”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-assus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to serve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌻𐌺𐍃</i> (skalks, “servant, slave”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (-inōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave</li><li>servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skalkaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tile, brick</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaljō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skamō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skamāną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskamaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skamāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) shame, disgrace</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skandō</i>. Cognates include English <i>shand</i>, Dutch <i>schande</i>, German <i>Schande</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandal strap</li></ol><p>The second element is <i>&#42;𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍀𐍃</i> (&#42;raips), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raipaz</i>. The first is unclear; perhaps from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaudō</i> (“sheath, husk”). Compare Old Norse <i>skauð</i> (“sheath”), German <i>Schote</i> (“pod, husk, sheath”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) beautiful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skauniz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskɔrp.joː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) scorpion (arachnid)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>scorpiō</i>, itself borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos), which the Gothic term translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hem, fringe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skautaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;epithet of God) creator</li></ol><p>A nomen agentis from the root of <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaskapjan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moneychanger, banker</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐍄𐍄𐍃</i> (skatts, “money”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “er”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a denarius</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) money, wealth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skattaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;reflexive) to ready oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaftijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskiː.ma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lantern, torch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīmô</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskiː.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīnaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskiːriːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explanation, clarification</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīrīniz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skeirjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, lucid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīriz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḱēy-</i> (“luster, gloss, shadow”). Cognate with English <i>sheer</i>, Danish <i>skær</i>, German <i>schier</i> (“sheer”), Dutch <i>schier</i> (“almost”). Outside Germanic, cognate to Albanian <i>hirrë</i> (“whey, serum”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (skeireins)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to proceed, to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skēwijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskil.dus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skelduz</i> (“shield”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)keit-</i>, <i>&#42;(s)keid-</i>, <i>&#42;kheit-</i> (“shield, cover”). Contains a Verner alternant of the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html>:/ˈskil.liŋɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of coin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skillingaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>shilling</i>, Dutch <i>schelling</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher</li></ol><p>Agent noun in <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja), likely from an otherwise unattested verb <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;skiljan) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skiljaną</i>. Alternatively, a direct inheritance from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skiljô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat</li><li>ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skipą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) mirror, looking-glass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuwwô</i> (“reflection”). Translates Ancient Greek <i>ἔσοπτρον</i> (ésoptron).</p></html>:/ˈsku.la/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a debtor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skulô</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽</i> (skulan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html>:/ˈskulan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to owe, to be indebted</li><li>to have to, to be obliged to, must</li><li>shall, will</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skulaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈskuː.ra/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm (disturbed state of the atmosphere) (only used of windstorms)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skūrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuftą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Scythian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σκύθης</i> (Skúthēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skōhaz</i> (“shoe”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skōhslą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsla.hals/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pugnacious person; a lout or bully</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slahalaz</i> or <i>&#42;slahulaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (slahan) + <i>-𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (-als), variant of <i>-𐌿𐌻𐍃</i> (-uls); the word is a substantivized adjective.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, strike, smite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slahaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;slak-</i> (“to hit, strike, throw”). Cognate with Old English <i>slēan</i>, Old Frisian <i>slā</i>, Old High German <i>slahan</i>, Old Norse <i>slá</i>, Old Saxon <i>slahan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (slahals)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blow, strike</li><li>affliction, plague</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slagiz</i> (“blow, strike”).</p></html>:/slɛxts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) smooth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slihtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slahtiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍃𐌻𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (slahan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs). Cognate with English <i>slaughter</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep quiet, be still</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)lew-</i> (“slack, loose, limp, flabby”) and related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slūm-</i> (“loose, limp”) (modern English <i>slumber</i>). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detriment, loss</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger, peril</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌸𐍃</i> (sleiþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce, dangerous</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with Old English <i>slīþe</i>, Old Norse <i>slíðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slēpaną</i> (“to sleep”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;slab-</i>, <i>&#42;slap-</i> (“to be weak, limp, languid”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slēpaz</i> (“sleep”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneak, creep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sleupaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat, grease</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smerwą</i> or <i>&#42;smirwijaną</i> with suffix <i>&#42;-þrą</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsmak.ka/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig (fruit)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smakkuz</i> (“taste”). Compare also Old Church Slavonic <i>смокꙑ</i> (smoky, “fig”).</p></html>:/ˈsmak.kaˌbaɡms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig tree</li><li>sycomore</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌼𐌰𐌺𐌺𐌰</i> (smakka) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌲𐌼𐍃</i> (bagms).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smallest; superlative degree of <i>&#42;𐍃𐌼𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;smals)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smalistaz</i>, superlative degree of <i>&#42;smalaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;𐍃𐌼𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (&#42;smals) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, feces</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σμύρνα</i> (smúrna).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak, robe</li></ol></html>:/ˈsnɛːws/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) snow (precipitation)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snaiwaz</i> (“snow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snóygʷʰos</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>snāw</i>, Old Dutch <i>snēo</i>, Old High German <i>sneo</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>снѣгъ</i> (sněgŭ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌽𐌰𐌿</i> (snau) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, reap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snīþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, rush</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sniumijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;𐍃𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌼𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;sniumeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, hurriedly</li></ol><p>Apparently from an unattested <i>&#42;𐍃𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;sniumunds, “quick, hurried”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix), from <i>&#42;𐍃𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌼𐍃</i> (&#42;sniums) or some other term related to <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sniumjan, “to hurry, haste, rush”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more urgently, with greater haste; comparative degree of <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌳𐍉</i> (sniumundō)</li></ol></html>:/ˈsniwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, go away, leave</li><li>to strive for</li><li>to come upon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snewaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsnu.triː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleverness; wisdom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snutrį̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, clever, sagacious</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snutraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woven basket</li></ol><p>From the same root as Dutch <i>snoer</i> and German <i>Schnur</i> (“cord, cable”), related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nēaną</i> (“to sew”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₁-</i> (“to spin”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) stomach (<i>genitive singular</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to itch, tickle</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to itch, tickle</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole of a shoe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>solea</i> (“sole, sandal”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌼𐌰𐌹𐌶𐌴</i> (sumaizē) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌼𐌰𐌹</i> (sumai) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈsumɛːθθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌼𐌰𐌹 &#43; -𐌿𐌷 &#43; 𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (sumai + -uh + þan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌼𐌰𐌼𐌼𐌰</i> (sumamma) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once, at some point (in the past)</li><li>in part</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;suman</i>, itself from <i>&#42;sumaz</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-n</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (sumans) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) some (one), a certain (one)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) some, certain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sumaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐌿𐌼𐍃</i> (sums) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alone, asunder, apart, privately</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundrô</i>, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sundraz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsun.noː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sun (star)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunnǭ</i>, derived from the oblique stem of <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i>, and cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>sune</i> and Old English <i>sunne</i>. The Indo-European lemma also yielded <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌹𐌻</i> (sauil), as well as Koine Greek <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios), which both of the Gothic terms translate.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunjō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, truthful, correct</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunjaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) truthfully</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (sunjis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰</i> (sunja, “truth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;friþus, “peace”). Why the attested form ends in <i>-as</i> is unclear, but compare the similarly non-Ulfilan form <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌸𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (friþareikeis); this may be due to dialectal differences between Ulfilan Gothic and Ostrogothic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>truthful, factual</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐌰</i> (sunja, “truth”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html>:/ˈsun.jis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, truthful, correct</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunjaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsunjoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to vindicate, defend as true, show to be true</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunjōną</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apologia, defence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (sunjōn, “to vindicate, show to be true”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apologia, defence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (sunjōn, “to vindicate, show to be true”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suHnús</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once, soon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;suniz</i> (“soon”), probably related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;sān(ō)</i> (“immediately”). Cognate with Old English <i>sōna</i> (English <i>soon</i>), Old Frisian <i>sōn</i>, Old Saxon <i>sāno</i>, Old High German <i>sān</i>, Old Norse <i>senn</i> (Icelandic <i>senn</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once, forthwith</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (suns) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅</i> (aiw).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as soon as, when</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐌿𐌽𐍃</i> (suns) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to season</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;supōną</i>, perhaps a variant of <i>&#42;suppōną</i> (or vice versa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mild, tolerable</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweh₂d-</i> (“sweet”), though this is not entirely certain due to the semantic shift "sweet" > "mild" when virtually all other Germanic descendants retain the original "sweet" sense.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sentinel, guard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>speculator</i>, possibly via Ancient Greek <i>σπεκουλάτωρ</i> (spekoulátōr).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saliva</li></ol><p>The first element is related to Dutch <i>speeksel</i>, German <i>Speichel</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/spɔːrds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race, racecourse, racetrack</li><li>stade, furlong</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurdz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈspar.wa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparrow (bird)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sparwô</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>spearwa</i> and Old High German <i>sparo</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) comparative degree of <i>&#42;𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;spēþs)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;spēþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>latest, last of all; superlative degree of <i>&#42;𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;spēþs)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;spēþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) latest, last of all; superlative degree of <i>&#42;𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;spēþs)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>&#42;𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;spēþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌳𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌹</i> (spēdizei)</li></ol></html>:/ˈspiːwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spīwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing-tablet</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>story, tale, fable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellą</i>. Cognates include English <i>spell</i>, Old Norse <i>spjall</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proclaimer, herald, announcer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellô</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>story, tale, fable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellą</i>. Cognates include English <i>spell</i>, Old Norse <i>spjall</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to narrate, relate, tell, bring tidings of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spinnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, hastily</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woven basket</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>σπυρίς</i> (spurís). Compare Classical Syriac <i>ܐܣܦܪܝܕܐ</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (staþs)</li></ol></html>:/staθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, location, site</li><li>land (<i>from the point of view of a boat on the water</i>), shore</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stadiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stéh₂tis</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staþaz</i>. Cognate with Crimean Gothic <i>statz</i>, Old High German <i>stad</i>, <i>stado</i> (German <i>Gestade</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way, path, road</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staigō</i> (“way; path”). Cognate with German <i>Steige</i> (“steep trail; steep ascent”). Related to sty.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stony, rocky</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainagaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (stains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (-ahs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stonen, made of stone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainīnaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (stains, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stone, pelt with stones</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (stains) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainaz</i>, whence also Old English <i>stān</i> (English <i>stone</i>, Scots <i>stane</i>), Old Frisian <i>stēn</i>, Old High German <i>stein</i> (German <i>Stein</i>), Proto-Norse <i>ᛊᛏᚨᛁᚾᚨᛉ</i> (stainaʀ) (Old Norse <i>steinn</i>). Ultimately from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;steyh₂-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈstɛr.noː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) star (luminous celestial body)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sternǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂stḗr</i>. Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>stein</i> (possibly a misprint for *<i>stern</i>). Identical in formation to Old Norse <i>stjarna</i>, and, more distantly, cognate to Koine Greek <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈstɛ.roː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infertile woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sterǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ster-</i>. Compare Latin <i>sterilis</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;hapax legomenon) stigma, wound mark, scar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stakiz</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. related to *stikaną?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stammering</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stammaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌸</i> (standaiþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈstandan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;standaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgment, trial, dispute</li><li>judge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judge's seat, tribunal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌰</i> (staua) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌻𐍃</i> (stōls).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punch, hit, slap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stautaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>exact meaning uncertain</i>) element, foundation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stabaz</i> or an i-stem alternative form <i>&#42;stabiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb, ascend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stīganą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stebnō</i> (“voice”). More at steven (etymology 1).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clash</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stinkwaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaker, chalice, goblet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stiklaz</i>, a derivation of <i>&#42;stikiz</i> (whence also Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (stiks, “point”)). Cognates include Old Norse <i>stikill</i> (“pointed end of the horn”), Old High German <i>stehhal</i> (“goblet”), Dutch <i>stekel</i> and English <i>stickle</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point, moment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stikiz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈsti.lan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to steal</li></ol></html>:/ˈstiu̯r/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male calf, young bull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;steuraz</i> (“bull”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;táwros</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>stjórr</i>, German <i>stior</i>, Old English <i>stēor</i> (English <i>steer</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set up, to establish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stiurijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teygʰ-</i> (“to await patiently”), see also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tixъ</i> (“still, quiet”), Sanskrit <i>तितिक्षु</i> (titikṣu, “awaiting patiently”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stubjuz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈstrɔː.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strawjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line, stroke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strikiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍃𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (stōþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chair</li><li>throne</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōlaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>stool</i>, Dutch <i>stoel</i>, German Low German <i>Stohl</i>, German <i>Stuhl</i>, Old Norse <i>stóll</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substantial grounds, just cause</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōmô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stéh₂mn̥</i> (“that which stands, stature”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge</li></ol><p>Usually derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stoh₂éyeti</i> (or some other derivational pathway from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”)).</p><p>Gusmani and Jasanoff reject the usual derivation of this verb from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>, and instead trace it to <i>&#42;stew-</i> (“to praise”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so, thus, like that, in that manner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swa</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so that, insomuch</li><li>therefore</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰</i> (swa) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html>:/swah/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so, thus</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰</i> (swa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father-in-law</li></ol><p>From a variant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swehuraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱuros</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>swēor</i>, Old High German <i>swehur</i> (German <i>Schwäher</i>, German <i>schwieger-</i>), Middle Dutch <i>swêer</i>, Old Swedish <i>svær</i> (Swedish <i>svär-</i>). The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin <i>socer</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἑκυρός</i> (hekurós), Sanskrit <i>श्वशुर</i> (śváśura), Old East Slavic <i>свекръ</i> (svekrŭ) (Russian <i>свёкор</i> (svjókor)), Albanian <i>vjehërr</i>, Lithuanian <i>šešuras</i>. Compare <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍂𐍉</i> (swaihrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swegrō</i>, with alteration of <i>g</i> to <i>h</i> under the influence of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍂𐌰</i> (swaihra, “father-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>so great, so extensive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰</i> (swa) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;lauþs, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laudaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>such, that kind of, that sort of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swalīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰</i> (swa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sponge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swammaz</i> (“sponge, mushroom”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear (an oath)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without a reason, in vain</li></ol><p>Unknown; no clear etymology and no cognates. It has been suggested to derive from <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌰𐌽</i> (swaran, “to swear an oath”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴</i> (-ē, adverbial suffix), although this is semantically difficult.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌴</i> (swarē, “in vain”)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dark) ink</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌰𐍂𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swartjan, “blacken”, from <i>&#42;swartijaną</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌻</i> (-izl, from <i>&#42;-slą</i>), calque of Ancient Greek <i>μέλαν</i> (mélan, “ink”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swartaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>swart</i>, Dutch <i>zwart</i>, German <i>schwarz</i>, Old Norse <i>svartr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>relative adverb: as</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so that</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌰</i> (swa) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴</i> (swē).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>like, similar to</li><li>just like, in the same manner as</li><li>as if</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swē</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jubilance, exultation, joy</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌲𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;swēgns, “happy, jubilant”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exult, triumph</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌲𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;swēgns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan). Closely related to Old English <i>swēġan</i> and to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōganą</i> (“to resound”), which is attested in Gothic in various affixed forms such as <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐍅𐍉𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gaswōgjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indeed, nevertheless, however</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēþauh</i>; compare Old English <i>swā þēah</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴</i> (swē) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐌷</i> (þauh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cease, end, finish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swībaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈswiːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig (animal, <i>Sus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swīną</i>, from an adjectival form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suH-</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>swīn</i> and Old Norse <i>svín</i>, and Latin <i>suīnus</i> (“porcine”, adjective).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swērāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honour</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (swērs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honored, dear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēraz</i>; literally, “heavy” (for sense development, compare Sanskrit <i>गुरु</i> (guru)). Cognates include English <i>sweer</i>, Dutch <i>zwaar</i>, German <i>schwer</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>own</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is owned, ownings, property, possession</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēsaz</i>.</p><p>Substantivized form of the above.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulfur, brimstone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweblaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flautist</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌲𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (swiglōn, “to play the flute”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html>:/ˈswiɣloːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play the flute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweglōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌲𐌽𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (swēgniþa)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌴𐌲𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (swēgnjan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>innocently, sincerely</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (swikns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity, innocence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (swikns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purification</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swiknjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). It should be noted that each of the apparent attestations of this word (all in the accusative singular) could also be forms of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (swiknei, “purity”), which is attested unambiguously elsewhere (in the nominative singular) and is thus confirmed to exist. However, at least the three attestations in the Skeireins fragments do seem in their context to be meant to be interpreted as <i>purification</i> rather than <i>purity</i>, which would point to the existence of this word as separate from the aforementioned <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (swiknei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity, chastity</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (swikns, “pure, innocent”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innocent, pure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swiknaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈswikunθaba/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly</li><li>expressly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swikunþs, “known openly”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, “adverb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known openly, conspicuously known</li><li>manifest</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹-</i> (swi-, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swe-</i>) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (kunþs).</p></html>:/ˈswiltan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sweltaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, power</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swinþs, “strong”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become strong</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swinþs, “strong”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, fientive suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, healthy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swinþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stronger; comparative degree of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swinþs)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐍃</i> (swinþs, “strong, healthy”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza, comparative suffix).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swestēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swésōr</i>.</p></html>:/sy.mɛː.oːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simeon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Σῠμεών</i> (Sŭmeṓn), from Biblical Hebrew <i>שִׁמְעוֹן</i> (shim'ón).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the leader of a synagogue; archisynagogue</li></ol><p><i>𐍃𐍅𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍉𐌲𐌴</i> (swnagōgē) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;faþs), calque of Koine Greek <i>ἀρχισυνάγωγος</i> (arkhisunágōgos).</p></html>:/ˈsynaɡoːɡeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a synagogue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>συναγωγή</i> (sunagōgḗ, “synagogue”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fey man; a man who is near death</li></ol><p>From a derivate of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swultiz</i> (“death”) + <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (wairþan, “to become”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, agent suffix). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. weird formation, any more info?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pool, place for swimming</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sumslą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍃𐍅𐌿𐌼𐍃𐌻</i> (swumsl)</li></ol><p>The origin of the -f- is unclear. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syria (a geographic region consisting of two provinces of the Roman Empire in Western Asia, Coele Syria and Syria Phoenice, approximately corresponding to modern Syria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Συρία</i> (Suría).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh</li><li>to groan</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍃𐍅𐍉𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;swōgan, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swōganą</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-atjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satisfaction, satiation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investigator, seeker</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sōkjan, “to seek”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a search, investigation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōkīniz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍃𐍉𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sōkjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question, point of controversy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōkniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek, look for</li><li>to seek to, desire, go about</li><li>to ask for, require</li><li>to question, reason, dispute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sōkijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taglą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teardrop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tahrą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dáḱru-</i>. The <i>h</i>/<i>g</i> alternation (due to Verner's law) in the Proto-Germanic forms has disappeared in Gothic through morphological leveling. Cognates include Latin <i>lacruma</i>, Ancient Greek <i>δάκρυ</i> (dákru), Old English <i>tēar</i>, and Sanskrit <i>अश्रु</i> (aśru).</p></html>:/ˈtaɡr.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to shed tears</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌰𐌲𐍂</i> (tagr) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan). See also Old English <i>tīeran</i> (“to tear”).</p></html>:/ˈtax.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, rend, rip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tahjaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtɛ.hun/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehun</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déḱm̥t</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>tīen</i> and Crimean Gothic <i>thiine</i>, and Koine Greek <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈtɛ.hun ˈθuː.sund.joːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) ten thousand (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “ten thousands”.</p></html>:/ˈtɛ.hun.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the tenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehundô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dekm̥tós</i>. Cognate to Old Norse <i>tíundi</i> and Ancient Greek <i>δέκᾰτος</i> (dékătos).</p></html>:/ˈtɛ.hun.teː.hund/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one hundred (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehuntēhundą</i> of obscure formation. Akin in some way to Old English <i>hundtēontiġ</i> and Old High German <i>zehanzo</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtɛ.hun.teː.hundˌfalθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) hundredfold (multiplied by a hundred)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (taihuntaihund, “(short) hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (-falþs, “-fold”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳</i> (taihuntaihund)</li></ol></html>:/ˈtɛ.hunˌteː.wiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ordered by tens; using a decimal system</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (taihun, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌴𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;tēweis, “ordered”, from <i>&#42;tēwijaz</i>).</p></html>:/ˈtɛh.swa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right (of direction, opposite of left)</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) right (right hand&#59; right, not left, side)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right (right hand&#59; right, not left, side)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehswô</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱs-</i>. Cognate to Old Irish <i>dess</i> and related to Koine Greek <i>δεξιός</i> (dexiós), which it translates.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tehswō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱs-</i>. Related to Koine Greek <i>δεξιά</i> (dexiá), which it translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, manifest</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to pretend, feign</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taiknijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌽𐍃</i> (taikns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, indication</li><li>miracle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;taikniz</i>, related to <i>&#42;taikną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tainijǭ</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) twig or branch of a bush</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tainaz</i> of uncertain etymology. Cognate to Old Norse <i>teinn</i> (“twig, sprout”) and Old Saxon <i>tēn</i> (“staff”), among others.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tetrarch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>τετράρχης</i> (tetrárkhēs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talitha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ταλιθά</i> (talithá), from Aramaic <i>טליתא</i> (ṭalyəṯāʾ, “female child, girl, maiden”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruction</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (talzjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/ˈtalzjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, instruct</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talō</i> (“narration, telling”)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher, instructor</li></ol><p>Substantivized form of <i>𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (talzjands), present participle of <i>𐍄𐌰𐌻𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (talzjan).</p></html>:/ˈtand.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle (to start (a fire))</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tandijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deed, act, accomplishment</li><li>task</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tōją</i>, specifically a form whence ultimately Proto-Samic <i>&#42;tuojē</i> also derives. By surface analysis, <i>𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taujan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/ˈtɔːjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>do, make</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tawjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to break out, break forth</li></ol><p>No known Germanic cognates. A connection with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to separate, to tear”) has been proposed. Translates Ancient Greek <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Timothy, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Τιμόθεος</i> (Timótheos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tēkaną</i> (“to touch”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, array</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tēwō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈti.ɡus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decade (series of ten things)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teguz</i>. Cognate with Old Norse <i>tigr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something fitting or suitable (<i>attested only as an accusative singular; plausibly not a noun at all</i>)</li></ol><p>Occurring only once in the accusative, the exact form and context of this word are not sufficient to determine its part of speech and etymology. There are two options:</p><p>Following Lehmann, the first option is here taken as a lemma.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edification</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌹𐌼𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (timrjan, “to build”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, agent noun suffix).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, construct</li><li>to strengthen, edify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;timrijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, construct</li><li>to strengthen, edify</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;timrijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtiu̯.han/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull, draw, drag</li><li>to lead, lead away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teuhaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtunɡl/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>firmament</li><li>constellation</li><li>star</li><li>luminous celestial body</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tunglą</i> (“celestial body”). Cognate to Old Norse <i>tungl</i> (“moon”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongue</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tungǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dn̥ǵʰwéh₂s</i>.</p><p>Germanic cognates: Old English <i>tunge</i>, Old Frisian <i>tunge</i>, Old Saxon <i>tunga</i>, Old Dutch <i>tunga</i>, Old High German <i>zunge</i>, Old Norse <i>tunga</i>.</p><p>Indo-European cognates: Sanskrit <i>जिह्वा</i> (jihvā), Old Latin <i>dingua</i> (Latin <i>lingua</i>), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬌𐬰𐬬𐬁</i> (hizvā), Old Church Slavonic <i>ѩзꙑкъ</i> (językŭ), Lithuanian <i>liežùvis</i>, Old Irish <i>tengae</i>, Welsh <i>tafod</i>, Tocharian A <i>käntu</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>dys-; Verbal prefix indicating negativity or difficulty.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuz-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dus-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doubt, disbelieve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐌿𐌶-</i> (tuz-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wērjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safety, security</li><li>bulwark, stronghold</li><li>foundation</li></ol><p><i>𐍄𐌿𐌻𐌲𐌿𐍃</i> (tulgus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reinforce, establish, confirm</li></ol><p><i>𐍄𐌿𐌻𐌲𐌿𐍃</i> (tulgus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steadfast, resistant, firm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tulguz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dl̥h₁gʰós</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtund.nan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to be on fire (being burned by fire)</li></ol><p>A fientive <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan)-formation derived from the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tandijaną</i> (“to kindle”) (whence <i>𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (tandjan)). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tunþuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trust, confidence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌰𐌽</i> (trauān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trust</li><li>to rely on</li><li>to confide in</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trūwāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covenant, pact</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;traustą</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pledge, covenant, testament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trewwō</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true</li><li>faithful, loyal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;triwwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>faithfully</li><li>confidently</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐍃</i> (triggws) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true</li><li>faithful, loyal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;triwwiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadness, regret</li></ol><p>Related to, but not directly inherited from, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tregô</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈtriu̯/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of wood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trewą</i> (“tree, wood”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>trēow</i>, Sanskrit <i>दारु</i> (dāru), and Hittite <i>𒋫𒀀𒊒</i> (tāru).</p></html>:/ˈtri.wiːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) wooden (made of wood)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;triwīnaz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿</i> (triu, “piece of wood”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins, “adjective-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step, tread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trudaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtwa ˈhun.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) two hundred (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “two hundreds”. Compare Old English <i>tū hund</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtwɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwóh₁</i>. Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>tua</i> and Old Norse <i>tveir</i>, and Koine Greek <i>δύο</i> (dúo), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈtwɛː ˈtiɡ.jus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) twenty (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twai tigiwiz</i> and akin to Old English <i>twēntiġ</i> and Old Norse <i>tuttugu</i>. Contrast Crimean Gothic <i>stega</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtwɛː ˈtiɡ.jus ˈθuː.sund.joː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) twenty thousand (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “twenty of thousands”.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍄𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌹𐍆</i> (twalif)</li></ol></html>:/ˈtwa.libˌwin.trus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) twelve years old</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twalifwintruz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>twelfwintre</i> and Old Norse <i>tolfvetr</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍄𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌹𐍆</i> (twalif, “twelve”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌹𐌽𐍄𐍂𐌿𐍃</i> (-wintrus, “winter”).</p></html>:/ˈtwa.lif/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelve (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twalif</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>twelf</i> and Old Norse <i>tolf</i>. Contrast Crimean Gothic <i>thunetua</i>.</p></html>:/ˈtwiːhnɛː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>a pair (collective number)</li><li>two each (distributive number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twīhnaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwóh₁</i>. Cognate to English <i>twin</i> and Old Norse <i>tvinnr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twīflaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disputation, doubting, hesitation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍆𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (tweifljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doubt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twīflijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍄𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍆𐌻</i> (tweifl) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Prefix - occurring only in <i>𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (twisstandan) and the related <i>𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (twisstass) - indicating separation from something.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twiz-</i>. Doublet of <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, depart or separate from</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃-</i> (twis-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discord</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃-</i> (twis-, “asunder, in twain”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;stass, “[act of] standing”) or from <i>𐍄𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (twisstandan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs). Probably a calque of Koine Greek <i>διχοστασία</i> (dikhostasía).</p></html>:/ˈtwoːs ˈθuː.sund.joːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) two thousand (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “two thousands”.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acts, a book of the New Testament: <i>a</i> clipping of <i>𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌰 𐌰𐍀𐌰𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌻𐌴</i> (tōja apaustaulē, “Acts of the Apostles”)</li></ol><p>A clipping.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deed, act, accomplishment</li><li>task</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tōją</i>, specifically a form whence ultimately Proto-Samic <i>&#42;tuojē</i> also derives. By surface analysis, <i>𐍄𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (taujan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acts of the Apostles</li></ol><p>A calque of Koine Greek <i>Πράξεις Ἀποστόλων</i> (Práxeis Apostólōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌲𐍉</i> (&#42;waggō, “cheek”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis, “-er”). The first element, the reconstruction of which is not entirely certain, likely derives from or is closely related to either Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wangô</i> or <i>&#42;wangiją</i>.</p><p>This word is alternatively reconstructed as neuter ja-stem <i>𐍅𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌹</i> (waggari) (only one form is attested, which could indicate either lemma).</p></html>:/ˈwaŋɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) paradise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wangaz</i> (“field, meadow, slope”). Cognate with Old English <i>wang</i>, <i>wong</i>, Old Saxon <i>wang</i>, Old High German <i>-wang</i>, Old Norse <i>vangr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li><li>to move around</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sum of money paid in advance as a deposit; a security, an earnest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wadją</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>written pledge, debenture, bond</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌳𐌹</i> (wadi, “monetary deposit, security”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍉𐌺𐍉𐍃</i> (bōkōs, “writings, a book”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahsijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>size, stature</li><li>increase, growth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahstuz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌰𐌷𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wahsjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus). Cognate with English <i>waist</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) watch, vigil (<i>dative plural</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahtwō</i> (“watch, vigil”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>woe! alas! (expression of grief)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wai</i>. Compare Latin <i>vae</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwɛː.an/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow (to produce an air current, of wind)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēaną</i> (“to blow”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evildoer, criminal, bandit</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;waidēþs, “crime, woeful deed”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, suffix forming agent nouns); the first element (the antonym of <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (wailadēþs), which is attested) derives from <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹</i> (wai, “woe”) + <i>&#42;𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;dēþs, “deed”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fight, quarrel, battle</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corner</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornerstone, quoin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍃𐍄𐌰</i> (waihsta, “corner”) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (stains, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihtą</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wihtiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well (in a good manner)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wela</i>. Germanic cognates: see there. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good deed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weladēdiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;dēþs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blessing</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;qiss, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) good reputation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;mērei). The latter derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mērį̄</i>. The entire compound may be a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐφημία</i> (euphēmía), the word found in the Greek passages corresponding to where this Gothic word is found.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evangelization, preaching, bringing good tidings</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wailamērjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of good repute, praiseworthy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mērijaz</i>. Contrast Proto-Norse <i>ᚹᚨᛃᛖᛗᚨᚱᛁᛉ</i> (wajemariʀ).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evangelize, preach, bring good tidings</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mērjan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐαγγελίζεσθαι</i> (euangelízesthai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evangelize, bring good tidings</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍀𐌹𐌻𐌻𐍉𐌽</i> (spillōn), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐαγγελίζεσθαι</i> (euangelízesthai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) nourishment, (good) food</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌶𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;wizns), for which see the etymology at <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌶𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (gawizneigs) and <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌶𐌽𐍃</i> (andawizns).</p></html>:/ˈwɛːnahs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wretched, unhappy, miserable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wainagaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>A phrase that signifies a wish or desire for the present or the future.: if only, would that; that not, how pitiful that not, woe!</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹</i> (wai, “woe, alas”) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌴𐌹</i> (nei, “not [at all]”).</p></html>:/wɛːps/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown, laurel, wreath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waipaz</i> and cognate to Old Norse <i>veipr</i> (“a kind of head cloth”). Related to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (weipan, “to wreathe, crown”).</p></html>:/wɛr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wiHrós</i>. Cognates include Old Norse <i>verr</i>, Ossetian <i>ир</i> (ir, “Ossetians”), Lithuanian <i>vyras</i>, Sanskrit <i>वीर</i> (vīrá), Latin <i>vir</i>, Old Irish <i>fer</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a manly fashion</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;wairaleiks, “manly”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, adverb-forming suffix); the former adjective is inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weralīkaz</i>, and superficially derives from <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂</i> (wair, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks, “-ly, -like”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>host</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werduz</i>. Likely contains the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus). Cognate with German <i>Wirt</i>, Dutch <i>waard</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worth, value</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþą</i>, related to <i>&#42;werþaz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become</li><li>to happen, to come to pass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy, suitable, useful, valuable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>worthily</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (wairþs, “worthy”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba, “adverb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰𐌹𐌳</i> (wairþaid) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈwɛrθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become</li><li>to happen, to come to pass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worthiness, fittingness, sufficiency</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (wairþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌳𐌰</i> (-ida).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy, suitable, useful, valuable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to value, estimate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werþōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weruz</i> (“lip”) + <i>-𐌹𐌻𐍉</i> (-ilō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw</li></ol><p>According to Kroonen, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werpaną</i> (whence also Old English <i>weorpan</i> and Old Norse <i>verpa</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wergʷ-</i>. Adams, however, connects the term with Latin <i>urbs</i> (“city”) and Tocharian B <i>wārp-</i> (“to surround”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werbʰ-</i> (“to enclose”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>worse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirsiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worse; comparative degree of <i>𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (ubils)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirsizô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worst; superlative degree of <i>𐌿𐌱𐌹𐌻𐍃</i> (ubils)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wirsistaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perhaps</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍄</i> (wait) +&lrm; <i>𐌴𐌹</i> (ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍄</i> (wait) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lament</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹</i> (wai) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍈𐌿𐍃</i> (fairƕus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈwakan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay awake</li><li>to watch, keep alert</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wakaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵ-</i> (“lively, awake”).</p></html>:/ˈwal.dan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule</li><li>to suffice</li><li>to be content with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waldaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authority, power</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐍅𐌰𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (waldan) + <i>-𐌿𐍆𐌽𐌹</i> (-ufni), a variant of <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni). See Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waldą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, genuine</li><li>beloved</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, select, pick</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waljaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, stick</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waluz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll, thrash or tumble about (said of waves)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waltijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to wallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walwisōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb</li><li>paunch, belly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wambō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stain, spot, blemish, flaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wammaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deficiency, lack</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waning, diminishing</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (*wanān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains), apparently a class 3 weak verb related to <i>&#42;wanōną</i> (“to wane”) and the adjective <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (wans, “lacking”).</p></html>:/ˈwandjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wandijaną</i>. Equivalent to a causative of <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;windan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rod</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanduz</i>. Cognates include English <i>wand</i>, Old Norse <i>vǫndr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack, shortage</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (wans) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacking, missing, void</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wanaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bad reputation</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌾𐌰𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;wajamēreis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wajamērijaz</i>. The entire compound may be a calque of Ancient Greek <i>δυσφημία</i> (dusphēmía), which this word is used to translate.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blasphemy, slander, sacrilegious or evil speech</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌾𐌰𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wajamērjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blaspheme, slander</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌾𐌰𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;wajamēreis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wajamērijaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹</i> (wai) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐍂𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mērjan). Cognate with Proto-Norse <i>ᚹᚨᛃᛖᛗᚨᚱᛁᛉ</i> (wajemariʀ /⁠wajēmāriz⁠/, “ill-famous, of poor repute”).</p></html>:/wɔrd/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurdą</i> (“word”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werdʰh₁om</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to words and speech</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (-ahs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word-fight, logomachy, debate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd, “word”) +&lrm; <i>𐌾𐌹𐌿𐌺𐌰</i> (jiuka, “quarrel”). Possible calque of Ancient Greek <i>λογομαχία</i> (logomakhía).</p></html>:/ˈwɔrk.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, make</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurkijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwɔrms/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake, serpent (reptile)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurmiz</i> (“worm, snake”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥mis</i> (“worm”).</p></html>:/ˈwɔrstu/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a work, deed, accomplishment</li></ol><p>Related to the verb <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waurkjan, “to work”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurkijaną</i>, whence also English <i>work</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worker</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅</i> (waurstw) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a work, deed, accomplishment</li></ol><p>Related to the verb <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waurkjan, “to work”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurkijaną</i>, whence also English <i>work</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act, deed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅</i> (waurstw) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effective, useful, operational</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅</i> (waurstw) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worker, esp. a farmer</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐍅</i> (waurstw) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html>:/ˈwɔrts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) root (part of a plant)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) root (primary source)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) lineage (descent)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurt-</i>, the oblique stem of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrōts</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>wyrt</i> and Old High German <i>wurz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condemnation, judgment, damnation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wargiþō</i>. Morphologically, it consists of <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wargjan, “condemn, damn”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard, sentry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardijô</i>; compare Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;vartija</i>. An agent noun in <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja) from the same root as the element <i>-wards</i> in <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (daurawards).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) wariness, wiliness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐍂𐍃</i> (wars) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈwarm.jan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm (to make or keep warm)</li><li>to cherish (to treat with affection, nurture with care)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warmijaną</i>. In the sense of "cherish", possibly a semantic loan from Koine Greek <i>θάλπω</i> (thálpō, “to warm, to care for, to cherish”). May be distantly related to Koine Greek <i>θερμαίνω</i> (thermaínō, “to warm, heat”), which it otherwise translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forbid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) alert, wary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waraz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwasjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clothe</li><li>to wear, be clothed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wazjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;woséyeti</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌰𐍃</i> (was) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌰𐍃</i> (was) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a garment, piece of clothing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wastijō</i>, or alternatively from <i>&#42;wastiz</i>, inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéstis</i> (“clothing, garment”); in either case ultimately from the root <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to clothe”). Cognate to Latin <i>vestis</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;watōr</i>, the stem of which alternated between ending in <i>-r</i> and <i>-n</i>. Note that the stem in Gothic has undergone some changes, losing the final <i>-r</i> in the nominative and accusative singular (presumably to resemble the inflection of neuter an-stem nouns more closely). The contracted plurals, however, are preserved. Gothic also preserves the alternate <i>-n</i> stem for the oblique cases; this contrasts with both the West Germanic languages, which instead regularized the <i>-r</i> stem (cf. Old English <i>wæter</i>, genitive <i>wæteres</i>), and the North Germanic languages, which extended the <i>-n</i> stem to the nominative and accusative cases (cf. Old Norse <i>vatn</i>).</p></html>:/ˈweːɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) agitation of the sea</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wave (moving disturbance, undulation)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēgaz</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>wǣġ</i>, North Frisian <i>waag</i>, and Swedish <i>våg</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃𐌰</i> (weihsa)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhô</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (weihs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hallow, consecrate, sanctify</li><li>to fight, to battle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhijaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiganą</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holy, sacred</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li><li>countryside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhsą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“house, settlement”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>विश्</i> (viś, “village, settlement, tribe”), <i>वेशः</i> (veśáḥ, “inhabitant, neighbor”), Ancient Greek <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos), dialectal <i>ϝοῖκος</i> (woîkos, “house, dwelling, fence, cage”), Latin <i>vīcus</i> (“group of houses, village”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>holily</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (weihs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hallow, consecrate, sanctify</li><li>to fight, to battle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhijaną</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiganą</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holy, sacred</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li><li>countryside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhsą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“house, settlement”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>विश्</i> (viś, “village, settlement, tribe”), <i>वेशः</i> (veśáḥ, “inhabitant, neighbor”), Ancient Greek <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos), dialectal <i>ϝοῖκος</i> (woîkos, “house, dwelling, fence, cage”), Latin <i>vīcus</i> (“group of houses, village”).</p></html>:/ˈwiː.hi.θa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanctity, holiness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhiþō</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (weihs, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa, “-ity, -ness”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become hallowed, sanctified</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (weihs, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, fientive suffix).</p></html>:/wiːhs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holy, sacred</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li><li>countryside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhaz</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhsą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“house, settlement”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>विश्</i> (viś, “village, settlement, tribe”), <i>वेशः</i> (veśáḥ, “inhabitant, neighbor”), Ancient Greek <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos), dialectal <i>ϝοῖκος</i> (woîkos, “house, dwelling, fence, cage”), Latin <i>vīcus</i> (“group of houses, village”).</p></html>:/wiːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) vine-</li></ol><p>Either inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīną</i>, or borrowed from its source, Latin <i>vīnum</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwiː.naˌba.si/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grape (fruit)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīnabasją</i>, a compound word whose first element is equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein, “wine”). Cognates include German <i>Weinbeere</i>, English <i>wineberry</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwiː.naˌɡards/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīnagardaz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (gards, “yard”).</p></html>:/ˈwiː.naˌtɛːns/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine twig</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (tains, “branch”).</p></html>:/ˈwiː.naˌtriu̯/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine (climbing plant that produces grapes.)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) vineyard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīnatrewą</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿</i> (triu, “piece of wood”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunkard, winebibber</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐍂𐌹𐌲𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (drigkan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drunk, wine-addicted</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein, “wine”), with an unclear second element forming the adjective. Köbler suggests <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (&#42;weinags) as the proper reconstruction; that is, <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (wein) + <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags). The suffix may also have been the related <i>-𐌰𐌷𐍃</i> (-ahs). (The fact that the only two attestations are from the same manuscript, Codex Ambrosianus B, makes comparison with other scribes impossible.) <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to wreathe, crown</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīpaną</i>.</p></html>:/wiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we (first-person plural personal pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) testimony</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (weitwōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of bearing witness, testifying</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (weitwōdjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) testimony</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (weitwōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) testimony</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (weitwōþs, “witness”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) to testify as a witness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐍅𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (weitwōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/ˈwiːt.woːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wītwōdz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to await, expect</li><li>to hope (for)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēnijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope, expectation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēniz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈweːpn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weapon (instrument of combat)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wēpną</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>wǣpn</i> and Old Norse <i>vápn</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>May be from earlier <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌰</i> (&#42;wēreika), where the first part probably corresponds to <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;wērei, “truth”) (as found in the wors <i>𐌰𐌻𐌻𐌰𐍅𐌴𐍂𐌴𐌹</i> (allawērei)). from <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;wērs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). The change of 𐌴𐌹 to 𐌴 in the spelling of <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei) would then either be reflective of a sound merger or a simple scribal error. The -𐌺𐌰 may be an <i>an</i>-stem variant of <i>&#42;-𐌿𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;-uks, “diminutive suffix”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌴𐍃𐌿𐌽</i> (wēsun) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌴𐍃𐌿𐌽</i> (wēsun) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html>:/ˈwi.ɡaˌdiː.noːm/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) thistle (prickly plant)</li></ol><p>Etymology unclear. First element is commonly related to <i>𐍅𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (wigs). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīgą</i> (“war”)? <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. This form is Köbler's reconstruction, but our PGmc lemma differs. The phrase is "du wigana"; looks kind of like a substantivized infinitive?</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road</li><li>path</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wegaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphan</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;widuwernô</i>, from the same source as <i>&#42;widuwǭ</i>, or a Gothic formation (based on <i>𐍅𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍅𐍉</i> (widuwō) or a related term) with no Proto-Germanic antecedent. The suffix is unclear and its origin disputed. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a widow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;widuwǭ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁widʰéwh₂</i>. Germanic cognates include English <i>widow</i>, Dutch <i>weduwe</i>, German <i>Witwe</i>; Indo-European cognates include Latin <i>vidua</i>, Sanskrit <i>विधवा</i> (vidhávā), Ancient Greek <i>ἠΐθεος</i> (ēḯtheos, “unmarried person”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍅𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍅𐍉</i> (widuwō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to live one's life</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably related to <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐍅𐌹𐌶𐌽𐍃</i> (andawizns) and its related terms. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈwi.θra/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+accusative</i>) against</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrą</i>, more specifically from the compound form <i>&#42;wiþra-</i>, which seems to have analogically replaced the form of the preposition. Cognate to German <i>wider</i> (“against”), Dutch <i>weer</i> (“again”), English <i>wither</i> (“against”), Old Norse <i>viðr</i> (“against”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go towards, go to meet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐌸𐍂𐌰</i> (wiþra) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐌸𐍂𐌰</i> (wiþra) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐍉𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamōtjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>witherward, opposite, against.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþrawardaz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌹𐌸𐍂𐌰</i> (wiþra) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (-wairþs).</p></html>:/ˈwi.θrus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) lamb (young sheep)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;weþruz</i> (“yearling lamb”). Cognate with Old English <i>weþer</i> and Old Norse <i>veðr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to shake, wag</li></ol><p>Isolated in Germanic. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sequence, routine</li><li>week</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wikǭ</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwil.θiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wild</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wilþijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will, desire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bias, partisanship, partiality</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (wilja) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;halþs, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halþaz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈwiljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to want</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₁-</i>.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will, desire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiljô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Viliaric</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰</i> (wilja, “will, desire”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍂𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;rēþs, “counsel”) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdaz</i>; cf. Vandalic <i>&#42;rith</i>, original East Germanic [e:] shifting to [i] in unstressed positions, in parallel with Greek and Latin <i>iotacism</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>Wilrǣd</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robber, bandit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;welwô</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (wilwan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a). Cognate with Old Norse <i>ᚢᛅᛚᚢᛅ</i> (ualua) (<i>accusative singular</i>).</p></html>:/ˈwilwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take by force (<i>of people or things</i>), to rob, to raven (obtain or seize by violence)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;welwaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₃-</i> (“to hit, strike”).</p></html>:/ˈwinds/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind (movement of air)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wéh₁n̥ts</i> (“blowing”), present participle of <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i> (“to blow”). Cognate with Old English <i>wind</i>, Latin <i>ventus</i> (“wind”), Ancient Greek <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “to blow”), Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣтръ</i> (větrŭ, “wind”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a tool for winnowing (probably a kind of shovel, or perhaps a fork or fan)</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;winþjan, “winnow”, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winþijaną</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;windwōną</i>) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍉</i> (&#42;skaurō). The latter element is cognate with Old High German <i>skora</i> (“shovel, hoe”), apparently deriving from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skurǭ</i>, related to <i>&#42;skeraną</i> (“to cut”) (compare Dutch <i>scheuren</i> (“to tear”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffering, passion, yearning</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (winnan, “to suffer”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p><p>Non-lemma forms.</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffering, passion, yearning</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍅𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (winnan, “to suffer”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) pasture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;winjō</i>. Cognates include Old Dutch <i>&#42;winna</i>, <i>&#42;wenne</i> (in toponyms), Old Norse <i>vin</i>, Old High German <i>winne</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwin.trus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) winter (fourth season, marked by short days and lowest temperatures)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) year (with numeral, used for ages and durations of time)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wintruz</i>&nbsp;m.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) crown</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (weipan). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. odd: looks like an agent noun in -ja, but a crown, well, isn't an agent</i></p></html>:/ˈwis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calm (of sea or wind)</li></ol><p>Unknown; no direct cognates are known in any Germanic language. Lehmann (1986) tentatively suggests a connection to the verb <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to be, abide”) (ostensibly in the sense of the sea "being" or abiding as one with the observer). Translates Ancient Greek <i>γαλήνη</i> (galḗnē).</p></html>:/ˈwisan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be</li><li>to remain, abide</li><li>to feast, make merry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (“to be, remain”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i>. Compare Latin <i>Vesta</i> (“goddess of the hearth”), Sanskrit <i>वसति</i> (vásati, “dwell”). Some forms are suppletive and instead derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésti</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wesaną</i> (“to consume, feast”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to graze”). The usage of the adverb <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (waila) to qualify this verb is very common (three out of the four total attestations of <i>wisan</i>); the resulting phrase <i>waila wisan</i> (in various conjugated forms) seemingly represents a calque of Ancient Greek <i>εὐφραίνεσθαι</i> (euphraínesthai), which it translates. However, the same Greek verb may also be translated simply as <i>wisan</i> without the adverb <i>waila</i> (Luke 15:24).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐍃𐌰</i> (wissa) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature, essence, being</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wistiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/ˈwit/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we two, the two of us (first-person dual personal pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wet</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>wit</i> and Old Norse <i>vit</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌴𐌳𐌿𐌽</i> (witaidēdun) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌸</i> (witaiþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈwitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li><li>to observe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witaną</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“see”).</p><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witāną</i>. Equivalent to <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐍄</i> (&#42;wit) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌽</i> (-ān).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan, “to know”) with a suffix <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌿𐌼</i> (witum) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html>:/ˈwi.toː.ða.ˌlɛː.saː.riːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law teacher</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (witōþ, “law”) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (laisāreis, “teacher”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawless</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (witōþ, “law”) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (laus, “devoid of”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawyer, law scholar, legist</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (witōþ, “law”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;fasteis, “expert”), the latter element related to <i>𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fastan) (possibly via an unattested <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;fasts, “firm, secure”), from <i>&#42;fastaz</i>, <i>&#42;fastuz</i>) with the agent noun–forming suffix <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lawfully</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌳𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (&#42;witōdeigs, “lawful”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉</i> (-ō, “adverbial suffix”), from <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐍉𐌸</i> (witōþ, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html>:/ˈwi.toːθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witōþą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look around</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlaitōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to mortify, bruise</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, possibly from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wliza-</i> (“rod”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to turn, wind, round”), the same root of Latin <i>lōrum</i> (“thong”), Old Armenian <i>լար</i> (lar).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face</li><li>countenance, appearance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wlitiz</i>. Cognates include German <i>Antlitz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to well up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious, splendid, wonderful</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (wulþus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags). Cognate with Old English <i>wuldriġ</i> (“glorious”).</p></html>:/ˈwul.θus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glory</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulþuz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wl̥tus</i>, whence Latin <i>vultus</i>. Contains the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) more significant, more valuable, more important</li></ol><p>Seemingly from <i>&#42;𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍂𐍃</i> (&#42;wulþrs, “significant, valuable, important”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza). For more information, see the etymology at <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍂𐍃</i> (wulþrs), which is apparently attested as a noun.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍃</i> (wulþrais)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) significance, importance, meaning</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (wulþus, “glory”).</p><p>It is not certain that this word in fact existed as a noun; it has also been credibly suggested that the attestation <i>wulþrais</i> (in Codex Ambrosianus A; this would morphologically be the genitive of an i-stem noun) is to be viewed as the genitive to a noun <i>wulþris</i> derived from a comparative adjective; the parallel attestation <i>wulþris</i> (in Codex Ambrosianus B) would then simply be the nominative. A comparative adjective is in fact attested elsewhere as <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍂𐌹𐌶𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (wulþrizans), which suggests the existence of an adjective <i>*wulþrs</i>. For more, see references below; Falluomini's reading (both a noun and an adjective <i>*wulþrs</i> existed) is followed here.</p></html>:/ˈwul.la/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) wool (cloth or yarn made of wool)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wullō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wĺ̥h₁neh₂</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>wull</i>, Dutch <i>wol</i>, Old High German <i>wolla</i>, Old Norse <i>ull</i>, and Latin <i>lāna</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuller; someone who fulls cloth</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌰</i> (wulla, “wool”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-āreis, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (wilwan, “to plunder, rob, violently seize”).</p></html>:/ˈwulfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolf (animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wulfaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wĺ̥kʷos</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>wulf</i>, Old High German <i>wolf</i>, Old Norse <i>ulfr</i>, <i>úlfr</i>, and Koine Greek <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) disease, plague</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wundō</i> + <i>-𐌿𐍆𐌽𐌹</i> (-ufni), a variant of <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) suffering, agony, torment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wunniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) crooked</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persecution</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrakō</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>wracu</i>. Likely related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrēkō</i>, see there for descendants. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persecution</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrekaną</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persecute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrakjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) persecutor, punisher</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍅𐍂𐌰𐍄𐍉𐌽</i> (wratōn, “to travel”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel, journey</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wratōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persecution</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrēkiz</i> +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ˈwri.θus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) herd</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrēþuz</i> (“herd”). Cognate to Old English <i>wrǣþ</i> (“band”) and Old Danish <i>vrāth</i> (“herd of swine”). The <i>-i-</i> in the Gothic descendant (which is attested only once, possibly indicating a spelling error) is unexpected; the form <i>&#42;𐍅𐍂𐌴𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;wrēþus) would be more consistent with its cognates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persecute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrekaną</i> (“to pursue”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tittle or single written stroke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;writiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a snare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrungǭ</i>; related to <i>&#42;wringaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈwroːhjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrōgijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wrōhiz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werh₁-</i> (“to tell, speak”). See also Old Saxon <i>wrōht</i> (“strife”), Old Norse <i>róg</i> (“quarrel, defamation”), Old English <i>wrēġan</i> (“to accuse, impeach; incite”).</p></html>:/woːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessed by a devil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōdaz</i> (“excited, agitated, angry”) (compare Middle Dutch <i>woet</i>, Old High German <i>wuot</i>, Old Norse <i>óðr</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₂t-ós</i>, from <i>&#42;weh₂t-</i> (“excited, possessed”) (compare Latin <i>vātēs</i> (“seer, prophet”), Old Irish <i>fáith</i> (“seer”), Welsh <i>gwawd</i> (“song”)).</p></html>:/ˈwoː.θiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet, pleasant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōþijaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) wakefulness, watch</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍅𐍉𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (*wōkān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-ains), apparently a class 3 weak verb related to <i>𐍅𐌰𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (wakan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usury</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōkraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out, lament, weep, call</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wōpijaną</i> (“to weep”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰</i> (frauja, “the Lord”)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faginōną</i>, <i>&#42;faganōną</i>, from <i>&#42;faganaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair, proper, suitable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fagraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) father</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fadēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfa.driːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parents (always plural in meaning, but often singular in grammatical number -- even when congruent with another plural)</li><li>(patri-)lineage, ancestry</li></ol><p>This word is problematic in some respects, which muddles the etymology. Note the apparent grammatical incongruence in various attestations, with apparently singular forms of <i>fadrein</i> agreeing with plural demonstratives: <i>þai fadrein</i> and <i>þans fadrein</i> (found in John 9:18-22). Note also the existence of one apparently neuter plural form <i>fadreina</i> (Corinthians II, 12:14), which contrasts with the masculine demonstratives <i>þai</i> and <i>þans</i> of the earlier-mentioned examples.</p><p>There are multiple theories of this word's origin, including:</p><p>It is formally identical to Old Norse <i>-feðri</i> (“parentage”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) (patri-)lineage, descent, kin</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>𐍆𐌰𐌳𐌰𐍂</i> (fadar, “father”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). This word is somewhat problematic. Note that the i-stem noun-forming suffix <i>-eins</i> is usually deverbal, which is not the case here, and the sole attested form <i>fadreinais</i> is considered by some to be a scribal error, presumably for <i>fadreinis</i> (see <i>𐍆𐌰𐌳𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (fadrein)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, seize</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanhaną</i> (“to take, capture”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (fahēþs)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“joyful, pretty”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence, hedge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþo-</i> (“fence”), perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂-t-eh₂-</i>, from <i>&#42;peh₂-t-</i> (“to protect, graze”), from <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfɛːan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to blame, find fault</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fēaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfɛ.hu/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property, possessions</li><li>wealth, riches</li><li>money</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fehu</i> (“livestock”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péḱu</i> (“livestock”). Cognate with Old English <i>feoh</i> (“livestock, property, money”) (modern English <i>fee</i>), Old Norse <i>fé</i> (“livestock, wealth, money”), and Latin <i>pecū</i> (“livestock”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) (the sin of) covetousness; greed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (faihugairns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). Cognate with Old Norse <i>fégirni</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>avaricious, covetous, greedy</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu, “wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (&#42;gairns, “desirous, eager”, from <i>&#42;gernaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(something done for) pecuniary gain, profit</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌹</i> (gawaurki).</p></html>:/fɛhuˈɡiːɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to covet</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu) +&lrm; <i>(𐌲𐌰)𐌲𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> ((ga)geigan)</p></html>:/ˈfɛː.huˌɣiː.roː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covetousness, greed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu, “wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌴𐌹𐍂𐍉</i> (&#42;geirō). The latter element derives from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gīrǭ</i>. Compare also Old Saxon <i>fehugiri</i> (“covetousness”).</p></html>:/ˈfɛhuθrɛːhna/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mammon (personification of avarice)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu, “property”) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (þreihan, “to press, force”), thus literally ‘money compulsion’.</p></html>:/ˈfɛ.huˌsku.la/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a debtor</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌻𐌰</i> (skula).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) (the sin of) covetousness; greed</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (faihufriks, “covetous”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covetous</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu, “wealth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;friks, “desirous, covetous”, from <i>&#42;frekaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿</i> (faihu)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix with intensifying meaning.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firi-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i>. Cognate with English <i>for-</i>, Dutch <i>ver-</i> and German <i>ver-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to partake of, to participate in</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (aihan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fergunją</i>.</p></html>:/fɛrˈɡriːpan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>grasp, take hold of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firigrīpaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (greipan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fersnō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tpḗrsneh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express <i>[gratitude]</i></li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (haitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guilt</li><li>accusation</li><li>reproach</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firinō</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfɛrinoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse, blame</li><li>to attack (used to translate a metaphorical use of <i>κατεσθίω</i> (katesthíō, “eat up, devour”))</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firinōną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌰</i> (fairina) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) abandonment</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;fairlētan, “to abandon”), from <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan); compare <i>𐌰𐍆𐌻𐌴𐍄</i> (aflēt), <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍄</i> (fralēt).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to abandon</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (ligan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍃</i> (fairns) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquity, oldness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (fairneis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌸𐌰</i> (-iþa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foregoing, previous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fernaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(reflexive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to depart, withdraw, move away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;firrijaną</i>. Compare Old English <i>feorran</i>, Old Norse <i>firra</i>, Old Saxon <i>firrian</i>, Old High German <i>firren</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍂𐌰</i> (fairra) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+ dative</i>) far away from</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferrai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-nóy</i> (locative), from <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to cross, pass”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from afar</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍂𐌰</i> (fairra) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html>:/fɛrˈrinnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, stretch</li><li>to achieve (with <i>𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍆𐍄𐍃</i> (þaurfts, “a purpose”))</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌸</i> (fairinōþ)</li></ol></html>:/fɛrˈwɔrkjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gain</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waurkjan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spectacle</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare, to gaze attentively</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌹𐍂-</i> (fair-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;weitjan).</p></html>:/ˈfɛr.ʍus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the world (the Universe) (almost always accompanied by the definite article)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferhuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falþaną</i> (“to fold”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to fold”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fraujan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of cloth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay, mire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fanją</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfɔ.hoː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuhǭ</i> (“vixen”). Cognates include Old High German <i>foha</i> and Old Norse <i>fóa</i> (“vixen”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before (<i>spatial and temporal</i>)</li><li>for, in favour of, on behalf of</li><li>near, next to, by</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furi</i>. Cognate to Old Norse <i>for-</i> and Old English <i>for</i>, <i>fore</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>spatial</i>) before, forth, forward; forth-</li><li>for- (privative)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furi-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before (<i>spatial and temporal</i>)</li><li>for (benefactive)</li><li>for (causal): because of</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in front, before</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furai</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>before, in front of, fore-, pre-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furai-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steward, manager</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (fauragaggan, “to precede, preside over, govern”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to precede</li><li>to govern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaiganganą</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stewardship, administration (of a household)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (fauragaggan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steward, manager</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (fauragaggan, “to precede, preside over, govern”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, occupational suffix).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stewardship, administration (of a household)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (fauragaggan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foretell, to announce beforehand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaigahaitaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (gahaitan).</p></html>:/fɔraɡaˈhuɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to purpose, determine, make up one's mind</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (hugjan)</p></html>:/fɔraɡaˈliːkaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to purpose</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (galeikān)</p></html>:/fɔraɡaˈmanwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare ahead of time, make up beforehand</li><li>to preordain</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamanwjan).</p></html>:/fɔraɡaˈmeːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write previously</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gamēljan).</p></html>:/fɔraɡaˈreːdan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predestine</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (garēdan), itself from <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) + <i>&#42;𐍂𐌴𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;rēdan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hreh₁dʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasandjan).</p></html>:/fɔraɡaˈsatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (gasatjan), itself from <i>𐌲𐌰-</i> (ga-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (satjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;satjaną</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foretell</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (gateihan, “to show”).</p></html>:/ˈfɔradɔri/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaidurją</i> (“place in front of a door”). By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (daur) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prophesy, to say beforehand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaikweþaną</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html>:/fɔraˈkʷiman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go before</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaikwemaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌹𐌸</i> (fauraqimiþ)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfɔrahaːh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain, veil</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) + the root of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (hahan, “to hang”); formally the exact equivalent of German <i>Vorhang</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leader, chief</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maþljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leadership, rulership</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maþljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leadership, rulership</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maþljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html>:/fɔraˈmanwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐌽𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (manwjan)</p></html>:/fɔraˈmeːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set forth, exhibit, display</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌴𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (mēljan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>προγράφω</i> (prográphō).</p></html>:/fɔraˈrahnjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outdo</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌰𐌷𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (rahnjan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>προηγέομαι</i> (proēgéomai).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sing before</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (siggwan, “recite, sing”). Compare the semantics of Latin <i>praecinō</i> (and the derived English <i>precentor</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand near or in front of something</li><li>to preside, supervise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furaistandaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (standan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miracle, wonder, marvel</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰</i> (faura) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌹</i> (&#42;tani). Second element is of unclear origin. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/fɔraˈweːnjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope in, trust in, first</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌴𐌽𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wēnjan), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>προελπίζω</i> (proelpízō)</p></html>:/fɔraˈwisan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present</li><li>to be superior</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan)</p></html>:/ˈfɔraˌfilli/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncircumcision</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰-</i> (faura-, “fore-”) + a descendant of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fellą</i> (“skin”) + the suffix <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). It would appear to be an abstract derivation from <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌰𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌻</i> (&#42;faurafill, “foreskin”); compare <i>𐌸𐍂𐌿𐍄𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌻</i> (þrutsfill, “leprosy”). However, since Ancient Greek <i>ἀκροβυστία</i> (akrobustía) meant both “foreskin” and “uncircumcision”, the same may be true of the Gothic word (and this is assumed in some dictionaries, e.g. Köbler’s). No verse in which the sense “foreskin” might surface has been handed down. Compare Dutch <i>voorvel</i> (“foreskin”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redemption through ransom payment</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;bauhts), from <i>𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bugjan, “to buy”); or directly from an unattested verb <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;faurbugjan, “to redeem, to ransom (?)”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/fɔrbiˈɡaŋɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go before</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forbid (to disallow)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furibeudaną</i>, from <i>&#42;furi-</i> + <i>&#42;beudaną</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>fyrirbjóða</i>, <i>forboða</i>, Old High German <i>farbiotan</i> (German <i>verbieten</i>), Old English <i>forbēodan</i> (English <i>forbid</i>).</p></html>:/fɔrbiˈsniwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go before</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌹-</i> (bi-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (sniwan).</p></html>:/fɔrˈɡaŋɡan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass by</li><li>to go before</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furiganganą</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obstruct, stop (as if by means of a dam)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌰𐌼𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;dammjan, from <i>&#42;dammijaną</i> (“to dam”)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prejudice</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;faurdōmjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins), from <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) + <i>𐌳𐍉𐌼𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dōmjan).</p></html>:/fɔrˈkʷiθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excuse</li><li>to make void, nullify</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan)</p></html>:/ˈfɔrhaːh/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain, veil</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) + the root of <i>𐌷𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (hahan, “to hang”); formally the exact equivalent of German <i>Vorhang</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fright, fear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtį̄</i> (compare English <i>fright</i>); equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (faurhts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear, be afraid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear, be afraid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearful, afraid, scared</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhtaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌸𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌹</i> (faurþizei)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfɔrθiziː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐌶𐌴𐌹</i> (þizei)</p></html>:/ˈfɔrθis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first</li><li>previously</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (faur) +&lrm; <i>𐌸𐌹𐍃</i> (þis)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a laying-before, a presentation</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (faurlagjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html>:/fɔrˈlaɡjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set before, put in front of</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lagjan); the same construction as German <i>vorlegen</i></p></html>:/fɔrˈmuːljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muzzle</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) + a denominal verb from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mūlō</i> (“muzzle”)</p></html>:/fɔrˈrinnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to precede, be a precursor to</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan)</p></html>:/fɔrˈsiɡljan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌲𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (sigljan).</p></html>:/fɔrˈsniwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come beforehand</li><li>to go ahead (<i>before someone else</i>)</li><li>to go before</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌽𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (sniwan)</p></html>:/ˈfɔrstassiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is over another, has authority over another; superior</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍃𐍄𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;faurstass) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis); the former is from <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>προϊστάμενος</i> (proïstámenos).</p></html>:/fɔrˈwɛːpjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muzzle</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;waipjan), the causative of <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (weipan).</p></html>:/fɔrˈwalwjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll (cause to revolve by turning over and over)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍂-</i> (faur-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌰𐌻𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;walwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>few, not many</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fawaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfa.rɛs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Perez, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Φαρές</i> (Pharés), <i>Φάρες</i> (Pháres), from Biblical Hebrew <i>פֶּרֶץ</i> (Pérets).</p></html>:/ˈfaran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to travel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faraną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharaoh</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharisee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Koine Greek <i>Φαρισαῖος</i> (Pharisaîos).</p></html>:/ˈfarjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail, row</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dative singular</i>) form</li></ol><p>From a form related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farwō</i> (“colour”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bandage, strip of linen, graveclothes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fascia</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɸastaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, to hold firm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fastāną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfastubni/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeping, observance (<i>of commandments etc.</i>)</li><li>fasting, an act of fasting</li><li>will-worship, self-imposed piety</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fastubniją</i> (“observance; fasting”), from <i>&#42;fastāną</i> (“to observe; to fast”). Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fastān) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fewer, less; comparative degree of <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (faus)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (faus) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌶𐌰</i> (-iza).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the body</i>) side, flank, part</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) region, part</li></ol><p>Related to Old High German <i>fera</i>, Old High German <i>fiara</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informer</li><li>spy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fērō</i> (“danger”) + <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “occupational suffix”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈfeːtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to adorn oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (fijands)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfiŋɡraˌɡulθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) finger ring (<i>literally, "finger-gold"</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fingragulþą</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌹𐌲𐌲𐍂𐍃</i> (figgrs, “finger”) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌿𐌻𐌸</i> (gulþ, “gold”).</p></html>:/ˈfiŋɡrs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) finger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fingraz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;penkʷrós</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfidur/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>four, quadri-</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedur-</i>, apparently a form of <i>&#42;feþur-</i> that arose in unstressed position by Verner's Law. Both ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwr̥-</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfi.durˌdoːɡs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) having lasted four days, four days old</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍂-</i> (fidur-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌳𐍉𐌲𐍃</i> (-dōgs, “days of age”). Close in formation to Old English <i>fiþerdōgor</i> (“lasting four days”).</p></html>:/ˈfi.durˌra.ɡin.ja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrarch or tetrarchate</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍂-</i> (fidur-) + the stem of <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽</i> (ragin) + a suffix, either <i>-𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (-eis) (compare <i>𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌽𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (ragineis)) or <i>-𐌹</i> (-i). Calque of Koine Greek <i>τετράρχης</i> (tetrárkhēs, “tetrarch”) or Koine Greek <i>τετραρχίᾱ</i> (tetrarkhíā, “tetrarchy”). The choice between these two suffixes, which would determine the lemma (nominative singular) form of the word, depend on whether one interprets this word as meaning "tetrach" (in which case <i>-eis</i> is likely the correct suffix) or "tetrachate" (in which case <i>-i</i> is the suffix). The word translates the participle <i>τετρααρχοῦντος</i> (tetraarkhoûntos, “being tetrarch”).</p></html>:/ˈfi.durˌfalθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fourfold (four times as great)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feþurfalþaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍂-</i> (fidur-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍆𐌰𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (-falþs). Close in formation to Koine Greek <i>τετραπλοῦς</i> (tetraploûs), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈɸid.woːr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedwōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>fēower</i> and Crimean Gothic <i>fyder</i>, and Koine Greek <i>τέσσαρες</i> (téssares), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈfid.woːr ˈθuː.sund.joːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>four thousand (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “four thousands”.</p></html>:/ˈfid.woːr ˈtiɡ.jus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forty (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedwōr tigiwiz</i> and cognate to Old Norse <i>fjórir tigir</i>. Contrast Crimean Gothic <i>furdeithien</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfid.woːrˌtɛ.hun/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourteen (cardinal number)</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌹𐌳𐍅𐍉𐍂</i> (fidwōr) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (taihun), a reforming of the elements of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fedurtehun</i> (whence Old English <i>fēowertīene</i> and Old Norse <i>fjórtán</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greatly</li><li>by far</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felauz</i>. Genitive form of <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu).</p></html>:/ˈfileːɡri/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌹𐌲𐍂𐌹</i> (filigri)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal, cover up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felhaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfiliɡri/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den (squalid or wretched place)</li></ol></html>:/ɸi.li.pːus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philip, the Biblical character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Φίλιππος</i> (Phílippos).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of leather or skin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fillīnaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfi.lu/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>much, a lot</li><li>(<i>+noun in genitive</i>) a lot of</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>much, a lot</li><li>very</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felu</i>. Originally the neuter singular form of older <i>&#42;feluz</i> (“many”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>feolo</i> (“much, many”), Latin <i>plūs</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πολύς</i> (polús).</p></html>:/ˈfiluɡalɔːfs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very costly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (galaufs); a calque of Ancient Greek <i>πολύτῑμος</i> (polútīmos).</p></html>:/ˈfiludiːsiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craftiness, wiliness</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌴𐌹</i> (&#42;deisei). The latter term is etymologically obscure. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude, amount</li><li>excess</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu) +&lrm; <i>-𐍃𐌽𐌰</i> (-sna).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wordiness</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;filuwaurds) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). Perhaps from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feluwurdį̄</i>, from <i>&#42;feluwurdijaz</i>. According to others (e.g. Köbler), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>πολυλογία</i> (polulogía).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use many words, be (overly) talkative</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feluwurdijaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌿</i> (filu, “much, many”) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (waurd, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan, “verbal suffix”). According to Köbler, a calque of Ancient Greek <i>βατταλογεῖν</i> (battalogeîn).</p></html>:/ˈfilufɛːhs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manifold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felufaihaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆</i> (fimf)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆 𐍄𐌹𐌲𐌾𐌿𐍃</i> (fimf tigjus)</li></ol></html>:/ˈɸimɸ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimf</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pénkʷe</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>fīf</i> and Crimean Gothic <i>fyuf</i>, and Koine Greek <i>πέντε</i> (pénte), which it translates.</p></html>:/ˈfimf ˈhun.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>five hundred (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “five hundreds”. Compare Old English <i>fīf hund</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfimf ˈθuː.sund.joːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>five thousand (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>Literally “five thousands”.</p></html>:/ˈfimf ˈtiɡ.jus/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fifty (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimf tigiwiz</i> and cognate to Old Norse <i>fimm tigir</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfimfˌtɛ.hun/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fifteen (cardinal number)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimftehun</i> and cognate to Old English <i>fīftīene</i> and Old Norse <i>fimtán</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆</i> (fimf) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (taihun).</p></html>:/ˈfimf.taˌtɛ.hun.da/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the fifteenth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fimftatehundô</i>; equivalent to <i>&#42;𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆𐍄𐌰</i> (&#42;fimfta) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌰</i> (taihunda).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌹𐌽</i> (fraujin)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, discover, learn of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (fraujins)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfi.jaθ.wa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hate, hatred, enmity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fijaþwō</i>, which is related to <i>&#42;fijāną</i>. Morphologically, it consists of <i>𐍆𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fijan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (-þwa).</p></html>:/ˈfi.jaːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fijāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, foe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fijandz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fijan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-ands).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol><p>Agent noun from <i>𐍆𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍉𐌽</i> (fiskōn, “to fish”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰</i> (-ja, “suffix forming agent nouns”).</p></html>:/ˈfisks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peysk-</i>. Cognate to Crimean Gothic <i>fisct</i>, Old English <i>fisċ</i> and Old Norse <i>fiskr</i>, and Latin <i>piscis</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fiskōną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in labor, be in the pain of childbirth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fetaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braid, plait</li></ol><p>Alternatively reconstructed as <i>&#42;𐍆𐌻𐌰𐌷𐍄𐍉</i> (&#42;flahtō); the only attestation (dative plural) could point to both lemma forms, but most dictionaries seem to consider <i>flahta</i> the likelier lemma. Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flehtaną</i>. Cognate with Dutch <i>vlecht</i>, German <i>Flechte</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈflɔːtjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vaunt oneself, to boast</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌻𐌰𐌿𐍄𐍃</i> (flauts) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan)</p></html>:/ˈflɔːts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) vainglorious</li></ol><p>Uncertain; perhaps from a Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flautaz</i>, but seemingly unrelated to (the root of) <i>&#42;flautijaną</i>. Old High German <i>flaozlihho</i> (“proudly”) and <i>flaozzan</i> (“to be proud”) are the only apparent cognates. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōduz</i>. Contains a Verner alternant of the suffix <i>-𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (-þus).</p></html>:/ˈɸloːkan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, mourn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōkaną</i> (“to beat, strike, lament”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fraujan)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfuɡls/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird (animal)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fuglaz</i>. Cognate with Old English <i>fugol</i>, Old High German <i>fogal</i>, and Old Norse <i>fogl</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfu.la/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colt (young male equid)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulô</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>fola</i> and Old High German <i>folo</i>, and related to Koine Greek <i>πῶλος</i> (pôlos), which it translates.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concealed, covered</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulginaz</i>. Compare Old Norse <i>folginn</i>, past participle of <i>fela</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secrecy, concealment</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌷𐌰𐌽</i> (filhan, “to bury, conceal”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈfullatoːjis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfect</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls, “full”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍄𐍉𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (-tōjis, “doing”).</p></html>:/ˈfullawiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mature, grown up, adult</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls, “full”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;weis, “wise”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persuade</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;weisjan). With the latter derived from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīsijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfullawits/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfect</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (witan).</p></html>:/fullaˈfahjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy, placate, content</li><li>to serve</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍆𐌰𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;fahjan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullaz</i> and <i>&#42;fagjaną</i>.</p></html>:/fullaˈfraθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be of sober mind, be sane</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls) +&lrm; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fraþjan)</p></html>:/ˈfulliθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fullness</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) new moon festival; Rosh Chodesh</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fulljan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/ˈɸullnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become full, to fill up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullnaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐍃</i> (fulls) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full (not empty)</li><li>(<i>strong declension only</i>) the whole, the entire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥h₁nós</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍆𐌿𐌻𐌻𐌽𐌰𐌹𐌸</i> (fullnaiþ)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul, putrid, fetid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fūlaz</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfu.nisks/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fiery (having been set on fire)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍉𐌽</i> (fōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix indicating separation, destruction, loss, or change&#59; in some cases marks perfectivity.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fra-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-</i>. Cognate with German <i>ver-</i> and English <i>for-</i>.</p></html>:/fraˈatjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bestow to feed, to distribute as food</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;atjaną</i>, causative of <i>&#42;etaną</i> (“to eat”).</p></html>:/ˌɸra.ˈbɛ.ran/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, endure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraberaną</i> (“to forbear”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) deed of sale</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;frabauhts, “sale”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐍉𐌺𐌰</i> (bōka, “deed, contract”). The first element is from <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (frabugjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌿𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bugjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forgive</li><li>to grant, give</li></ol><p>Diachronically, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fragebaną</i> (“to give”). By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (giban).</p></html>:/ˌɸra.ˈɣil.dan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay back, restore</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frageldaną</i> (“to pay back”). Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌲𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;gildan).</p></html>:/ˌɸra.ˈɣiɸts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift, grant</li><li>betrothal, engagement</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌹𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (fragiban) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distribute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fradailijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌳𐌰𐌹𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (dailjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare invalid, reject</li><li>to curse</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (qiþan).</p></html>:/fraˈkʷiman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, use up, consume</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frakwemaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (qiman).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perish, be destroyed</li></ol><p>From either <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (&#42;qists, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwistiz</i>) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan), or <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (qistjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy utterly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frakwistijaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌵𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (qistjan).</p></html>:/fraˈhinθan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take captive, bring into captivity, capture</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;hinþan), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinþaną</i> (“to reach for, catch”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reason, mind, intellect</li><li>understanding</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraþją</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fraþjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deception of the mind</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌹</i> (fraþi) +&lrm; <i>𐌼𐌰𐍂𐌶𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (marzeins).</p></html>:/ˈfraθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand, comprehend</li><li>to perceive, think</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraþjaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈɸrɛxnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask, to inquire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frehnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling or misspelling of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌹𐌳𐌰𐌿</i> (fraþjaidau)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, to attempt</li><li>to tempt, to test, to dare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraisaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temptation</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (fraisan, “to tempt”) with a suffix <i>-𐌿𐌱𐌽𐌹</i> (-ubni).</p></html>:/fraˈitan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraetaną</i>; cognate with German <i>fressen</i> and English <i>fret</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (itan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semen</li><li>seed</li><li>offspring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraiwą</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to spurn, reject, scorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frakunnaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌺𐌿𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (kunnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fralētan)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgiveness, remission</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fralētan).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, release, send away</li><li>to permit</li><li>to forgive, absolve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fralētaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, release, send away</li><li>to permit</li><li>to forgive, absolve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fralētaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (lētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberated or freed man</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌴𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fralētan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to betray</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌻𐌴𐍅𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (lēwjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose (something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraleusaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become lost, to perish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraluznaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (fraliusan, “to lose”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (-nan, fientive suffix).</p></html>:/fraˈlusts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, perdition</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fralustiz</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌻𐌹𐌿𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (fraliusan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs).</p></html>:/ɸram/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) from</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) by, due to</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forwards, forth</li><li>away, further</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fram</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strange, alien</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framaþiz</i>. An alternate possible lemma <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (framaþs) (i-stem) for the attested forms (as the nominative masculine singular is not attested) may instead be correct and is mentioned by some scholarly sources, but conventionally the lemma is listed in dictionaries as <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (framaþeis).</p></html>:/ˈframaθjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alienate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;framaþjaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (framaþeis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (-jan).</p></html>:/framˈaldrs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>advanced in years, of a great age</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼-</i> (fram-) +&lrm; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aldrą</i>.</p></html>:/framˈaldroːza/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>advanced in age</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼𐌰𐌻𐌳𐍂𐍃</i> (framaldrs, “advanced in age”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza, comparative suffix). In form a comparative, but used to translate Ancient Greek <i>προβεβηκυῖα</i> (probebēkuîa), which is not comparative in meaning.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progress, advancement</li></ol><p>A derivation from <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼-</i> (fram-) +&lrm; <i>𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (gaggan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs) following the pattern of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganhtiz</i>. Compare Swedish <i>framgång</i> (“progress”).</p></html>:/ˈframis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far</li><li>farther</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼</i> (fram) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is, comparative adverb suffix)</p></html>:/ˈframwɛrθis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>continuing into the future</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼</i> (fram) +&lrm; <i>-𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐍃</i> (-wairþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is).</p></html>:/ˈframwiɡis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forever, evermore</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼-</i> (fram-, “forward”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;wigan, “to move”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃</i> (-is); compare English <i>moving forward</i></p></html>:/fraˈniman/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;franemaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽</i> (niman).</p></html>:/ˈfrɔː.ja/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Lord; i.e. God</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawjô</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lordship, dominion</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰</i> (frauja, “lord”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐌽𐌰𐍃𐍃𐌿𐍃</i> (-inassus).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+dative</i>) to rule</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌰</i> (frauja, “lord”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler</li></ol><p>Substantivized from the above; <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌿𐌾𐌹𐌽𐍉𐌽</i> (fraujinōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-ōnds)</p></html>:/fraˈrinnan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run awry</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (rinnan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow up, devour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fraslindaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍃𐌻𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;slindan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoption</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (frasts) +&lrm; <i>𐍃𐌹𐌱𐌾𐌰</i> (sibja).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frastiz</i>, of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to trample</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fratrudaną</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍄𐍂𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (trudan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawerþaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, discard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawerpaną</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹𐍂𐍀𐌰𐌽</i> (wairpan). Cognates include Old English <i>forweorpan</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) sin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) sinful</li></ol><p>From the past participle of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (frawaurkjan, “to sin”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍃</i> (-þs). Compare Old English <i>forwyrht</i>, Old High German <i>firuuoraht</i>, Old Saxon <i>farwurht</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sin, transgress</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawurkijaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (waurkjan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a destruction, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌰𐍂𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (frawardjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins). Compare Old Frisian <i>werden</i>, <i>werdene</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wardīniz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt, disfigure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawardijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revenge, vengeance, retribution</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄</i> (&#42;weit, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wītą</i>).</p></html>

<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawītaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;weitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawītaną</i>. Equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (&#42;weitan).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avenger, revenger</li></ol><p>Substantivized form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (fraweitands), present participle of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐍅𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fraweitan).</p></html>:/fraˈwilwan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize</li><li>to take away</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐌻𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (wilwan).</p></html>:/fraˈwisan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;+dative) to squander through feasting</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌰𐌽</i> (wisan, “to feast, make merry”).</p></html>:/fraˈwrikan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to persecute</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (wrikan).</p></html>:/fraˈwroːhjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse</li></ol><p><i>𐍆𐍂𐌰-</i> (fra-) +&lrm; <i>𐍅𐍂𐍉𐌷𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (wrōhjan)</p></html>:/ˈfriːdjan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spare (preserve from danger or punishment)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with genitive object) to spare</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to forbear, spare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frīdijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom, liberty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijahalsaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (freis, “free”) +&lrm; <i>𐌷𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (hals, “neck”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌷𐌰𐌻𐍃</i> (freihals)</li></ol></html>:/friːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;priHós</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (frijaþwa)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfri.(j)aθ.waˌmil.diːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) feeling kind affection</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (frijaþwa) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌳𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (&#42;mildeis, “mild”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mildijaz</i>. Calque of Ancient Greek <i>φιλόστοργος</i> (philóstorgos). This lemma may also be given as <i>frijaþwamildeis</i> based on its derivation; intervocalic -j- is sometimes omitted in Gothic spelling, as in the single attestation of this term, but it is etymological and visible in most cases. Some sources reconstruct the second part of this lemma as <i>&#42;𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌳𐍃</i> (&#42;milds) (the attested form is ambiguous).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;genitive singular) a male given name</li></ol><p>This single attested form is very likely inherited from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Friþurīks</i> or is very closely related to that form. However, the expected Ulfilan Gothic reflex of this Proto-Germanic form would be *<i>friþureiks</i>, which is not attested: a compound of <i>&#42;𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌸𐌿𐍃</i> (&#42;friþus, “peace”) + <i>𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (reiks, “ruler, potentate, king”). The present form thus poses some problems which could potentially be explained by dialectal differences between the expected (fourth-century) Ulfilan Gothic and this actually attested (fifth or sixth-century) Ostrogothic form:</p></html>:/ˈfri.jaθ.wa/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, affection</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) charity, agape</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijaþwō</i>, a derivate of <i>&#42;frijaz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (freis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍅𐌰</i> (-þwa).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom, liberty</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌴𐌹𐍃</i> (freis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌸</i> (frijōþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌸</i> (frijōþ) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html>:/ˈfri.joːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to love</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female friend</li></ol><p>Feminine equivalent of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (frijōnds), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijōndz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frijōndz</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (frijōn) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐌳𐍃</i> (-ōnds).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiss</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌾𐍉𐌽</i> (frijōn, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌽𐍃</i> (-ōns).</p></html>:/ˈfriu̯s/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) cold (low temperature)</li></ol><p>Of unclear derivation. The formation is similar to Old Norse <i>frør</i> (“frost”), though this formation features the zero-grade of the root. Sturvevant proposed (according to Lehmann 1986) that it derives from an unattested adjective <i>𐍆𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃</i> (frius, “very cold”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prews-</i> (“to freeze”)</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image, representation, effigy</li><li>example</li></ol><p>Unknown; has no cognates in other Germanic languages.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frumą</i> (“beginning”).</p></html>:/ˈfru.ma/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the former, the first of two</li><li>former (previous)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning (initial portion of some extended thing)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frumô</i> and cognate to Old English <i>forma</i>. Contains the comparative suffix <i>-𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (-uma).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firstborn</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (fruma, “first”) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂</i> (baur, “one who is born”).</p></html>:/ˈfrumadiː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preeminence</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (fruma) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. The -d- represents a suffix here not accounted for</i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌴𐌹</i> (frumei)</li></ol></html>:/ˈfru.mi.sti/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning (that which begins or originates something)</li><li>a matter of utmost importance</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (frumists, “first”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹</i> (-i, “abstract noun”).</p></html>:/ˈfru.mists/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (fruma)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>first (preceding all others of a series or kind&#59; the ordinal of one&#59; earliest) (used of the first of more than two, or of an unspecified number)</li><li>first (most eminent or exalted&#59; most excellent&#59; chief&#59; highest)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frumistaz</i> and cognate to Old English <i>fyrmest</i>, whence English <i>foremost</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌰</i> (fruma, “former”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃</i> (-ists, “superlative suffix”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>intelligently</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (frōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (frōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei). Diachronically, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōdį̄</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wiser; comparative degree of <i>𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (frōþs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōdōzô</i>; equivalent to <i>𐍆𐍂𐍉𐌸𐍃</i> (frōþs) +&lrm; <i>-𐍉𐌶𐌰</i> (-ōza).</p></html>:/ɸroːθs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frōdaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Phoenician</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Φοινῑ́κη</i> (Phoinī́kē) +&lrm; <i>-𐌹𐍃𐌺𐍃</i> (-isks).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdīniz</i> (whence Middle High German <i>vüete</i>). By surface analysis, <i>𐍆𐍉𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fōdjan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (-eins).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nourish</li><li>to rear, nurture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdijaną</i>.</p></html>:/ˈfoːdr/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) scabbard</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdrą</i>&nbsp;n (“encasing”). Cognate with Old English <i>fōdor</i> (“scabbard”) and Old Norse <i>fóðr</i> (“lining, holster”).</p></html>:/ˈɸoːn/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire (chemical reaction)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōr</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂wr̥</i>. Cognate to Old English <i>fȳr</i> and Old Norse <i>funi</i>, and Koine Greek <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr), which it translates. This word was at one point an r-/n-stem (compare Hittite <i>𒉺𒄴𒄯</i> (paḫḫur)), with the -r supplanted by -n in the nominative and accusative by paradigmatic levelling.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot-shackle, leg-chain</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍉𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (fōtus) +&lrm; <i>𐌱𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌹</i> (bandi).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstool</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍆𐍉𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (fōtus, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌳</i> (&#42;baurd, “board, plank”, from <i>&#42;burdą</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pṓds</i>.</p><p>The transfer from consonant-stem inflection to u-stem inflection was spurred by the accusative, which had identical endings between the u-stems and consonant stems in Germanic.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿</i> (xristau)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌿𐍃</i> (xristaus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌿</i> (xristu)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (xristus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Christ</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>𐍇𐍂𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (xristus)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍈𐌰𐌸</i> (ƕaþ)</li></ol></html>:/ʍadreː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whither</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwadrê</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each, everyone, everything</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwazuh</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh). Cognate with Latin <i>quisque</i>.</p></html>:/ʍaθ/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whither</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas, “who, what”) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸</i> (-þ, location adverb suffix).</p></html>:/ˈʍaθar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>which, what (of two)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaþeraz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>each, every (of two)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaþerazuh</i>. By surface analysis, <i>𐍈𐌰𐌸𐌰𐍂</i> (ƕaþar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh). Compare Latin <i>uterque</i>, which may be inherited from the same source.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaþjaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kweth₂-</i> (“to bubble, foam”). Related to Old English <i>hwaþerian</i> (“to roar, foam, surge”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whence, from where, from what</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas) +&lrm; <i>-𐌸𐍂𐍉</i> (-þrō).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foam</li></ol><p>Related to <i>𐍈𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕaþjan, “to foam”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ˈʍɛrban/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to walk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwerbaną</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwirnį̄</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-</i>, <i>&#42;kʷern-</i> (“bowl, vessel”), whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hweraz</i> (“kettle”).</p></html>:/ˈʍɛː.tiːs/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of corn, probably wheat (cereal grain)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaitijaz</i> (“wheat”). Cognate to Old English <i>hwǣte</i> and Old High German <i>weizzi</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaz</i>. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html>:/ʍan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) when</li><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) whenever, if</li><li>(<i>before adjectives and adverbs</i>) to what degree, how much/many</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwan</i>. Cognate with English <i>when</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ever, at any time</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕan) +&lrm; <i>-𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (-hun).</p></html>:/ʍar/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwar</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, wander, roam</li><li>to go about, to pass by</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwarbōną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>each, every (of many)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwarjazuh</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍈𐌰𐍂𐌾𐌹𐍃</i> (ƕarjis) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html>:/ˈʍarjis/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>which, what (of many)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwarjaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anywhere, everywhere</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaruh</i>, from <i>𐍈𐌰𐍂</i> (ƕar) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html>:/ʍas/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>who, what</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anyone</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas) +&lrm; <i>-𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (-hun).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sharply</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;ƕass) +&lrm; <i>-𐌱𐌰</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>severity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwassį̄</i>, equivalent to <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐍃𐍃</i> (&#42;ƕass) +&lrm; <i>-𐌴𐌹</i> (-ei).</p></html>:/ʍeː/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with/to whom/what?</li><li>how</li><li>somehow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwē</i>, the instrumental neuter singular of <i>&#42;hwaz</i> (whence <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas)).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only, just</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌴</i> (ƕē) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an hour</li><li>a while, some time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwīlō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest, pause</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwīlāną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>temporary, transient</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (ƕeila) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐍈𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐍃</i> (&#42;ƕairbs), from the root of <i>𐍈𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽</i> (ƕairban).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for an hour or a short while, briefly</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌴𐌹𐌻𐌰</i> (ƕeila) +&lrm; <i>-𐌷𐌿𐌽</i> (-hun).</p></html>:/ʍiːts/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwītaz</i>. Cognates include English <i>white</i>, Dutch <i>wit</i>, German <i>weiß</i>, Old Norse <i>hvítr</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) how large, how extensive</li></ol><p>From <i>𐍈𐌴</i> (ƕē) +&lrm; <i>&#42;𐌻𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (&#42;lauþs, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laudaz</i>).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐍈𐌹𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐌰</i> (ƕileika)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>𐍈𐌹𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺</i> (ƕileik) +&lrm; <i>-𐌿𐌷</i> (-uh)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>what kind of, what sort of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwilīkaz</i>, equivalent to <i>𐍈𐌹-</i> (ƕi-) +&lrm; <i>-𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (-leiks), the former being the stem of <i>𐍈𐌰𐍃</i> (ƕas).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffin, bier (<i>dative</i>)</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwalbą</i> (“vault; arch”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boast</li><li>to rejoice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōpaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōtō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten, reprimand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōtijaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rejoicing, boasting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwōftuljō</i>.</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>O... ! (expresses a vocative)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ha! (expressing scorn)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has been linked to both Ancient Greek <i>Ο</i> (O, “omikron”) and <i>Ω</i> (Ō, “omega”), as well as Runic ᛟ (/⁠o/ or /ō⁠/). Snædal (2015; see references) favours the first derivation, interpreting it as being (like the letter <i>𐌿</i> (u, “urus”)) an adapted allograph of Greek <i>omikron</i>.</p><p>Compare Ancient Greek <i>ὦ</i> (ô) (which it is used to translate), Old English <i>ō</i>.</p><p>Onomatopoeic. Translates Ancient Greek <i>οὐὰ</i> (ouà).</p></html>:/ˈoːɣan/
<h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear, be afraid</li><li>to revere, to be awed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aganą</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eh₂ógʰe</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i> (“to fear, be distressed”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare, frighten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōgijaną</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i> (“to be afraid”). Essentially a causative of <i>𐍉𐌲𐌰𐌽</i> (ōgan, “to be afraid”).</p></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍉</i> (uhteigō)</li></ol></html><h3>Gothic</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hosanna</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ὡσαννά</i> (hōsanná), from Aramaic <i>אושענא</i> ('ōsha‘nā), from Biblical Hebrew <i>הוֹשַׁע נָא</i> (hōsha‘ nā, “please save”).</p></html>